{"_id": "JKdrYFx9Dc8Z6AuB", "name": "Introduction", "pages": [{"_id": "6Rmc1hh9WAQsVJxm", "name": "Overview", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h1>INTRODUCTION</h1>\n<p><em>You are not the chosen one. There is no chosen one. There is a river valley with too many hungry mouths, a wound in the world that never healed, and you \u2014 armed, skilled, mortal, and free to do something about any of it. Welcome to the Dying Age.</em></p>\n<p><strong>BLIGHTMARCH</strong> is a tabletop roleplaying game of grim sword &amp; sorcery, set in the long afterward of an age that died badly. It is a game about ordinary, capable, damaged people making consequential choices in a world that keeps its own books \u2014 where the harvest can fail, the wolves can vanish, the grain barons can notice both, and all of it happens whether or not the player characters are watching.</p>\n<p><strong>Contents:</strong> What This Game Is \u00b7 Who It&rsquo;s For \u00b7 Who It Isn&rsquo;t For \u00b7 The Campaigns It Runs \u00b7 What You Need \u00b7 How This Book Is Organized \u00b7 The Table Contract \u00b7 Inspirations and Design Debts</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 0, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "fPzzgWQMisRn9Btn", "name": "What This Game Is", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>What This Game Is</h2>\n<p><strong>A d100 game where low is good.</strong> Every uncertain, risky action is a Reckoning: roll d100, and if the die shows your Skill Rating or less, you succeed \u2014 with grades of triumph and cost on either side (Chapter 1). There are no classes and no levels. A character is the sum of what they&rsquo;ve survived, studied, and practiced, and they improve by <em>doing</em>: the swordsman by fighting, the cook by cooking, the liar by lying to someone who mattered.</p>\n<p><strong>A brutal game, honestly labeled.</strong> A common soldier can kill a veteran here. Wounds leave real marks and the worst of them cripple; armor works and wears you down; fear is a stat; what&rsquo;s lost generally stays lost, and the few ways around that cost more than they give (Chapters 3\u20134). Magic exists \u2014 rare, feared, priced in a corruption called Ash \u2014 and every tradition of it is a different way of going too far (Chapter 4).</p>\n<p><strong>A living world.</strong> This is the heart of the design. The setting runs on a <strong>Season Turn</strong>: weather rolls, harvests succeed or fail, factions act on their own goals, named NPCs advance their private agendas, animal populations shift, and consequences travel outward along written chains called <strong>Ripples</strong>. Kill the wolves and the deer eat the winter wheat. Kill the river wyrm and the Blight it was holding back starts walking. The players are never the reason the world moves \u2014 they are the most interesting thing moving through it.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>The world does not exist for the players. The players exist within the world.</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 100, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "2nK6MnL8WJS2W4bU", "name": "Who This Game Is For", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>Who This Game Is For</h2>\n<p>You will probably love Blightmarch if you want:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Consequences that outlive the session.</strong> Sandbox play where the map, the markets, and the politics genuinely respond \u2014 and where standing in front of an oncoming consequence is the game.</li>\n<li><strong>Characters defined by scars, not levels.</strong> Drives, Convictions, Traumas, Bonds, and Secrets are mechanics here, not flavor text \u2014 and the people you play are closer to Witchers and worn-down soldiers than to heroes of prophecy.</li>\n<li><strong>Craft as play.</strong> Smithing, cooking, alchemy, harvesting, and herbalism are full systems with teeth. The party&rsquo;s cook is a load-bearing member. A named sword accrues Deeds. Dinner has a stat block.</li>\n<li><strong>Politics as terrain.</strong> Factions with public faces and true purposes, cults with genuine anatomy, and a merchant guild that considers famine an investment opportunity \u2014 all of it playable, all of it interceptable.</li>\n<li><strong>Horror that tempts.</strong> Corruption here doesn&rsquo;t just disfigure \u2014 several of its Brands <em>help</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>GMing as world-operation.</strong> If you&rsquo;d rather run twenty minutes of honest procedure and discover the story with your players than steer them down one you wrote in advance, the Game Master chapter was built for you.</li>\n</ul>"}, "sort": 200, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "KXJ3CasgVt22OQn8", "name": "Who It Isn't For", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>Who It Isn&rsquo;t For</h2>\n<p>There&rsquo;s no wrong way to like games \u2014 but this one knows what it is, and it would rather tell you at the door. Look elsewhere if your table wants <strong>heroic power fantasy</strong> (competence here is hard-won and never safe), <strong>balanced encounters</strong> (the world is not calibrated to your party; the bestiary tells you what&rsquo;s true, not what&rsquo;s fair), <strong>guaranteed character survival</strong> (death is always on the table, telegraphed but real), <strong>high-magic problem-solving</strong> (casters are hunted, haunted, and billed), or <strong>a single authored plot</strong> (the GM operates a world; the story is what happens when you touch it).</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 300, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "U3giBFBE6gz12DpC", "name": "The Campaigns It Runs", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>The Campaigns It Runs</h2>\n<p>Blightmarch supports several campaign shapes out of the box \u2014 most real campaigns braid two or three of them:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The Frontier Sandbox.</strong> A region, a map, rumors, ruins, and roads \u2014 classic exploration play with survival pressure and a world that changes between visits. <em>(Chapters 3, 6, 8)</em></li>\n<li><strong>The Hunter&rsquo;s Economy.</strong> Track it, kill it, harvest it, craft it, cook it, sell it \u2014 a full monster-hunting loop where every creature is also an ecology, an ingredient list, and a political event. <em>(Chapters 5, 7)</em></li>\n<li><strong>The Long Game.</strong> Faction play: the party as agents, leverage, or kingmakers among noble houses, guilds, tribunals, and cults that act every season whether or not anyone is watching. <em>(Chapter 6)</em></li>\n<li><strong>Low-Magic Horror.</strong> Seam mysteries, Weighted texts, witch-finders, and congregations that can out-cast any lone sorcerer \u2014 investigation play where knowledge itself corrupts. <em>(Chapters 4, 6, 7)</em></li>\n<li><strong>Settlement and Domain.</strong> Claim a place, feed it, defend it, and watch the Provision number politics are made of \u2014 building play wired directly into the harvest, trade, and famine machinery. <em>(Chapters 5, 6, 8)</em></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Chapter 8 also ships three ready campaign frames \u2014 <strong>The Ford Reopened</strong>, <strong>The Saint&rsquo;s Vigil</strong>, and <strong>Wyrmfall</strong> \u2014 each starting the world mid-motion. The intended cadence: sessions in the field, a Season Turn between them, and rumors carrying the news of what moved.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 400, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "ZDq9BGB7lYCA8EjC", "name": "What You Need", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>What You Need</h2>\n<p>Two to five players and a Game Master (the system runs well as a duet). Percentile dice \u2014 two d10s read as d100 \u2014 plus a d4, d6, d8, and d10. This book, pencils, and paper \u2014 or the <strong>Character Ledger</strong>, the game&rsquo;s auto-calculating character sheet. One region to start: the <strong>Cindral Reach</strong> (Chapter 6) is provided, pre-loaded with seven powers and enough fault lines for years of play.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 500, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "nqAeMv8NKL0fvLEV", "name": "How This Book Is Organized", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>How This Book Is Organized</h2>\n<p>The book comes in two parts. <strong>Part One \u2014 the Player&rsquo;s Book (Chapters 1\u20135)</strong> is everything you need to make and play a character; it is safe and intended for everyone at the table. <strong>Part Two \u2014 the Game Master&rsquo;s Book (Chapters 6\u20138)</strong> is the machinery behind the curtain, written for the GM alone: the true purposes of factions, the secrets of the setting, what the monsters really are, and the engines that turn the world between sessions. Players get the most out of Blightmarch meeting Part Two from the inside, in play \u2014 so if you intend to play rather than run it, read to the end of Chapter 5 and stop.</p>\n<p><strong>Part One \u2014 The Player&rsquo;s Book.</strong> <em>Chapter 1 \u2014 Core Rules:</em> the Reckoning, Attributes, derived statistics, and the skill system. <em>Chapter 2 \u2014 Character Creation:</em> thirteen steps from concept to a person with a past. <em>Chapter 3 \u2014 Combat and Survival:</em> brutal and honest about weather, hunger, and fear. <em>Chapter 4 \u2014 Magic and Corruption:</em> the Seam, the six traditions, and the price of all of it. <em>Chapter 5 \u2014 Crafting, Cooking and Professions:</em> the maker&rsquo;s economy, from forge to stewpot.</p>\n<p><strong>The Player&rsquo;s Reference</strong> follows Part One, usable by everyone at the table: <em>Appendix A \u2014 The Armory</em> (some two hundred priced items, from arms to trade goods to cooking ingredients) and <em>Appendix B \u2014 The Field Guide</em> (over two hundred fauna, flora, and horrors, with what each yields when harvested).</p>\n<p><strong>Part Two \u2014 The Game Master&rsquo;s Book.</strong> <em>Chapter 6 \u2014 World Building and Factions:</em> the Cindral Reach, the faction engine, cults, the Rig, and the Ripple system. <em>Chapter 7 \u2014 Bestiary and Ecology:</em> creatures as ecosystems with stat blocks attached, and the Dread rule for apex threats. <em>Chapter 8 \u2014 Game Master Guide:</em> the Season Turn, the agrarian and congregation engines, ready-to-run enemy groups, one-minute NPCs, every generator, the plague list, and the one-page reference cards.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 600, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "V8y7r4YAX5IWpy0C", "name": "The Table Contract", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>The Table Contract</h2>\n<p>Read this with your group before the first session. Blightmarch is lethal, and it plays fair about it: danger is telegraphed the way the world telegraphs \u2014 hound-sign on the road, a falling Provision number, a moth-cloud on the horizon \u2014 and then the dice mean what they said. Character death is an ending, not a punishment; costs are the game, not obstacles to it. And because the game trades in dark material \u2014 trauma, plague, corruption, the Hollowed wearing familiar faces \u2014 calibrate its darkness together before play, and treat any player&rsquo;s stated line the way the system treats a natural 100: absolute, no negotiation past it. Chapter 8, Section 12 covers this in full.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 700, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "XjRWqqchZ5KwrASB", "name": "Inspirations and Design Debts", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>Inspirations and Design Debts</h2>\n<p>No game is built alone. Blightmarch stands on the following shoulders, and says so gladly:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Worlds Without Number and Cities Without Number</strong> <em>(Kevin Crawford)</em> \u2014 the sandbox scaffolding: faction turns, tag-driven generators, and the conviction that a well-built world runs itself.</li>\n<li><strong>Mythras and RuneQuest</strong> \u2014 the d100 roll-under engine and skill-by-use advancement: you become what you do.</li>\n<li><strong>Warhammer Fantasy Roleplay</strong> \u2014 professions as starting points rather than destinies, corruption with real teeth, and the grim confidence that consequences are content.</li>\n<li><strong>Forbidden Lands</strong> \u2014 survival as pressure: the Conditions system and the belief that the journey itself should cost something.</li>\n<li><strong>Shadow of the Demon Lord and Symbaroum</strong> \u2014 darkness that escalates, atmosphere as a mechanic, and above all corruption that <em>tempts</em> before it takes \u2014 several Marks of the Seam help you.</li>\n<li><strong>Monster Hunter</strong> and <strong>Heliana&rsquo;s Guide to Monster Hunting</strong> \u2014 creature ecology, the harvest window, and the loop of hunt \u2192 carve \u2192 craft that makes a bestiary an economy.</li>\n<li><strong>Delicious in Dungeon and the Atelier series</strong> \u2014 cooking as a survival engine and experimentation as discovery: the Nine Properties, the meal that matters, and the Wild Preparation you probably shouldn&rsquo;t drink.</li>\n<li><strong>The Witcher</strong> \u2014 monster-work as a trade with invoices, moral grey as the default weather, and politics that bites the people doing the work.</li>\n<li><strong>Dark Souls and Berserk</strong> \u2014 the tone of a wounded, beautiful, continuing world; the Hollowed; the conviction that horror lands hardest when it used to be someone.</li>\n<li><strong>Wuthering Waves</strong> \u2014 the Masks and Truths archetypes (Chapter 2) owe their existence to its layered character writing: the Velvet Mask, the Long Game, the Beautiful Ruin, and the Last Vow are studies of Phrolova, Changli, Camellya, and the Shorekeeper, respectively.</li>\n<li><strong>Remarkable Cults &amp; Their Followers</strong> <em>(JVC Parry, Jeff Lee, R P Davis)</em> \u2014 the anatomy lesson behind the Congregation Engine (Chapter 8): every cult rank as a two-column trade of dues paid and boons received, a face, a house, unique relics, and hooks that aim at the members you&rsquo;ll grow to like.</li>\n<li><strong>The Grainbound</strong> <em>(Tony&rsquo;s worldbuilding channel on YouTube)</em> \u2014 the agrarian and political spine of the GM chapter: the ox-paradox, the wood and salt takes, the parasite city, four-layered political characters, and the conviction that a properly rigged society is playable terrain, not background injustice.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>None of these works are required to play this one. All of them are recommended anyway.</p>"}, "sort": 800, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}], "folder": null, "sort": 0, "ownership": {"default": 0}, "flags": {}}
{"_id": "R78CsKEsocd2021g", "name": "Chapter 1 \u2014 Core Rules", "pages": [{"_id": "rar5kgUk7IWuvSoH", "name": "Overview", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h1>CHAPTER 1: CORE RULES</h1>\n<p>How uncertain actions are resolved, what a character is made of before any training, and how people grow more capable in a world that does not make growth easy.</p>\n<p><strong>Contents:</strong> 1. The Reckoning \u00b7 2. Attributes \u00b7 3. Derived Statistics \u00b7 4. Skills \u00b7 5. Worked Example</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 0, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "sUFPAldmQEUpCKHE", "name": "1. The Reckoning \u2014 Core Mechanic", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>1. The Reckoning \u2014 Core Mechanic</h2>\n<p>Whenever a character attempts something with a real chance of failure and a real cost to failing, they make a <strong>Reckoning</strong>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Roll d100 and read it against your Skill Rating (Section 4). If the die shows your Skill Rating or less, you succeed. Low is good</strong> \u2014 the further <em>under</em> your Rating you land, the better the result. Rolling <em>above</em> your Rating means the attempt fails, though a near miss (within 20) still buys the goal at a price.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Degrees of Success</h3>\n<p>The bands run in one unbroken ladder from 1 to 100 \u2014 best outcome at the bottom of the dice range, worst at the top:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The d100 shows\u2026</th>\n<th>Result</th>\n<th><em>Example: Skill Rating 46</em></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1 up to (Skill Rating \u00f7 5, round up)</td>\n<td><strong>Masterstroke</strong></td>\n<td>1\u201310</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>From there up to your Skill Rating</td>\n<td><strong>Success</strong></td>\n<td>11\u201346</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Above your Rating, up to Rating + 20 (never past 95)</td>\n<td><strong>Costly Success</strong></td>\n<td>47\u201366</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Above that, up to 95</td>\n<td><strong>Failure</strong></td>\n<td>67\u201395</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>96\u201399</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Failure \u2014 the World&rsquo;s Share: always, at any Rating</strong></td>\n<td>96\u201399</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A natural <strong>100</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Catastrophe</strong> \u2014 always, at any Rating</td>\n<td>100</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>Masterstroke</strong> succeeds beyond what was attempted \u2014 extra damage, unasked-for information, a task finished faster or better than planned. <em>(At Ratings 1\u20134 the band is simply a natural 1 \u2014 every attempt keeps one gleam of possibility.)</em></li>\n<li>A <strong>Success</strong> achieves exactly what was attempted, cleanly.</li>\n<li>A <strong>Costly Success</strong> achieves the goal but at a price introduced on the spot: the blade lodges in bone, the lock opens but the noise carries, the wound closes but leaves a scar that aches in the cold. Success is rarely clean.</li>\n<li>A <strong>Failure</strong> means the attempt simply doesn&rsquo;t come off \u2014 and whatever made the roll worth making now happens.</li>\n<li>A <strong>Catastrophe</strong> is more than failure. Something breaks, is lost, or turns against the character. Full Catastrophe tables by activity appear in Chapters 3\u20135.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Two things to hold onto at the table: <strong>you want to roll at or under your Rating</strong>, and within that, <strong>lower is better</strong> \u2014 with the single exception of the natural 100, which is always a Catastrophe no matter how skilled you are.</p>\n<p><strong>The World&rsquo;s Share.</strong> The top of the die \u2014 96 to 100 \u2014 belongs to the world. No Rating, modifier, or Technique buys it back: the greatest living swordsman still misses one swing in twenty, and one in a hundred goes catastrophically wrong. From Rating 76 upward the Costly Success band compresses against the 95 ceiling, so mastery trades messy wins for clean ones \u2014 but never for safe ones.</p>\n<h3>Opposed Reckonings</h3>\n<p>When two characters act directly against each other \u2014 a grapple, a bargaining table, a chase \u2014 both make a Reckoning. Compare degrees of success (Masterstroke &gt; Success &gt; Costly Success &gt; Failure &gt; Catastrophe); the better degree wins. <strong>On a tie of degree, the higher <em>successful</em> roll wins</strong> \u2014 both rolled under their Rating, and the one who rolled closer to their own ceiling pressed the advantage hardest. (If neither succeeded, the contest is a wash: nothing happens, and someone will have to try again.)</p>\n<p><strong>Degrees keep their teeth on both sides.</strong> The winner&rsquo;s degree decides the contested stake \u2014 but anyone who rolled a Costly Success still pays its cost, and anyone who rolled a Catastrophe still suffers it, win or lose. Winning ugly is still winning; losing expensively is both.</p>\n<h3>Difficulty Modifiers</h3>\n<p>Circumstances shift the Skill Rating used for a single roll \u2014 not the character&rsquo;s actual Rating going forward. In roll-under terms: a <strong>\u221220 means the number you must roll at or under is 20 lower</strong> for this attempt; a +20 means it&rsquo;s 20 higher.</p>\n<p><strong>The Clamps.</strong> No stack of modifiers can push the effective Rating <strong>above 95 or below 5</strong>. If circumstances would take it past 95, treat it as 95 \u2014 the World&rsquo;s Share stands no matter how easy the task. If they would drive it below 5, treat it as 5 \u2014 desperation always keeps a sliver \u2014 or, better, don&rsquo;t roll at all: past those bounds a task is either beneath the attempt or beyond it, and the GM should simply say which. The Masterstroke band is always computed from the clamped value.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Circumstance</th>\n<th>Modifier</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Trivial (real risk, but barely)</td>\n<td>+40</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Easy</td>\n<td>+20</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Standard</td>\n<td>+0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hard</td>\n<td>\u221220</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Very Hard</td>\n<td>\u221240</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nearly Impossible</td>\n<td>\u221260</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>If a task carries no real chance of failure or no real cost to failing, don&rsquo;t call for a Reckoning \u2014 just narrate the outcome.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 100, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "pVcSBtLIgZWIdw2H", "name": "2. Attributes", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>2. Attributes</h2>\n<p>Six Attributes describe what a character&rsquo;s body and mind are capable of before training. They&rsquo;re rated 1\u201320. Most adults fall between 6 and 14; 15+ marks someone renowned for that trait, and 18+ borders on legend.</p>\n<p>Each Attribute has an <strong>Attribute Bonus</strong>, used throughout the Derived Statistics below:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Attribute Bonus = Attribute Score \u2212 10</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Attributes rarely change after character creation, and never through spending points \u2014 only through experience severe enough to actually reshape a person.</p>\n<h3>Might</h3>\n<p><em>Raw physical power \u2014 muscle, brute force, the ability to break, crush, carry, and kill with the body.</em>\n- <strong>Governs:</strong> Heavy Weapons, Grappling, feats of raw strength.\n- <strong>Feeds:</strong> Melee Damage Bonus, Encumbrance Threshold.\n- <strong>Advancement:</strong> Sustained brutal physical hardship, survived and overcome \u2014 a season hauling ore, a hard campaign on foot, months under a master-at-arms&rsquo;s drills.</p>\n<h3>Agility</h3>\n<p><em>Speed, balance, coordination \u2014 precision of the body in motion.</em>\n- <strong>Governs:</strong> Light Weapons, Archery, Stealth, Acrobatics.\n- <strong>Feeds:</strong> Initiative, Defense Rating.\n- <strong>Advancement:</strong> Repeated life-or-death reactions \u2014 surviving ambushes, sustained practice of a precision discipline under real pressure, not in a training yard.</p>\n<h3>Endurance</h3>\n<p><em>Stamina and toughness \u2014 the body&rsquo;s resistance to damage, disease, poison, exhaustion, and privation.</em>\n- <strong>Governs:</strong> Wilderness Survival, resisting fatigue, cold, poison, and disease.\n- <strong>Feeds:</strong> Health, Stamina.\n- <strong>Advancement:</strong> Prolonged suffering endured and survived \u2014 famine, plague, a winter crossing, torture.</p>\n<h3>Intellect</h3>\n<p><em>Reasoning, memory, and the capacity to learn, understand, and apply knowledge.</em>\n- <strong>Governs:</strong> Lore skills, Alchemy, Engineering, Medicine.\n- <strong>Feeds:</strong> starting Lore and Language skills, Ritual capacity (Chapter 4).\n- <strong>Advancement:</strong> Genuine study at the edge of one&rsquo;s understanding \u2014 mastering a forbidden text, solving what no one else could, years under a real scholar.</p>\n<h3>Willpower</h3>\n<p><em>Discipline and self-control \u2014 resistance to fear, corruption, domination, and the crushing weight of the world.</em>\n- <strong>Governs:</strong> resisting Fear and Corruption, ritual discipline.\n- <strong>Feeds:</strong> Resolve, Corruption Threshold.\n- <strong>Advancement:</strong> Horror faced and not broken by \u2014 witnessing true horror and staying functional, resisting corruption&rsquo;s pull without submitting to it.</p>\n<h3>Presence</h3>\n<p><em>Force of personality \u2014 the ability to be believed, feared, loved, or obeyed.</em>\n- <strong>Governs:</strong> Persuasion, Intimidation, Deception, Leadership.\n- <strong>Feeds: Standing = Presence.</strong> This is the cap on how many <strong>loyal Followers</strong> a character can hold \u2014 retainers, students, sworn hands who act on their word (Chapter 6&rsquo;s Regard and faction rules use it). It is also the number of <strong>close Bonds</strong> a character can maintain at full strength before the newest starts to fray. Once per session, when the character leads openly at real personal risk, they may lend their Presence Bonus as a bonus to one ally&rsquo;s Reckoning who can see or hear them.\n- <strong>Advancement:</strong> Consequential exercises of influence \u2014 swaying a crowd at real risk, commanding troops in a real battle, a public act that becomes known.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 200, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "2NcQRdtOC5fbRiqh", "name": "3. Derived Statistics", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>3. Derived Statistics</h2>\n<p>Full application of each appears in the chapter noted; they&rsquo;re defined here because everything downstream depends on Attributes.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Statistic</th>\n<th>Formula</th>\n<th>Fully Used In</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Health</td>\n<td>Endurance + 10</td>\n<td>Chapter 3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stamina</td>\n<td>Endurance + Willpower</td>\n<td>Chapter 3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Resolve</td>\n<td>Willpower \u00d7 2</td>\n<td>Chapter 4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Encumbrance Threshold</td>\n<td>Might \u00d7 3</td>\n<td>Chapter 5</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Melee Damage Bonus</td>\n<td>Might Bonus</td>\n<td>Chapter 3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Initiative</td>\n<td>Agility Bonus</td>\n<td>Chapter 3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Defense Rating</td>\n<td>10 + Agility Bonus</td>\n<td>Chapter 3</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 300, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "MWb18NIhavMpSZF6", "name": "4. Skills", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>4. Skills</h2>\n<p>There are no classes in Blightmarch. A character is the sum of what they&rsquo;ve survived, studied, and practiced.</p>\n<h3>Skill Rating</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Skill Rating = Base Chance + Ranks</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>Base Chance</strong> is raw, untrained aptitude \u2014 what an average adult could manage on instinct alone. It comes from the two Attributes that govern a skill:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Base Chance = Governing Attribute 1 + Governing Attribute 2</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It&rsquo;s rarely above 30 without training.</p>\n<p><em>A few skills name the same Attribute twice \u2014 Light Weapons (Agility + Agility), Unarmed &amp; Grappling (Might + Might), Lore: History (Intellect + Intellect). These disciplines live or die on a single quality: they reward specialists and punish the one-sided harder than a split skill would. A clumsy brawler is bad at grappling in a way a merely-clumsy archer isn&rsquo;t at archery \u2014 the fiction, and the math, agree. If your table dislikes the sharper swing, the only house rule needed is to read a doubled Attribute as &ldquo;Attribute + 10&rdquo; instead; nothing else in the system depends on it.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Ranks</strong> are earned through play (see Advancement, below) and represent real training and experience. A starting character typically has 20\u201340 Ranks in their trained skills and 0 in everything else.</p>\n<h3>Tiers of Mastery</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Tier</th>\n<th>Skill Rating</th>\n<th>What It Means</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Untrained</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>No training. Restricted skills (marked below) cannot be attempted without at least 1 Rank.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Novice</td>\n<td>1\u201325</td>\n<td>Knows the basics. Can attempt standard tasks.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Journeyman</td>\n<td>26\u201350</td>\n<td>Reliably competent. Recognized as capable within a community.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Adept</td>\n<td>51\u201375</td>\n<td>Real mastery. Unlocks the skill&rsquo;s first <strong>Technique</strong>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Master</td>\n<td>76\u201395</td>\n<td>Renowned. Unlocks a second <strong>Technique</strong>. NPCs may know the character by name.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Legendary</td>\n<td>96+</td>\n<td>The stuff of songs. Unlocks one <strong>Legendary Ability</strong> \u2014 bespoke, built with the GM around a specific defining deed, never chosen from a list.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><em>Techniques are special options available on a Masterstroke, or as a resource-spending action. Full lists by skill live with their domains: combat Techniques in Chapter 3, crafting in Chapter 5, and all the rest \u2014 physical, social, knowledge, wilderness \u2014 in Chapter 2 \u00a711. Legendary Abilities are unique per character; two Legendary swordsmen should never have the same one.</em></p>\n<h3>Skill List</h3>\n<p>Governing Attributes are listed for each skill. <strong>(R)</strong> marks a Restricted skill \u2014 dangerous or forbidden knowledge nobody acquires by accident. A character cannot begin play with Ranks in one without an explicit story reason agreed with the GM.</p>\n<p><strong>Combat</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Skill</th>\n<th>Governing Attributes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Heavy Weapons</td>\n<td>Might + Agility</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Light Weapons</td>\n<td>Agility + Agility</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Polearms</td>\n<td>Might + Agility</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Unarmed &amp; Grappling</td>\n<td>Might + Might</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Archery</td>\n<td>Agility + Intellect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Thrown Weapons</td>\n<td>Agility + Might</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shield Craft</td>\n<td>Might + Endurance</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Physical</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Skill</th>\n<th>Governing Attributes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Athletics</td>\n<td>Might + Endurance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Acrobatics</td>\n<td>Agility + Agility</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stealth</td>\n<td>Agility + Intellect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wilderness Survival</td>\n<td>Endurance + Intellect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Riding</td>\n<td>Agility + Endurance</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Mental &amp; Knowledge</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Skill</th>\n<th>Governing Attributes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Lore: History</td>\n<td>Intellect + Intellect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lore: Nature</td>\n<td>Intellect + Endurance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lore: Occult (R)</td>\n<td>Intellect + Willpower</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alchemy</td>\n<td>Intellect + Endurance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Medicine</td>\n<td>Intellect + Agility</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Engineering</td>\n<td>Intellect + Might</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Navigation</td>\n<td>Intellect + Agility</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Social</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Skill</th>\n<th>Governing Attributes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Persuasion</td>\n<td>Presence + Intellect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Intimidation</td>\n<td>Presence + Might</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Deception</td>\n<td>Presence + Intellect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Insight</td>\n<td>Intellect + Willpower</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Leadership</td>\n<td>Presence + Willpower</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Streetwise</td>\n<td>Presence + Intellect</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Craft &amp; Trade</strong> <em>(expanded fully in Chapter 5)</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Skill</th>\n<th>Governing Attributes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Blacksmithing</td>\n<td>Might + Intellect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Carpentry</td>\n<td>Agility + Intellect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Leatherworking</td>\n<td>Agility + Intellect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cooking</td>\n<td>Intellect + Endurance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Brewing &amp; Distilling</td>\n<td>Intellect + Endurance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Herbalism</td>\n<td>Intellect + Endurance</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Wilderness &amp; Underworld</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Skill</th>\n<th>Governing Attributes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Tracking</td>\n<td>Intellect + Endurance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hunting</td>\n<td>Agility + Intellect</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Larceny</td>\n<td>Agility + Agility</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sailing</td>\n<td>Agility + Intellect</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Esoteric</strong> <em>(expanded fully in Chapter 4)</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Skill</th>\n<th>Governing Attributes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Ritual Casting (R)</td>\n<td>Willpower + Intellect</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Which Skill? \u2014 Boundaries</h3>\n<p>Several skills share a patch of ground. The rule is simple: <strong>each skill answers one question.</strong> Find the question being asked at the table, and you have the skill. If two could apply, the player picks and says which question they&rsquo;re answering \u2014 but a skill answers <em>its</em> question only.</p>\n<p><strong>In the wild.</strong> This is the densest cluster, so it gets the sharpest lines:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The question at the table</th>\n<th>Skill</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Which way did it go \u2014 how many, how heavy, how long ago?</em></td>\n<td><strong>Tracking</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Can I get close enough, kill it cleanly, and take what I need?</em></td>\n<td><strong>Hunting</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Do we live through this night, this weather, this crossing?</em></td>\n<td><strong>Wilderness Survival</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>What is that, and why is it here?</em> (no need to be present)</td>\n<td><strong>Lore: Nature</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Can I find this plant and make it do something?</em></td>\n<td><strong>Herbalism</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Where are we, and how do we get there?</em></td>\n<td><strong>Navigation</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>So: you <strong>Track</strong> a stag to the clearing, <strong>Hunt</strong> to put it down and butcher it, use <strong>Wilderness Survival</strong> to keep the meat and yourself through the night, ask <strong>Lore: Nature</strong> why stags are down at the field margins this early, <strong>Herbalism</strong> to find something to pack the wound you took, and <strong>Navigation</strong> to get home. One trip, six questions, no argument.</p>\n<p><strong>Talking.</strong> All four move a person; the difference is <em>why they move</em>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The question at the table</th>\n<th>Skill</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>They act because I&rsquo;ve convinced them it&rsquo;s right or in their interest.</em></td>\n<td><strong>Persuasion</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>They act because they believe something untrue.</em></td>\n<td><strong>Deception</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>They act because they&rsquo;re afraid of me.</em></td>\n<td><strong>Intimidation</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>They act because they already accept my authority.</em></td>\n<td><strong>Leadership</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>What do they actually want, and are they lying?</em> (reading, not moving)</td>\n<td><strong>Insight</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Who do I need, and where do I find them?</em> (finding, not moving)</td>\n<td><strong>Streetwise</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Making.</strong> Craft skills are told apart by <em>material</em>, not by task: <strong>Blacksmithing</strong> (metal), <strong>Carpentry</strong> (wood), <strong>Leatherworking</strong> (hide), <strong>Cooking</strong> (food for the table), <strong>Brewing &amp; Distilling</strong> (food that keeps or intoxicates), <strong>Alchemy</strong> (extracts and reagents), <strong>Herbalism</strong> (the plants those last two start from). If a project spans two \u2014 a hafted axe, a poisoned blade \u2014 roll the harder one and let the other grant +10.</p>\n<p><strong>A GM&rsquo;s shortcut.</strong> When it&rsquo;s genuinely ambiguous, ask: <em>what is the character&rsquo;s expertise doing here?</em> A hunter reads a trail as a hunter. A scholar reads it as Lore. Both can succeed; they&rsquo;ll notice different things, and that&rsquo;s a feature. Never make a player roll twice for one question.</p>\n<h3>Advancement</h3>\n<p>Characters grow through four methods, matching how the world actually teaches people:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Marking a Skill.</strong> Whenever a skill is used in a Reckoning where real failure was possible and something meaningful was at stake \u2014 or used in a way the character has never attempted before \u2014 mark it. A skill can only be marked once per session.</li>\n<li><strong>The Growth Check.</strong> In downtime between chapters, roll d100 for each marked skill. If the result is <em>higher</em> than the skill&rsquo;s current Rating, it improves by 1d6. Low skills grow fast, high skills grow slowly: a Rating of 20 improves on any roll of 21+ (80% chance); a Rating of 80 only improves on a roll of 81+ (20% chance). Mastery is meant to be hard-won.</li>\n<li><strong>Teachers.</strong> A qualified teacher \u2014 someone with a higher Rating, willing and able to spend real time on it \u2014 grants an automatic Growth Check without needing to Mark the skill first. This costs weeks or months of story time, and often coin, favor, or service.</li>\n<li><strong>Experimentation.</strong> Attempting a skill in a genuinely untested, risky, or innovative way (an unproven alchemical mixture, an unfamiliar stance against a strange foe) marks the skill twice instead of once \u2014 but doubles the Catastrophe range (both 99 and 100 become Catastrophes) for that attempt. Greater risk, greater reward.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 400, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "2ZPcZopNhezTLh2z", "name": "5. Worked Example", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>5. Worked Example</h2>\n<p><em>Dren, a hired sword with Light Weapons Rating 35, is cornered on a bridge at night. The GM calls for a Reckoning.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Attack roll:</strong> Dren&rsquo;s player rolls a 41. Skill Rating is 35, so Masterstroke is 1\u20137, Success is 8\u201335, and Costly Success runs 36\u201355 (35+20). A 41 falls in that band. <strong>Result: Costly Success.</strong> Dren&rsquo;s blade takes the bandit down \u2014 but the GM rules it skids off a rib and lodges in the bridge railing. Freeing it will cost Dren an action.</p>\n<p>Because this was a real, dangerous Reckoning, the player marks Light Weapons.</p>\n<p><em>That night, at camp, the party rests and the GM calls for Growth Checks.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Growth Check:</strong> Dren&rsquo;s player rolls d100 for Light Weapons and gets 58 \u2014 higher than the current Rating of 35, so it improves. A 1d6 roll comes up 4. <strong>Light Weapons rises from 35 to 39.</strong></p>"}, "sort": 500, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}], "folder": null, "sort": 100, "ownership": {"default": 0}, "flags": {}}
{"_id": "cCYbPYbX5ANugeKa", "name": "Chapter 2 \u2014 Character Creation", "pages": [{"_id": "wsFoEDulalrHEmOK", "name": "Overview", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h1>CHAPTER 2: CHARACTER CREATION</h1>\n<p><em>No one arrives at the sword-point of their life as who they were at the start of it. Ask any survivor what they lost becoming what they are, and watch how long it takes them to answer.</em></p>\n<p>Nobody in Blightmarch is a blank-slate hero waiting for a destiny. Every character arrives at the table already shaped \u2014 by where they were born, what they were trained to do, what they&rsquo;ve survived, and what they&rsquo;re still not telling anyone. This chapter builds characters the way the world actually makes people: unevenly, at a cost, and never quite finished. It assumes Chapter 1 (Attributes, Skills, the Reckoning) is on hand.</p>\n<p><strong>Contents:</strong> 1. The Creation Sequence \u00b7 2. Attributes \u00b7 3. Background \u00b7 4. Culture \u00b7 5. Profession \u00b7 6. Free Ranks and Derived Statistics \u00b7 7. Drive, Conviction, and Trauma \u00b7 8. Bonds and Secrets \u00b7 9. Masks and Truths \u00b7 10. Worked Example \u00b7 11. Techniques for the Other Skills</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 0, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "Uua0prmSTNkmF9PE", "name": "1. The Creation Sequence", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>1. The Creation Sequence</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Concept</strong> \u2014 one sentence. Who are they, and what&rsquo;s wrong?</li>\n<li><strong>Attributes</strong> \u2014 roll or assign an array (Section 2).</li>\n<li><strong>Background</strong> \u2014 where they came from (Section 3).</li>\n<li><strong>Culture</strong> \u2014 who raised them (Section 4).</li>\n<li><strong>Profession</strong> \u2014 what they do to survive (Section 5).</li>\n<li><strong>Free Ranks</strong> \u2014 round the character out (Section 6).</li>\n<li><strong>Derived Statistics</strong> \u2014 Health, Stamina, Resolve, and the rest (Section 6).</li>\n<li><strong>Drive</strong> \u2014 why they&rsquo;re doing any of this (Section 7).</li>\n<li><strong>Conviction</strong> \u2014 one thing they believe enough to act on (Section 7).</li>\n<li><strong>Trauma</strong> \u2014 one thing that still has teeth (Section 7).</li>\n<li><strong>Bonds</strong> \u2014 one Ally, one Debt or Rival (Section 8).</li>\n<li><strong>Secret</strong> \u2014 one fact with real stakes if it gets out (Section 8).</li>\n<li><strong>Masks and Truths</strong> <em>(optional)</em> \u2014 a complexity archetype, for PCs and NPCs who deserve one (Section 9).</li>\n</ol>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 100, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "Xjd8eiUDnwHLZLTe", "name": "2. Attributes", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>2. Attributes</h2>\n<p>Choose one method:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Method A \u2014 The Roll of Fate.</strong> Roll 3d6 six times. Assign the results to Might, Agility, Endurance, Intellect, Willpower, and Presence however you like.</li>\n<li><strong>Method B \u2014 The Measured Path.</strong> Assign this array as you like: <strong>14, 12, 11, 10, 9, 8.</strong></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Background (Section 3) then adds +1 to two specific Attributes on top of this.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 200, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "L7sAtwGe0haaXeLW", "name": "3. Background", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>3. Background</h2>\n<p>Where a character comes from \u2014 the years before anyone handed them a weapon or a trade. Roll d10 or choose. Each Background grants +1 to two Attributes, two starting skills at listed Ranks, and a <strong>Scar</strong>: a seed for later Trauma or Secret sections, usable as written or reinterpreted freely.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>d10</th>\n<th>Background</th>\n<th>+1 Attributes</th>\n<th>Starting Skills</th>\n<th>Scar</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Ward-Orphan</td>\n<td>Agility, Endurance</td>\n<td>Larceny +15, Streetwise +10</td>\n<td>A face from the fire you can&rsquo;t forget.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Fallen House Scion</td>\n<td>Presence, Intellect</td>\n<td>Lore: History +15, Persuasion +10</td>\n<td>Your family&rsquo;s ruin was not an accident, and someone knows why.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Temple-Raised</td>\n<td>Willpower, Intellect</td>\n<td>Insight +15, Medicine +10</td>\n<td>You stopped believing the night you saw what the rites were actually for.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>War-Born</td>\n<td>Might, Endurance</td>\n<td>Heavy Weapons +10, Wilderness Survival +15</td>\n<td>You killed someone before you were old enough to understand what that meant.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Village-Bound</td>\n<td>Endurance, Intellect</td>\n<td>Herbalism +15, Lore: Nature +10</td>\n<td>The harvest failed once before, in a year everyone agreed never to speak of.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Road-Born</td>\n<td>Agility, Presence</td>\n<td>Riding +15, Navigation +10</td>\n<td>You still don&rsquo;t know why your caravan really changed its route that one winter.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Guild-Bound</td>\n<td>Intellect, Might</td>\n<td>Blacksmithing +15, Engineering +10</td>\n<td>The master who trained you vanished owing a debt you inherited without asking.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Condemned</td>\n<td>Willpower, Endurance</td>\n<td>Intimidation +15, Larceny +10</td>\n<td>There&rsquo;s a mark on you \u2014 brand, ledger, or memory \u2014 that says exactly what you did.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Sailor&rsquo;s Get</td>\n<td>Agility, Endurance</td>\n<td>Sailing +15, Athletics +10</td>\n<td>The sea took someone from you and gave nothing back.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Hunter&rsquo;s Kin</td>\n<td>Agility, Intellect</td>\n<td>Hunting +15, Tracking +10</td>\n<td>You know a place in the deep woods the animals refuse to enter, and you&rsquo;ve never told anyone why.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 300, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "eMwdwkBkHHRYP0ke", "name": "4. Culture", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>4. Culture</h2>\n<p>The people who raised a character, and what they were taught to believe about the world. Named kingdoms and cities arrive with world generation (Chapter 6) \u2014 until then, these six Culture Archetypes cover the ground. Roll d6 or choose. Each grants a Language, one skill at +10, and a <strong>Tenet</strong>: the culture&rsquo;s default belief, which a character can keep or later override with a personal Conviction (Section 7).</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>d6</th>\n<th>Culture</th>\n<th>Language</th>\n<th>Skill</th>\n<th>Tenet</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Highland Clans</td>\n<td>Old Clan-Tongue</td>\n<td>Athletics +10</td>\n<td>A debt unpaid is a wound unhealed.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>River-Cities</td>\n<td>Trade-Cant</td>\n<td>Persuasion +10</td>\n<td>Everything has a price; the fool is the one who doesn&rsquo;t know his.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Steppe-Riders</td>\n<td>Steppe-Tongue</td>\n<td>Riding +10</td>\n<td>The land doesn&rsquo;t belong to anyone who stops moving.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Coastal Free Towns</td>\n<td>Coastal Pidgin</td>\n<td>Sailing +10</td>\n<td>Loyalty is bought fresh every season; anyone who says otherwise is selling something.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Feudal Heartlands</td>\n<td>High Courtly</td>\n<td>Lore: History +10</td>\n<td>Your place was decided before you were born; what you do with it is the only choice you get.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Deep-Forest Folk</td>\n<td>Wood-Speech</td>\n<td>Lore: Nature +10</td>\n<td>The forest remembers everyone who breaks a promise in it.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 400, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "0m4Qq3eKDGvupDXW", "name": "5. Profession", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>5. Profession</h2>\n<p>What a character actually does to eat. Not a class \u2014 it sets a starting point, nothing more; any character can train into any skill later through Chapter 1&rsquo;s advancement rules. Roll d10 or choose. Each Profession grants three or four skills at listed Ranks, a small equipment package, and a <strong>Trade Boon</strong>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>d10</th>\n<th>Profession</th>\n<th>Starting Skills</th>\n<th>Equipment</th>\n<th>Trade Boon</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Sellsword</td>\n<td>Heavy Weapons +20, Shield Craft +15, Intimidation +10</td>\n<td>Worn arming sword, dented shield, patched mail</td>\n<td>Once per session, treat a Costly Success on a combat skill as a plain Success.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Hedge-Alchemist</td>\n<td>Alchemy +25, Herbalism +15, Medicine +10</td>\n<td>Traveling still, locked case of components, battered formulary</td>\n<td>Once per day, attempt an Alchemy Reckoning without a material component (Chapter 5).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Former Soldier</td>\n<td>Polearms +20, Athletics +15, Leadership +10</td>\n<td>Spear, worn gambeson, a unit token from a war no one discusses</td>\n<td>+10 to Reckonings made to steady a routed ally (Chapter 3).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Poacher</td>\n<td>Archery +20, Stealth +15, Hunting +15</td>\n<td>Hunting bow, skinning kit, a mental map of every warden&rsquo;s blind spot</td>\n<td>Always knows whether a wild area is actively patrolled.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Con Artist</td>\n<td>Deception +20, Insight +15, Streetwise +15</td>\n<td>Forged papers (at least one still good), fine clothes worn thin at the cuffs</td>\n<td>Once per session, retroactively declare a piece of &ldquo;established&rdquo; backstory true.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Lay Priest (Excommunicate)</td>\n<td>Insight +20, Lore: History +15, Medicine +10</td>\n<td>Symbol of a faith that no longer claims them, a much-read prayer book</td>\n<td>Resolve recovers fully on a full night&rsquo;s rest instead of half (Chapter 4).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Grave-Robber</td>\n<td>Lore: History +15, Larceny +15, Athletics +10</td>\n<td>Collapsible spade, grave-wax candle, unsold &ldquo;finds&rdquo;</td>\n<td>+20 to Reckonings to appraise a found object&rsquo;s age and value.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Camp Cook</td>\n<td>Cooking +25, Herbalism +15, Brewing &amp; Distilling +10</td>\n<td>Well-kept knife roll, portable cookpot, a spice satchel worth more than it looks</td>\n<td>A prepared meal&rsquo;s bonus applies to the whole party, not just the cook (Chapter 5).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Wandering Medic</td>\n<td>Medicine +25, Herbalism +15, Insight +10</td>\n<td>Surgeon&rsquo;s roll, clean bandages, a ledger of favors owed</td>\n<td>A Masterstroke on Medicine heals an additional Wound level (Chapter 3).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Smuggler</td>\n<td>Sailing +15, Streetwise +15, Deception +15</td>\n<td>False-bottomed chest, a share in a small unmarked boat, three names that owe a favor</td>\n<td>+20 to Reckonings to conceal goods from a search.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 500, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "prFzZ3MUT1KXzntZ", "name": "6. Free Ranks and Derived Statistics", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>6. Free Ranks and Derived Statistics</h2>\n<p><strong>Free Ranks.</strong> Distribute 20 additional Ranks across any skills already touched by Background, Culture, or Profession, or into new ones \u2014 maximum +10 to any single skill from this pool. Restricted skills still require the GM&rsquo;s sign-off (Chapter 1, Section 4).</p>\n<p><strong>Derived Statistics.</strong> Calculate Health, Stamina, Resolve, Encumbrance Threshold, Melee Damage Bonus, Initiative, and Defense Rating using the formulas in Chapter 1, Section 3, now that Attributes are final.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 600, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "FDnknAeidtqnOeXh", "name": "7. Drive, Conviction, and Trauma", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>7. Drive, Conviction, and Trauma</h2>\n<h3>Drive</h3>\n<p>Why the character is doing any of this. Roll d8 or choose. When a character acts directly on their Drive in a scene with real risk, the GM may grant +10 to the relevant Reckoning \u2014 once per session.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>d8</th>\n<th>Drive</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td><strong>Debt</strong> \u2014 you owe something to someone, or something, that can&rsquo;t be repaid in coin.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td><strong>Vengeance</strong> \u2014 someone specific has to answer for something specific.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td><strong>Atonement</strong> \u2014 you did a thing you cannot undo, and you&rsquo;re trying to be someone else now.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td><strong>Legacy</strong> \u2014 you&rsquo;re building or protecting something meant to outlast you.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td><strong>Hunger</strong> \u2014 you want more than you have, and you&rsquo;ve stopped pretending otherwise.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td><strong>Belonging</strong> \u2014 you&rsquo;re looking for a place, or people, that will actually keep you.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td><strong>Curiosity</strong> \u2014 some question matters more to you than your own safety.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td><strong>Freedom</strong> \u2014 someone or something owns a piece of you, and you intend to take it back.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Conviction</h3>\n<p>One sentence, written by the player: something the character believes strongly enough to act on at real cost. <em>(Resolve is defined in Chapter 1; full spending and corruption rules arrive in Chapter 4 \u2014 for now, treat it as a pool that rises and falls by small amounts.)</em></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Act on your Conviction at real cost to yourself: recover 1 Resolve at the end of the scene.</li>\n<li>Abandon it for convenience: the GM may mark it <strong>Broken</strong>. A Broken Conviction can be rewritten between sessions, but the character takes 1 Trauma first.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Trauma</h3>\n<p>Every character starts with one Trauma \u2014 from their Background&rsquo;s Scar, or freshly chosen below. A Trauma has a <strong>Trigger</strong> (what sets it off) and a <strong>Flinch</strong> (the mechanical effect). When the Trigger occurs in play, the GM calls for the Flinch \u2014 it isn&rsquo;t optional, though how the character plays it is always the player&rsquo;s choice. Characters can gain further Trauma through play; see Chapter 3 (violence) and Chapter 4 (corruption).</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>d8</th>\n<th>Trauma</th>\n<th>Trigger</th>\n<th>Flinch</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>The Cold Room</td>\n<td>Confinement, being trapped</td>\n<td>\u221220 to all Reckonings until you act to escape or fight free.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>The Silence After</td>\n<td>Sudden violence against someone helpless</td>\n<td>Willpower Reckoning or freeze for one exchange.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>The Debt of Breath</td>\n<td>Someone dies because of a choice you made</td>\n<td>\u221220 to Reckonings touching that person&rsquo;s name, image, or memory, for the rest of the scene.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>The Watching Dark</td>\n<td>Magic used nearby, or its aftermath</td>\n<td>\u221210 to all Reckonings until real distance is put between you and the working.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Never Enough</td>\n<td>Offered charity or unearned help</td>\n<td>Willpower Reckoning to accept it gracefully.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>The Last Order</td>\n<td>Given a direct command by an authority figure</td>\n<td>Willpower Reckoning to refuse it, even when refusal is clearly right.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Salt and Ash</td>\n<td>Fire, or the smell of a structure burning</td>\n<td>\u221220 to all Reckonings for the rest of the scene.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>The Unanswered Question</td>\n<td>A direct lie, told to your face, by someone you trusted</td>\n<td>You believe it once, automatically \u2014 no Reckoning permitted.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 700, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "h9yajPdBZ2C91RAg", "name": "8. Bonds and Secrets", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>8. Bonds and Secrets</h2>\n<h3>Bonds</h3>\n<p>Two relationships, written now so the GM has hooks from session one.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ally.</strong> Name them. What would they do for you that they wouldn&rsquo;t do for anyone else? What have you never told them?</li>\n<li><strong>Debt or Rival.</strong> Name them. What&rsquo;s owed, and in which direction? What happens if it&rsquo;s never settled?</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Secret</h3>\n<p>One fact about the character that isn&rsquo;t public. Write the fact, then the <strong>Stakes</strong> \u2014 what actually happens if it comes out \u2014 and optionally <strong>Who Knows</strong> besides the character. Full Complication rules arrive in Chapter 8; until then, treat a Secret as a loaded weapon left on the table. The GM should always know when it might go off.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 800, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "KfuZ8nxXtpTehelu", "name": "9. Masks and Truths", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>9. Masks and Truths</h2>\n<p>Not every character needs this layer \u2014 save it for PCs and the NPCs whose complexity should be felt at the table, not just described. Each archetype has a <strong>Mask</strong> (what the world sees), a <strong>Truth</strong> (what&rsquo;s actually there), and a mechanical <strong>Hook</strong>. Choose one, or none.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Archetype</th>\n<th>Mask</th>\n<th>Truth</th>\n<th>Hook</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>The Velvet Mask</strong></td>\n<td>Warm, useful, easy to trust.</td>\n<td>Every kindness serves a goal they&rsquo;d never say aloud.</td>\n<td>Once per session, treat any Social skill as Deception for one Reckoning.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>The Long Game</strong></td>\n<td>Patient, reasonable, never in a hurry.</td>\n<td>Has been positioning for this exact moment longer than anyone realizes.</td>\n<td>Once per session, bank an unused Reckoning as Preparation; spend it later for an automatic Success on a task you could plausibly have set up in advance.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>The Beautiful Ruin</strong></td>\n<td>Magnetic, memorable, impossible to look away from.</td>\n<td>Pursuing something with an intensity that frightens the people closest to them.</td>\n<td>+20 to Reckonings made in service of the obsession \u2014 but the GM may add a complication even to a plain Success when it&rsquo;s involved.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>The Last Vow</strong></td>\n<td>Dutiful, composed, dependable.</td>\n<td>Privately unsure the vow was ever worth what it&rsquo;s cost.</td>\n<td>May downgrade one of their own Reckoning results by one degree (Success \u2192 Costly Success, Costly Success \u2192 Failure) to serve the Vow, recovering 2 Resolve when they do.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 900, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "ql8Rq78E4lhyJHWI", "name": "10. Worked Example: Building a Character", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>10. Worked Example: Building a Character</h2>\n<p><strong>Concept:</strong> Yorna Vask \u2014 a former soldier turned sellsword, running from a war she can&rsquo;t stop thinking about.</p>\n<p><strong>Attributes</strong> (Method B: 14, 12, 11, 10, 9, 8): Might 14, Endurance 12, Agility 11, Willpower 10, Presence 9, Intellect 8.</p>\n<p><strong>Background \u2014 War-Born:</strong> +1 Might, +1 Endurance \u2192 Might 15, Endurance 13. Heavy Weapons +10, Wilderness Survival +15. Scar: <em>&ldquo;You killed someone before you were old enough to understand what that meant.&rdquo;</em></p>\n<p><strong>Culture \u2014 Feudal Heartlands:</strong> High Courtly. Lore: History +10. Tenet: <em>&ldquo;Your place was decided before you were born.&rdquo;</em></p>\n<p><strong>Profession \u2014 Former Soldier:</strong> Polearms +20, Athletics +15, Leadership +10. Spear, gambeson, unit token. Trade Boon: +10 to steady a routed ally.</p>\n<p><strong>Free Ranks (20, max +10/skill):</strong> Heavy Weapons +10, Intimidation +10.</p>\n<p><strong>Skill Ratings (Base Chance + Ranks):</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Skill</th>\n<th>Base Chance</th>\n<th>Ranks</th>\n<th>Rating</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Heavy Weapons</td>\n<td>Might 15 + Agility 11 = 26</td>\n<td>20</td>\n<td>46</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Polearms</td>\n<td>Might 15 + Agility 11 = 26</td>\n<td>20</td>\n<td>46</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Athletics</td>\n<td>Might 15 + Endurance 13 = 28</td>\n<td>15</td>\n<td>43</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Intimidation</td>\n<td>Presence 9 + Might 15 = 24</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>34</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wilderness Survival</td>\n<td>Endurance 13 + Intellect 8 = 21</td>\n<td>15</td>\n<td>36</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Leadership</td>\n<td>Presence 9 + Willpower 10 = 19</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>29</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lore: History</td>\n<td>Intellect 8 + Intellect 8 = 16</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>26</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Derived Statistics:</strong> Health 23 \u00b7 Stamina 23 \u00b7 Resolve 20 \u00b7 Encumbrance 45 \u00b7 Melee Damage Bonus +5 \u00b7 Initiative +1 \u00b7 Defense Rating 11.</p>\n<p><strong>Drive \u2014 Atonement:</strong> she did a thing she cannot undo, and she&rsquo;s trying to be someone else now.</p>\n<p><strong>Conviction:</strong> <em>&ldquo;I will not follow an order that gets someone killed for nothing \u2014 not again.&rdquo;</em></p>\n<p><strong>Trauma \u2014 The Last Order:</strong> given a direct command by an authority figure, she needs a Willpower Reckoning to refuse it, even when refusal is obviously right. Note the tension this creates against her own Conviction \u2014 that&rsquo;s intentional.</p>\n<p><strong>Bonds:</strong> Ally \u2014 Cobb, the quartermaster who covered for her when she nearly deserted; he&rsquo;d lie to a tribunal for her, and has. Debt/Rival \u2014 a blood-debt owed to the family of a soldier who died following her order; unsettled, they&rsquo;ll come looking, and not alone.</p>\n<p><strong>Secret:</strong> she gave the order that got her unit killed at Aldwatch Ford \u2014 the official record blames the enemy&rsquo;s ambush. Only Cobb knows the truth. Stakes: her Trade Boon, her Leadership skill, and every soldier who ever serves under her again.</p>\n<p><strong>Masks and Truths \u2014 The Last Vow:</strong> dutiful and composed on the surface; privately unsure the vow was ever worth what it&rsquo;s cost.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 1000, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "xcPnca4Dr4V0FLX6", "name": "11. Techniques for the Other Skills", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>11. Techniques for the Other Skills</h2>\n<p>Chapter 1 promises that every skill unlocks a <strong>Technique</strong> at Adept (Rating 51+) and a second at Master (76+). Combat&rsquo;s live in Chapter 3, the crafts&rsquo; in Chapter 5; the rest \u2014 physical, knowledge, social, wilderness, and underworld \u2014 are here. A Technique is either a special option triggered on a <strong>Masterstroke</strong>, or an always-available option that costs a resource (Stamina, Resolve, time, or a point of Regard). <strong>At each tier you choose one of the two options listed</strong> \u2014 the other remains available to learn later from a teacher or a defining moment. Untrained characters have none; reaching the tier <em>is</em> the unlock.</p>\n<p><em>Restricted skills (Lore: Occult, Ritual Casting) gain their Techniques through Chapter 4, alongside the traditions they serve.</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Skill</th>\n<th>Adept \u2014 choose one at 51+</th>\n<th>Master \u2014 choose one at 76+</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Athletics</td>\n<td><strong>Second Wind</strong> \u2014 1/scene, spend no Stamina on an action that would cost it. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Sure Grip</strong> \u2014 you never fall from a climb on a Costly Success; the cost is time.</td>\n<td><strong>Unstoppable</strong> \u2014 on a Masterstroke, ignore difficult terrain and the first prone/Off-Balance this scene. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Iron Frame</strong> \u2014 once per fight, shrug a Crushing Grievous Wound down to an ordinary Wound.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Acrobatics</td>\n<td><strong>Cat&rsquo;s Landing</strong> \u2014 take no damage from a fall of Near or less; halve greater falls. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Tumbler&rsquo;s Guard</strong> \u2014 you may Dodge attacks from foes you can&rsquo;t see.</td>\n<td><strong>Impossible Line</strong> \u2014 on a Masterstroke, cross a space a foe controls without a free attack. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Wall-Runner</strong> \u2014 traverse sheer or moving surfaces for a few seconds as if they were ground.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stealth</td>\n<td><strong>Ghost-Step</strong> \u2014 stay hidden after a Costly Success that would reveal you, at the cost of your next action. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Blend</strong> \u2014 in a crowd or wilderness, become effectively unfindable without magic or a Masterstroke.</td>\n<td><strong>Second Skin</strong> \u2014 on a Masterstroke, a fooled watcher stays fooled the whole scene. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>The Quiet Kill</strong> \u2014 an attack from hiding on an unaware foe is automatically Grievous (already true) and silent.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wilderness Survival</td>\n<td><strong>Living Off the Land</strong> \u2014 provision yourself and one other indefinitely in known terrain. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Weather-Wise</strong> \u2014 read the coming day&rsquo;s weather; never caught unsheltered by surprise.</td>\n<td><strong>Read the Sky</strong> \u2014 on a Masterstroke, learn the next day&rsquo;s weather and gain +20 to act on it. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Trailmaster</strong> \u2014 you and your party ignore the first Condition of any journey through wild land.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Riding</td>\n<td><strong>As One</strong> \u2014 your mount acts on your Initiative and never panics from Fear below \u221220. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Trick Rider</strong> \u2014 fight, shoot, or leap from the saddle with no penalty.</td>\n<td><strong>The Long Gallop</strong> \u2014 on a Masterstroke, double travel distance for a day without tiring the beast. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>War-Trained</strong> \u2014 your mount fights as a partner: its hooves are your second attack in a melee.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lore: History</td>\n<td><strong>I Have Read of This</strong> \u2014 1/session, ask the GM one true fact about an institution, bloodline, or power. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Genealogist</strong> \u2014 trace any noble or guild line; know its debts, feuds, and skeletons.</td>\n<td><strong>Precedent</strong> \u2014 on a Masterstroke in a formal setting, cite history to cancel the Rig for that contest. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>The Long View</strong> \u2014 once per session, know how a faction has behaved in this exact situation before.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lore: Nature</td>\n<td><strong>Forager&rsquo;s Eye</strong> \u2014 identify plants, tracks, and safe water; never poisoned by ignorance. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Beast-Lore</strong> \u2014 know any animal&rsquo;s habits, range, and weaknesses on sight.</td>\n<td><strong>The Turning Season</strong> \u2014 on a Masterstroke, predict an ecological shift before its signs are plain. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Keystone Sense</strong> \u2014 name what a region&rsquo;s ecology depends on, and what its removal would cascade into.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Navigation</td>\n<td><strong>Dead Reckoning</strong> \u2014 never lose your way in known regions; halve travel time on charted routes. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Star-Read</strong> \u2014 fix your position and heading by night sky or coastline anywhere.</td>\n<td><strong>Uncharted</strong> \u2014 on a Masterstroke, map an unknown region well enough to grant +20 to those who follow. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>The Smuggler&rsquo;s Way</strong> \u2014 always know one route that avoids tolls, patrols, and witnesses.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Persuasion</td>\n<td><strong>Common Ground</strong> \u2014 on a Masterstroke, shift a listener&rsquo;s Regard one step for the scene. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Honest Face</strong> \u2014 +20 to be believed when you are, in fact, telling the truth.</td>\n<td><strong>The Turned Room</strong> \u2014 1/session, when you win a social exchange, sway a second undecided listener. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Kingmaker</strong> \u2014 on a Masterstroke before a group, move the whole room&rsquo;s Regard one step.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Intimidation</td>\n<td><strong>Cold Certainty</strong> \u2014 on a Masterstroke, a weaker-willed foe won&rsquo;t raise a hand this scene unless forced. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Reputation Precedes</strong> \u2014 1/session, a foe who&rsquo;s heard of you begins the scene already Shaken.</td>\n<td><strong>Name Your Fear</strong> \u2014 2 Resolve: impose your Presence Bonus as a Fear rating on one target. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Break the Line</strong> \u2014 on a Masterstroke vs a group, force an immediate Morale check at \u221220.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Deception</td>\n<td><strong>Straight Face</strong> \u2014 on a Masterstroke, an Insight Reckoning against this lie fails automatically. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Quick Cover</strong> \u2014 invent a plausible alias, story, or excuse with no time to prepare.</td>\n<td><strong>The Long Con</strong> \u2014 bank a Deception Masterstroke as an established fact to spend later. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>False Colors</strong> \u2014 maintain a full assumed identity indefinitely; only a Masterstroke or hard proof cracks it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Insight</td>\n<td><strong>Tell</strong> \u2014 on a Masterstroke, learn whether someone lies and the emotion driving it. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>First Read</strong> \u2014 at a glance, gauge a stranger&rsquo;s Caliber and general danger.</td>\n<td><strong>Read the Table</strong> \u2014 1/scene, learn each present NPC&rsquo;s strongest immediate Want. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Crack the Mask</strong> \u2014 on a Masterstroke, glimpse the Truth beneath an NPC&rsquo;s Mask (Ch. 2).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Leadership</td>\n<td><strong>Hold the Line</strong> \u2014 your Rally restores Morale to all who hear it, not one ally. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Drillmaster</strong> \u2014 allies you&rsquo;ve led before act on your Initiative in the first round.</td>\n<td><strong>Banner</strong> \u2014 on a Masterstroke, allies who see you gain your Presence Bonus vs Fear for the scene. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Last Stand</strong> \u2014 1/session, allies who can hear you ignore Shaken and cannot break for one round.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Streetwise</td>\n<td><strong>A Guy Who Knows a Guy</strong> \u2014 in any settlement, find a fence, bed, or name within the hour. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Local Knowledge</strong> \u2014 arrive in any town already knowing its powers, dangers, and grudges.</td>\n<td><strong>The Whisper Network</strong> \u2014 on a Masterstroke, plant a rumor that reaches a faction by next Season Turn. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Underworld Standing</strong> \u2014 call in a favor from the criminal element once per settlement.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tracking</td>\n<td><strong>Never Lost the Trail</strong> \u2014 a Costly Success follows the trail anyway; the cost is time. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Counter-Track</strong> \u2014 cover your party&rsquo;s trail so well that pursuers roll at \u221220.</td>\n<td><strong>Backtrack</strong> \u2014 on a Masterstroke, read a trail&rsquo;s past: number, weight, age, and what they feared. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>The Long Hunt</strong> \u2014 once locked onto a quarry, you always know its rough direction until you rest.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hunting</td>\n<td><strong>Killing Blow</strong> \u2014 on a Masterstroke vs a beast, the first hit lands as Grievous if it wounds. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Patient Blind</strong> \u2014 set an ambush that grants the whole party +20 to their first strikes.</td>\n<td><strong>Clean Harvest</strong> \u2014 on a Masterstroke, a harvest yields one extra part or one grade finer. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Master of the Hunt</strong> \u2014 you know each season what a region&rsquo;s game Dials will bear without collapse.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Larceny</td>\n<td><strong>Nimble Fingers</strong> \u2014 pick a common lock or lift a purse with no roll when unwatched. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Trapwise</strong> \u2014 spot and disarm mundane traps and alarms on sight.</td>\n<td><strong>No Trace</strong> \u2014 on a Masterstroke, leave no evidence a crime occurred until you&rsquo;re long gone. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>The Inside Job</strong> \u2014 on a Masterstroke casing a place, learn its guards, routines, and one weakness.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sailing</td>\n<td><strong>Weather the Blow</strong> \u2014 on a Masterstroke, bring a vessel through a storm that would wreck it. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Deft Hand</strong> \u2014 dock, launch, or come about in half the time and space others need.</td>\n<td><strong>Run the Blockade</strong> \u2014 on a Masterstroke, slip a watched strait or harbor without inspection. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Master Mariner</strong> \u2014 your ship travels a full day faster than its make should allow.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Not every table will use all of these, and that&rsquo;s fine \u2014 but no player who reaches Adept in a skill should find the shelf empty. Where a group wants a Technique the list doesn&rsquo;t cover, build it like a Legendary Ability: name the fictional mastery, then write the smallest rule that delivers it \u2014 a Masterstroke rider or a resource cost \u2014 and keep it roughly as strong as its neighbors here.</p>"}, "sort": 1100, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}], "folder": null, "sort": 200, "ownership": {"default": 0}, "flags": {}}
{"_id": "mefrGhUiUK16fwaT", "name": "Chapter 3 \u2014 Combat and Survival", "pages": [{"_id": "21buop4xWIHEukAT", "name": "Overview", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h1>CHAPTER 3: COMBAT AND SURVIVAL</h1>\n<p><em>Steel does not care how many years you carried it. Ask the veterans of Aldwatch Ford \u2014 the ones the crows left.</em></p>\n<p>Violence in Blightmarch is fast, ugly, and permanent. A fight avoided is usually a fight won; a fight taken is paid for in blood, stamina, nerve, or all three.</p>\n<p><strong>Contents:</strong> 1. The Shape of a Fight \u00b7 2. Attack and Defense \u00b7 3. Damage, Armor, and Wounds \u00b7 4. Death \u00b7 5. Exertion \u00b7 6. Fear, Morale, and the Mind \u00b7 7. Combat Techniques \u00b7 8. Survival \u00b7 9. Recovery and Healing \u00b7 10. Worked Example</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 0, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "ftF0UDpBVVR7M9FS", "name": "1. The Shape of a Fight", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>1. The Shape of a Fight</h2>\n<p><strong>Rounds and turns.</strong> A combat round is roughly five seconds. At the start of a fight, everyone rolls <strong>1d10 + Initiative</strong> (Agility Bonus, Chapter 1) and acts from highest to lowest.</p>\n<p>On your turn you get <strong>one Action</strong> plus free movement of one range band. Once per round \u2014 on anyone&rsquo;s turn \u2014 you may also take <strong>one Reaction</strong> (Parry or Dodge; Section 2). Your Reaction refreshes at the start of your own turn.</p>\n<p><strong>A second guard, at a price.</strong> When you have already spent your Reaction and are attacked again in the same round, you may <strong>spend 1 Stamina to Parry or Dodge that attack too</strong> \u2014 a desperate, tiring second guard. This is the difference between being outnumbered and being overwhelmed: a surrounded fighter can hold, but every extra guard burns wind they&rsquo;ll want later. You cannot spend Stamina you don&rsquo;t have, and an Exhausted character (Stamina 0) gets no second guard \u2014 which is often how the outnumbered finally fall.</p>\n<p><strong>Range bands.</strong> Distances are abstract: <strong>Reach</strong> (weapons touch), <strong>Near</strong> (a few strides \u2014 reachable with your free move), <strong>Far</strong> (a Sprint or more away). Weapons with the <em>Reach</em> trait strike foes entering Reach before those foes can act against the wielder.</p>\n<p><strong>Actions.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Action</th>\n<th>Effect</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Strike</td>\n<td>One attack Reckoning with a wielded weapon.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>All-Out Attack</td>\n<td>+20 to your Strike; forfeit your Reaction until your next turn. Costs 1 Stamina.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Guarded Attack</td>\n<td>\u221210 to your Strike; opponents take \u221210 attacking you until your next turn.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Charge</td>\n<td>Move up to two bands and Strike with +2 damage; attacks against you gain +10 until your next turn.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Aim</td>\n<td>+10 to your next ranged Strike (stacks to +20 over two rounds).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sprint</td>\n<td>Move two range bands. Costs 1 Stamina.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Withdraw</td>\n<td>Move one band away without provoking a free attack from Reach.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reload</td>\n<td>Ready a crossbow or similar <em>Reload</em> weapon.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>First Aid</td>\n<td>Medicine Reckoning to stop Bleeding on yourself or someone at Reach (Section 9).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rally</td>\n<td>Leadership Reckoning to restore a failed Morale test in allies who can hear you (Section 6).</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 100, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "KHGj4tYtp70cfHSZ", "name": "2. Attack and Defense", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>2. Attack and Defense</h2>\n<p><strong>Passive defense.</strong> Every attack Reckoning against a target is modified by the target&rsquo;s poise: <strong>\u22125 for every point of the target&rsquo;s Defense Rating above 10, +5 for every point below</strong> (Defense Rating = 10 + Agility Bonus, Chapter 1).</p>\n<p><strong>Unopposed attacks.</strong> If the target has no Reaction left, or is unaware of the attack, the attacker simply rolls. Unaware targets grant an additional +20, and the attacker&rsquo;s hit counts as Grievous if it deals any damage at all \u2014 a blade at an undefended throat needs no luck.</p>\n<p><strong>Opposed attacks.</strong> If the defender spends their Reaction \u2014 <strong>Parry</strong> (weapon skill or Shield Craft) or <strong>Dodge</strong> (Acrobatics) \u2014 both sides roll and compare degrees of success (Masterstroke &gt; Success &gt; Costly Success &gt; Failure &gt; Catastrophe):</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Comparison</th>\n<th>Result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Attacker higher by 1</td>\n<td>Hit. Roll damage normally.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Attacker higher by 2+</td>\n<td>Hit hard \u2014 <strong>+2 damage</strong> for a clean, well-placed blow.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tie</td>\n<td>The blow is turned \u2014 no damage. If the parrying implement is two size classes lighter (a dagger against a maul), the defender is <strong>Off-Balance</strong>: \u221210 to everything until their next turn.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Defender higher by 1</td>\n<td>Miss. The attack is avoided or turned cleanly.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Defender higher by 2+</td>\n<td><strong>Advantage.</strong> A parrying defender may immediately <strong>Riposte</strong> \u2014 a free Strike at \u221210, costing 1 Stamina. A dodging defender may instead move one band or force the attacker Off-Balance.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>A Costly Success that still wins the exchange hits <em>and</em> costs \u2014 the attacker&rsquo;s blade lodges, they drift Exposed (+10 to attacks against them until their next turn), the weapon takes a notch, or the GM invents worse. A <strong>Masterstroke</strong> that hits rolls damage twice and takes the higher, <strong>and its blow always counts as Grievous</strong> \u2014 roll on the weapon&rsquo;s critical table regardless of the damage total \u2014 and triggers Techniques (Section 7). A perfect strike finds the gap.</p>\n<p><strong>Catastrophes in combat (d6):</strong> 1 \u2014 weapon flies from your grip, lands Near; 2 \u2014 weapon breaks or is hopelessly lodged; 3 \u2014 you strike an adjacent ally (roll damage normally); 4 \u2014 you fall prone; 5 \u2014 you wound yourself (half damage); 6 \u2014 you&rsquo;re utterly Exposed: the enemy&rsquo;s next attack against you gains +20 and is automatically Grievous if it hits.</p>\n<p><strong>Ranged fire.</strong> Long range \u221220; partial cover \u221220, heavy cover \u221240. Firing into a melee: on any Failure, roll 1d6 \u2014 on a 1\u20132 the shot strikes a random other combatant instead. Bows reload freely; <em>Reload</em> weapons need the Reload Action.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 200, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "Av2XMiS8TJtTiFNt", "name": "3. Damage, Armor, and Wounds", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>3. Damage, Armor, and Wounds</h2>\n<p><strong>Damage</strong> = weapon dice + Melee Damage Bonus (melee and thrown only). Subtract the target&rsquo;s Armor Value (AV); the rest comes off Health.</p>\n<h3>Weapons</h3>\n<p>Every weapon belongs to a <strong>damage class</strong> \u2014 Blade, Crushing, or Piercing \u2014 which decides its critical table and its identity.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Weapon</th>\n<th>Damage</th>\n<th>Class</th>\n<th>Traits</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Dagger</td>\n<td>1d4</td>\n<td>Blade</td>\n<td>Close (usable in a grapple), Concealable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Arming sword</td>\n<td>1d8</td>\n<td>Blade</td>\n<td>Balanced (+5 to your Parries)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Longsword (2h)</td>\n<td>1d10</td>\n<td>Blade</td>\n<td>Balanced</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Greatsword (2h)</td>\n<td>2d6</td>\n<td>Blade</td>\n<td>Heavy (Might 12+, else \u221210)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hand axe</td>\n<td>1d6</td>\n<td>Blade</td>\n<td>Sunder, Throwable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Battle axe</td>\n<td>1d8</td>\n<td>Blade</td>\n<td>Sunder</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Warhammer</td>\n<td>1d8</td>\n<td>Crushing</td>\n<td>Brutal (target AV \u22121)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maul (2h)</td>\n<td>2d6</td>\n<td>Crushing</td>\n<td>Brutal, Heavy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Spear</td>\n<td>1d8</td>\n<td>Piercing</td>\n<td>Reach, Set (vs a charger: free Strike first, +2 damage)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Poleaxe (2h)</td>\n<td>1d10</td>\n<td>Piercing</td>\n<td>Reach, Armor-Piercing (halve AV, round up)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hunting bow</td>\n<td>1d8</td>\n<td>Piercing</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Warbow</td>\n<td>1d10</td>\n<td>Piercing</td>\n<td>Heavy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Crossbow</td>\n<td>1d10</td>\n<td>Piercing</td>\n<td>Armor-Piercing, Reload</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Unarmed</td>\n<td>1d3</td>\n<td>Crushing</td>\n<td>Close</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Sunder:</strong> when an axe scores a Masterstroke, or a shield turns it on a tie, the shield or struck armor takes a <strong>notch</strong>. Three notches splinter a shield or drop armor&rsquo;s AV by 1 (repairable \u2014 Chapter 5).</p>\n<h3>Armor</h3>\n<p>Armor keeps you alive and wears you down. <strong>Burden reduces your maximum Stamina</strong> \u2014 the game&rsquo;s answer to &ldquo;armor prevents damage but creates disadvantages.&rdquo; Burden also applies as \u22125 \u00d7 Burden to Stealth, Acrobatics, Swimming, and climbing.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Armor</th>\n<th>AV</th>\n<th>Burden</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Unarmored</td>\n<td>0 (but see below)</td>\n<td>0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Heavy leathers</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gambeson</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mail hauberk</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reinforced mail</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Half-plate</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Full harness</td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>4</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Even the unarmored are not bare meat.</strong> A character wearing no armor still reduces each hit&rsquo;s damage by <strong>1</strong> \u2014 the body flinching, the blow rolling off bone and muscle.</p>\n<p>A worn <strong>helm</strong> adds nothing to AV on its own but guards against the worst: while helmed, treat the first Grievous Wound you take each fight as an ordinary Wound instead. An open helm costs nothing; a closed great helm gives \u221210 to sight-and-hearing Reckonings and +5 Intimidation.</p>\n<p>Shields are parrying tools, not worn AV: a shield lets you Parry <em>missiles</em> (weapons can&rsquo;t) and grants +10 to your Parries.</p>\n<h3>Wounds</h3>\n<p>Compare each hit&rsquo;s <strong>net damage</strong> (after armor) to the target&rsquo;s <strong>Wound Threshold = Endurance \u00f7 2, round up.</strong></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>At Endurance 6 the Threshold is 3 \u2014 a Wound on 3+ net, a Grievous on 6+. At Endurance 20 it&rsquo;s 10 and 20. Low-Endurance characters die fast. <strong>Optional \u2014 the Hardened Floor:</strong> no character&rsquo;s Wound Threshold is ever less than 4.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Net damage below Threshold:</strong> the target loses that Health, no more. Blood, not crippling.</li>\n<li><strong>Net damage \u2265 Threshold: a Wound.</strong> Beyond the Health lost, the target takes a stacking <strong>\u22125 to all Reckonings</strong> until healed (Section 9), as pain, blood-loss, and shock accumulate. Two Wounds, \u221210 \u2014 <strong>and there it stops: however many Wounds pile up, the total never exceeds \u221210.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Net damage \u2265 2 \u00d7 Threshold: a Grievous Wound.</strong> The blow does something the body remembers: roll on the weapon&rsquo;s <strong>critical table</strong> (below) for the specific catastrophe \u2014 a severed artery, a shattered joint, a punctured lung \u2014 and apply the Wound penalty as well.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Critical Tables (d6, by damage class)</h3>\n<p><strong>Blades</strong> \u2014 <em>deep cuts, severed fingers, artery damage</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>d6</th>\n<th>Result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Deep laceration \u2014 Bleeding 2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Muscle sliced open \u2014 this Wound&rsquo;s \u22125 penalty is doubled to \u221210</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Severed finger(s) \u2014 drop anything held; permanent \u22125 with that hand</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Face laid open \u2014 Bleeding 1; permanent scar (GM may apply to Presence-based dealings)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Hamstrung / tendon cut \u2014 that limb useless until Surgery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Artery opened \u2014 Bleeding 4</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Crushing</strong> \u2014 <em>fractures, internal bleeding</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>d6</th>\n<th>Result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Cracked ribs \u2014 \u22122 maximum Stamina until healed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Fractured limb \u2014 useless; heals in weeks even with care</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Internal bleeding \u2014 hidden Bleeding 1: only a Medicine Reckoning detects it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Shattered kneecap or elbow \u2014 as fractured limb; permanent \u22125 after healing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Skull rung \u2014 unconscious 1d6 minutes; lingering headaches (\u22125 Intellect Reckonings, one week)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Ruptured organ \u2014 Bleeding 2 (internal); Death Reckonings at \u221220 if Downed by this fight</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Piercing</strong> \u2014 <em>penetration injuries</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>d6</th>\n<th>Result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Deep puncture \u2014 Bleeding 2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Lodged point \u2014 weapon or head stuck; wrenching it free deals 1d4 unless removed with Medicine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Pierced lung \u2014 Winded: all Stamina costs doubled until Surgery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Punctured gut \u2014 Bleeding 1; infection Reckoning at \u221240 (Section 8)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Through-and-through \u2014 Bleeding 2, but +20 to Surgery to treat it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Heart-struck or throat-pierced \u2014 drop to 0 Health immediately</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Fire</strong> \u2014 <em>burns, infection</em> (any fire or heat source that inflicts a Grievous Wound)</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>d6</th>\n<th>Result</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Searing pain \u2014 \u221210 to everything until treated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Catching \u2014 you are Burning: 1d4 damage at the end of each of your turns until extinguished (an Action)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Second-degree burns \u2014 infection Reckoning at \u221240; permanent scarring</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>Smoke-seared lungs \u2014 Winded until the end of the scene</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Burned hands \u2014 \u221220 to fine work until healed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Charred deep \u2014 as 2 and 3 together; the scar never fully fades</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Bleeding (X):</strong> lose X Health at the end of each of your turns. Halted by First Aid, or paused (not cured) by an ally applying pressure as their Action. Multiple sources stack.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 300, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "ZQ6ObmZxUVrYCROO", "name": "4. Death", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>4. Death</h2>\n<p><strong>No single blow ends a whole and healthy life outright.</strong> A character who is not yet Downed and stands <strong>above half their Health</strong> cannot be dropped below 0 by one hit \u2014 however monstrous the damage, it takes them to exactly <strong>0 Health and Downed</strong>, no further. The killing power is real, but it must be <em>finished</em>: the second blow, the Death Reckonings, the Bleeding. <em>(A character already below half Health, or already Downed, has no such protection.)</em></p>\n<p>At <strong>0 Health</strong> you are <strong>Downed</strong> \u2014 dying where you fall. At the start of each of your turns, make a <strong>Death Reckoning</strong>: d100 against <strong>Endurance \u00d7 5</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Masterstroke:</strong> you stabilize yourself \u2014 unconscious but no longer dying.</li>\n<li><strong>Success:</strong> you hold on.</li>\n<li><strong>Costly Success:</strong> you hold on, but carry something out of this \u2014 a permanent scar, tremor, or mark the GM defines when you wake.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure:</strong> mark a <strong>Death&rsquo;s Mark</strong>. At three Marks, you die.</li>\n<li><strong>Catastrophe:</strong> you die now.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Any hit taken while Downed inflicts an automatic Death&rsquo;s Mark; a Grievous Wound while Downed is death outright. A successful Medicine Reckoning stabilizes a Downed character.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 400, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "02ibK4Yje6s89ZM6", "name": "5. Exertion", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>5. Exertion</h2>\n<p>Stamina (Chapter 1) is combat&rsquo;s fuel and survival&rsquo;s ledger.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Costs:</strong> All-Out Attack, Sprint, Charge beyond the first in a fight, and Riposte each cost 1 Stamina. Techniques list their own costs.</li>\n<li><strong>Armor:</strong> Burden reduces <em>maximum</em> Stamina \u2014 a knight in full harness (Burden 4) fights with 4 less in the tank, always.</li>\n<li><strong>Conditions:</strong> Hungry, Freezing, and the rest (Section 8) each cut maximum Stamina by a further 2.</li>\n<li><strong>Exhausted (Stamina 0):</strong> \u221220 to everything, no Reactions, no Stamina-cost options. An Exhausted fighter is meat.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Stamina recovers 1 per hour of genuine rest, or fully with a night&rsquo;s sleep and a real meal \u2014 which is one of several reasons Cooking (Chapter 5) is a survival skill, not a flavor skill.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 500, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "LsBg1DpAINDS6Ab1", "name": "6. Fear, Morale, and the Mind", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>6. Fear, Morale, and the Mind</h2>\n<h3>Composure</h3>\n<p>When facing something that ought to break a person \u2014 the dead walking, a friend opened up beside you, a thing with too many mouths \u2014 make a <strong>Composure Reckoning</strong>: d100 against <strong>Willpower \u00d7 5</strong>, modified by the source&rsquo;s <strong>Fear rating</strong> (monsters in Chapter 7 carry these, e.g. <em>Fear \u221220</em>).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Masterstroke:</strong> unshakable \u2014 immune to this source for the scene; allies who can see you gain +5 to their own Composure against it.</li>\n<li><strong>Success:</strong> you hold.</li>\n<li><strong>Costly Success:</strong> you hold, but Shaken: \u221210 to everything while the source persists.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure:</strong> <strong>Shaken</strong>, and you cannot willingly close to Reach with the source.</li>\n<li><strong>Catastrophe (or a second Failure):</strong> <strong>Panicked</strong> \u2014 flee the source by the fastest route or freeze (player&rsquo;s choice of flavor; no Actions against the source) for 1d4 rounds.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>NPC Morale</h3>\n<p>Groups carry a <strong>Morale rating</strong>: Wavering 30, Steady 50, Hard 70, Fanatic 90. Test (d100 \u2264 rating) when their leader drops, half their number are down, a comrade dies gruesomely, or a Fear source manifests. Failure means rout, surrender, or fighting retreat per their temperament. A leader&rsquo;s <strong>Rally</strong> Action (Leadership Reckoning) restores one failed test \u2014 the Former Soldier&rsquo;s Trade Boon (Chapter 2) adds +10 here.</p>\n<h3>Aftermath</h3>\n<p>Violence follows people home. After any of the following, each character involved makes a Composure Reckoning in the quiet afterward: their first kill; killing the helpless or surrendered; surviving a massacre; torture, inflicted or endured; a Bonded ally dying in their arms; the openly unnatural.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Failure:</strong> gain a new <strong>Trauma</strong> (Chapter 2 table, or one written for the moment).</li>\n<li><strong>Costly Success:</strong> Shaken until the next full night&rsquo;s rest.</li>\n<li><strong>Masterstroke:</strong> hard-won steel \u2014 +5 Composure against that category of horror thereafter.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 600, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "fl6ikcew4qbT9cTo", "name": "7. Combat Techniques", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>7. Combat Techniques</h2>\n<p>Chapter 1 promised Technique lists; here are combat&rsquo;s. Adept Techniques unlock at Skill Rating 51+, Master at 76+. <strong>When a character reaches Adept (51+) in a skill, they choose <em>one</em> of that skill&rsquo;s two Adept Techniques; at Master (76+), one of the two Master Techniques.</strong> The unchosen option is not lost forever \u2014 a later teacher or hard-won insight can grant it \u2014 but a character has one of each at a time. Legendary Abilities remain bespoke \u2014 built with the GM around a defining deed, never from a list.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Skill</th>\n<th>Adept \u2014 choose one at 51+</th>\n<th>Master \u2014 choose one at 76+</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Heavy Weapons</td>\n<td><strong>Cleaving Blow</strong> \u2014 on a Masterstroke, damage beyond what drops your target carries into an adjacent foe. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Crushing Advance</strong> \u2014 1 Stamina: on a hit, drive your foe back one band and follow; they cannot Riposte.</td>\n<td><strong>Executioner&rsquo;s Arc</strong> \u2014 2 Stamina: one Strike at \u221210 against two foes at Reach. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Sundering Might</strong> \u2014 your Sunder notches ignore the shield entirely, striking the arm behind it.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Light Weapons</td>\n<td><strong>Serpent&rsquo;s Reply</strong> \u2014 your Ripostes cost no Stamina. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Duelist&rsquo;s Read</strong> \u2014 spend your Reaction to study a foe: +10 to attack and Parry vs them next round.</td>\n<td><strong>Red Whisper</strong> \u2014 1 Stamina: after you hit, force the target to reroll their next Parry/Dodge, worse result. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Thousand Cuts</strong> \u2014 on a Masterstroke, apply Bleeding 1 in addition to damage.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Polearms</td>\n<td><strong>Braced Hedge</strong> \u2014 Set triggers against any foe entering Reach, not only chargers. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Sweep the Line</strong> \u2014 1 Stamina: a low sweep, target Acrobatics or fall prone.</td>\n<td><strong>Iron Hedge</strong> \u2014 your Reaction may Parry attacks against allies at Reach. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Impaling Set</strong> \u2014 a Set hit that Wounds also holds the foe fast: an Action to pull free.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Unarmed &amp; Grappling</td>\n<td><strong>Lock and Throw</strong> \u2014 winning a grapple by 2+ throws the foe prone for free. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Disarming Grip</strong> \u2014 win a grapple exchange to strip a held weapon instead of damage.</td>\n<td><strong>Bonebreaker</strong> \u2014 1 Stamina: your unarmed Grievous Wounds roll Crushing at +1. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Chokehold</strong> \u2014 a held foe must pass Endurance\u00d75 each round or fall unconscious.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Archery</td>\n<td><strong>Hawk&rsquo;s Patience</strong> \u2014 your Aim bonus caps at +30. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Snap Shot</strong> \u2014 draw and loose as a Reaction against a foe closing from Far.</td>\n<td><strong>Threading the Eye</strong> \u2014 2 Stamina: your shot ignores cover entirely. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Heartseeker</strong> \u2014 on a Masterstroke at Far or nearer, the hit is automatically Grievous.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Thrown Weapons</td>\n<td><strong>Sudden Steel</strong> \u2014 draw and throw in one motion; +10 vs a foe who hasn&rsquo;t acted. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Ricochet</strong> \u2014 a missed throw may strike a second foe at Near (re-roll vs them).</td>\n<td><strong>Pinning Cast</strong> \u2014 on a Masterstroke, the struck limb is pinned to terrain. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Fan of Blades</strong> \u2014 2 Stamina: throw three knives at three foes at \u221210 each.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shield Craft</td>\n<td><strong>Shield Wall</strong> \u2014 allies at Reach gain +5 to defenses while your shield holds. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Shield Bash</strong> \u2014 1 Stamina: after a Parry, a free Crushing 1d4 that may stagger.</td>\n<td><strong>Bulwark</strong> \u2014 1/round, negate one hit on you or an ally at Reach; shield takes a notch. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Immovable</strong> \u2014 you cannot be driven back, knocked prone, or moved against your will while braced.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 700, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "8dgFQbtE6D9ZYCb3", "name": "8. Survival", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>8. Survival</h2>\n<p><em>The Forbidden Lands inheritance: the wilderness kills more travelers than bandits ever will.</em></p>\n<h3>Conditions</h3>\n<p>Five Conditions track privation: <strong>Hungry</strong> (a day without a real meal), <strong>Parched</strong> (half a day without water), <strong>Freezing</strong> (cold without adequate warmth), <strong>Sweltering</strong> (heat plus exertion), <strong>Sleepless</strong> (no proper sleep). Each Condition: <strong>\u22125 to all Reckonings and \u22122 maximum Stamina</strong>, stacking.</p>\n<p><strong>Escalation:</strong> for each full day a Condition persists, make an Endurance Reckoning (Endurance \u00d7 5) or lose 1d4 Health \u2014 and Health cannot recover while the Condition holds. Parched escalates every <em>half</em>-day after the first day. Freezing and Sweltering escalate per day of exposure and can inflict Grievous Wounds (frostbite, heatstroke) on a Catastrophe.</p>\n<h3>Travel</h3>\n<p>A travel day holds four watches: morning, day, evening, night. On roads a party covers ~15 miles a day on foot; ~8 in rough country; mounts roughly double it. A <strong>Forced March</strong> adds half again \u2014 and each marcher takes the Sleepless or Hungry Condition (their choice of which corner got cut).</p>\n<p>Each evening, one character makes a <strong>Wilderness Survival Reckoning</strong> to make camp (helpers grant +5 each, max +10): Success \u2014 safe camp, normal recovery; Costly \u2014 recovery with a complication (gear damp, fire smokes, watch disturbed); Failure \u2014 cold camp, no Stamina recovery; Catastrophe \u2014 hazard or encounter finds you. Foraging and Hunting Reckonings during a watch produce rations and raw ingredients \u2014 the supply line into Cooking (Chapter 5) and harvesting (Chapter 7).</p>\n<h3>Hazards</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Falling:</strong> 1d6 per 2 yards; Acrobatics Reckoning halves it.</li>\n<li><strong>Drowning:</strong> hold your breath for Endurance rounds; then Death Reckonings each round.</li>\n<li><strong>Fire:</strong> 1d4\u20131d10 per round by size; any Grievous result uses the Fire critical table.</li>\n<li><strong>Infection:</strong> any Wound untreated for a day \u2014 or immediately for gut and burn Grievous Wounds \u2014 demands an Endurance Reckoning (\u221220 if Grievous, \u221240 for gut/burn). Failure: <strong>Infected</strong>, a Condition that also blocks <em>all</em> healing. Each dawn, a new Endurance Reckoning: Masterstroke shakes it off; Failure costs 1d4 Health; Catastrophe deepens it (\u221220 to future checks). Medicine and Herbalism care grants +20 and a fresh check.</li>\n<li><strong>Disease:</strong> each disease carries a <strong>Virulence</strong> penalty to the victim&rsquo;s Endurance Reckonings and a three-stage course (onset \u2192 grip \u2192 crisis). The framework lives here; the plague list arrives with the bestiary and GM tools (Chapters 7\u20138).</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 800, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "qNYVqmgrHnz8loqx", "name": "9. Recovery and Healing", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>9. Recovery and Healing</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Natural healing:</strong> with food and rest, regain (1 + Endurance Bonus, minimum 1) Health per day. Halved in the field. Zero while any Condition or Infection holds.</li>\n<li><strong>First Aid</strong> (Medicine, one minute): stops Bleeding; once per person per day, restores 1d4 Health.</li>\n<li><strong>Surgery</strong> (Medicine; \u221220 in field conditions): required to treat Grievous Wounds and their critical effects. The Wandering Medic&rsquo;s Trade Boon (Chapter 2) \u2014 an extra Wound level healed on a Masterstroke \u2014 applies here.</li>\n<li><strong>Wounds:</strong> a Wound heals in one week (halved under Medicine care). A Grievous Wound needs successful Surgery, then 2 + 1d4 weeks; left untreated, it heals <em>wrong</em> \u2014 a permanent \u22125 or lasting effect chosen by the GM.</li>\n<li><strong>What&rsquo;s gone is gone:</strong> severed fingers, lost eyes, and their kin do not grow back. Chapter 4 will offer ways around that. None of them are free.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Treating Grievous Wounds \u2014 a reference for the table</h3>\n<p>Every result on the four critical tables answers three questions: <em>what stops it now</em>, <em>what heals it</em>, and <em>what it leaves behind</em>. Two rulings settle most of them. First: <strong>internal Bleeding cannot be halted by First Aid or by pressure</strong> \u2014 you can&rsquo;t put a hand on a burst organ or a collapsed lung. It stops only when Surgery succeeds, or when a rite intervenes. External Bleeding follows the normal rule (First Aid stops it; an ally&rsquo;s pressure pauses it). Second: <strong>Surgery is the gate.</strong> A Grievous Wound&rsquo;s critical effect persists until a Medicine Reckoning treats it (\u221220 in the field), after which the standard 2 + 1d4 weeks of recovery apply; left untreated, it heals <em>wrong</em> into a permanent mark.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Critical effect</th>\n<th>Stops now</th>\n<th>Heals with</th>\n<th>Leaves behind</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ruptured organ</strong> (Crushing 6)</td>\n<td>Surgery only \u2014 the internal Bleeding 2 is not First-Aidable</td>\n<td>Successful Surgery, then 2 + 1d4 weeks bed rest; abdominal infection check applies</td>\n<td>Costly/untreated: reduced max Stamina or a permanent \u22125; the \u221220 to Death Reckonings ends when the bleeding is stopped</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Pierced lung</strong> (Piercing 3)</td>\n<td>Surgery to close; until then, Winded (Stamina costs doubled)</td>\n<td>Successful Surgery, then full recovery weeks</td>\n<td>Untreated: the Winded effect becomes permanent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Punctured gut</strong> (Piercing 4)</td>\n<td>First Aid stops the external Bleeding 1; the wound still needs Surgery</td>\n<td>Surgery + recovery; <strong>immediate</strong> infection check at \u221240</td>\n<td>Infection (blocks all healing) if the check fails; a wrong-healed \u22125 otherwise</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Through-and-through</strong> (Piercing 5)</td>\n<td>Pressure pauses it; Surgery stops it (+20 to that Surgery)</td>\n<td>Surgery + recovery</td>\n<td>Clean scar if treated promptly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Heart-struck / throat-pierced</strong> (Piercing 6)</td>\n<td>Nothing \u2014 drop to 0 Health, now Downed</td>\n<td>Stabilize first (Medicine), then treat as any Downed character</td>\n<td>Whatever the Downed rules and the Costly Success clause hand out</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Fractured / shattered limb</strong> (Crushing 2 / 4)</td>\n<td>Splint (First Aid) to allow travel; the limb stays useless</td>\n<td>Sets and heals over weeks even with care</td>\n<td>Shattered joint: permanent \u22125 after healing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hamstrung / tendon cut</strong> (Blades 5)</td>\n<td>Nothing stops the disability; bind to move</td>\n<td>Surgery, then recovery</td>\n<td>Untreated: the limb stays useless</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Severed finger / lost eye / severed part</strong> (Blades 3, and kin)</td>\n<td>First Aid stops the Bleeding</td>\n<td>Nothing regrows it \u2014 Chapter 4&rsquo;s Quickened Graft is the only road, and it bills in donor flesh and Ash</td>\n<td>Permanent, always</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Burns, second/third degree</strong> (Fire)</td>\n<td>First Aid; keep it clean</td>\n<td>Recovery weeks; infection check at \u221240</td>\n<td>Permanent scarring; burned hands \u221220 to fine work until healed</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>The sorcerous road, briefly.</strong> The Red Tithe (Chapter 4) can close what surgery can&rsquo;t \u2014 but the Red Mending <em>relocates</em> a wound onto a donor rather than erasing it, and the Quickened Graft regrows a lost part from living donor tissue. Both cost Resolve and Ash and risk Backlash. Magic turns a grievous wound into a different problem; it never makes it free. <em>(This box is table-reference guidance, not a new subsystem \u2014 every ruling here follows from Surgery, Bleeding, infection, and the Red Tithe as already written.)</em></p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 900, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "VhDso5pG9xbY9xlu", "name": "10. Worked Example: Yorna on the Road", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>10. Worked Example: Yorna on the Road</h2>\n<p><em>Yorna Vask (Chapter 2: Health 23, Stamina 23 \u2212 1 Burden = 22, Wound Threshold 7, Defense Rating 11, Polearms 46, spear 1d8+5) is stopped on a forest track by two brigands \u2014 arming swords, gambesons, Light Weapons 35, Morale Wavering 30.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Initiative:</strong> Yorna d10+1 = 8; brigands roll 6. She acts first \u2014 and Sets her spear.</p>\n<p><strong>Round 1.</strong> Brigand A charges. Her Set spear triggers: free Strike at 46, +2 damage. She rolls 33 \u2014 Success; he doesn&rsquo;t Parry (saving his Reaction). Damage 1d8+5+2: she rolls 6 \u2192 13, minus his gambeson AV 2 \u2192 <strong>11 net</strong>. His Threshold is 5; 11 \u2265 10 is a <strong>Grievous Wound</strong>: \u22125 to everything, and the Piercing table comes up 4: <em>punctured gut</em> (Bleeding 1, an infection check to come). He&rsquo;s at 9 Health, leaking, and the charge dies on her point. His own attack now comes at \u22125 (her Defense Rating 11): effective 30, minus his Grievous \u22125. He rolls 44 \u2014 Failure.</p>\n<p>Brigand B swings at 30 effective; rolls 22 \u2014 Success. Yorna spends her Reaction to Parry: 46, rolls 9 \u2014 46 \u00f7 5 rounds to 10, so a <strong>Masterstroke</strong>. She wins by 2+: <strong>Advantage</strong>. She Ripostes (1 Stamina, \u221210, at 36): rolls 31 \u2014 Success. 1d8+5 = 10, minus his AV 2 \u2192 8 net \u2265 7: a <strong>Wound</strong> \u2014 he takes \u22125 and is rattled.</p>\n<p><strong>Round 2.</strong> Bleeding drops A to 8. Yorna Strikes him: 46, rolls 17 \u2014 Success; damage 11, minus AV 2 \u2192 9 net. A is <strong>Downed</strong>. Brigand B watches his partner fold around a spear: <strong>Morale test</strong>, d100 = 61 against 30 \u2014 he breaks and runs.</p>\n<p><strong>Aftermath:</strong> armed men, a fair fight \u2014 no Composure check owed. But Yorna binds A&rsquo;s gut wound rather than leave him to bleed out, because her Conviction is watching. Had a sergeant been bellowing at her to stand down mid-fight, <em>The Last Order</em> would have had its teeth in her throat \u2014 that&rsquo;s the seam where Chapter 2 meets this one.</p>"}, "sort": 1000, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}], "folder": null, "sort": 300, "ownership": {"default": 0}, "flags": {}}
{"_id": "aRfGCgHmafAbgve6", "name": "Chapter 4 \u2014 Magic and Corruption", "pages": [{"_id": "i2syCkDG78XrBAGQ", "name": "Overview", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h1>CHAPTER 4: MAGIC AND CORRUPTION</h1>\n<p><em>The old age did not end. It died \u2014 and everything that dies leaves a wound. Sorcery is the art of reaching into that wound and taking. The wound reaches back.</em></p>\n<p>Magic is not a career or a class here. It is rare, feared, and it costs \u2014 always. There are no spell slots: there is knowledge, preparation, sacrifice, and what the Art leaves behind in anyone who practices it.</p>\n<p><strong>Contents:</strong> 1. What Magic Is \u00b7 2. The Caster&rsquo;s Toolkit \u00b7 3. Casting a Rite \u00b7 4. Resolve in Full \u00b7 5. Ash \u2014 the Corruption System \u00b7 6. The Six Traditions \u00b7 7. Forbidden Knowledge and Magical Disease \u00b7 8. Witnessing the Art \u00b7 9. Worked Example</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 0, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "SBcT4sFo7q9K08pt", "name": "1. What Magic Is", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>1. What Magic Is</h2>\n<p>When the old age died, it tore something on its way down. Scholars who won&rsquo;t say the word call it <em>the discontinuity</em>. Everyone else calls it <strong>the Seam</strong> \u2014 the ragged join between the living world and whatever the dying one became. All sorcery is Seamcraft: prying that join open a finger&rsquo;s width and taking what leaks through.</p>\n<p>What leaks back is <strong>Ash</strong> \u2014 the residue of a dead age settling into living flesh and soul. Every tradition of the Art is a different technique for opening the Seam; every one of them pays in Ash eventually.</p>\n<p>This is why magic is feared, and why the fear is <em>correct</em>. Villages burn hedge-witches not from ignorance but from experience. Kingdoms license perhaps a dozen practitioners and hunt the rest. A revealed caster loses more than reputation: in most lands, sorcery outside sanction is a capital crime, and witch-finders (Chapter 6) are paid by the head.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 100, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "UWzQnwW2UoxL89cE", "name": "2. The Caster's Toolkit", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>2. The Caster&rsquo;s Toolkit</h2>\n<p>Two Restricted skills (Chapter 1) govern the Art. Neither can be taken at character creation without an explicit story reason.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lore: Occult (R)</strong> \u2014 knowing. Identifying workings, reading grimoires, recognizing what a Brand means, knowing which questions never to ask aloud.</li>\n<li><strong>Ritual Casting (R)</strong> \u2014 doing. The discipline of actually opening the Seam without being unmade.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Rites</h3>\n<p>A caster does not &ldquo;know spells.&rdquo; They know <strong>Rites</strong> \u2014 specific, named procedures, each learned individually from a grimoire, a teacher, or torn out of the dark by experiment. Every Rite lists its Tradition, its <strong>Grade</strong>, its requirements, and its costs.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Grade</th>\n<th>Name</th>\n<th>Preparation</th>\n<th>Base Cost</th>\n<th>Base Ash</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>I</td>\n<td>Lesser</td>\n<td>One minute</td>\n<td>2 Resolve</td>\n<td>0 (1 on Costly Success or Catastrophe)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>II</td>\n<td>Greater</td>\n<td>Ten minutes</td>\n<td>4 Resolve</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>III</td>\n<td>Profane</td>\n<td>One hour, prepared ground</td>\n<td>6 Resolve + a stated sacrifice</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IV</td>\n<td>Forbidden</td>\n<td>A full night, prepared ground</td>\n<td>8+ Resolve + a grave sacrifice</td>\n<td>3+</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Learning a Rite</strong> requires the listed Lore: Occult tier (Grade I: Novice; II: Journeyman; III: Adept; IV: Master), access to a source, and downtime. <strong>Holding Rites:</strong> a caster keeps <strong>Intellect \u00f7 2 (round down)</strong> Rites <em>held</em> \u2014 castable from memory. Any others must be cast from the text at \u221210 (this is the Ritual capacity promised in Chapter 1).</p>\n<p><strong>Grimoires are treasure.</strong> Rites travel as physical books, tablets, and worse. A discovered grimoire is faction bait, cult bait, and a regional power shift in one object (Chapter 6).</p>\n<p><strong>Wild Working.</strong> Improvising an effect with no learned Rite is possible and stupid: the GM sets the Grade, the Reckoning takes \u221220, Ash is +1 over base \u2014 and it counts as Experimentation (Chapter 1): the skill marks twice, and both 99 and 100 are Catastrophes.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 200, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "T19bGsT5nHpN2uay", "name": "3. Casting a Rite", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>3. Casting a Rite</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Meet the requirements: components, preparation time, sacrifice if listed.</li>\n<li>Pay the Resolve cost.</li>\n<li>Make a <strong>Ritual Casting Reckoning</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Result</th>\n<th>Outcome</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Masterstroke</td>\n<td>Full effect; the base Ash is waived.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Success</td>\n<td>Full effect; pay the listed Ash.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Costly Success</td>\n<td>Effect <em>and</em> the Rite&rsquo;s <strong>Backlash</strong> \u2014 its tradition-flavored bite; pay Ash.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Failure</td>\n<td>Nothing. The Resolve is spent anyway. The Seam does not refund.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Catastrophe</td>\n<td>Roll the tradition&rsquo;s Catastrophe; Ash is doubled <em>(see the ordering rule below)</em>.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Ash ordering.</strong> Some Rites list a bump \u2014 <em>&ldquo;Ash 0 (1 on Costly Success or Catastrophe)&rdquo;</em> \u2014 that raises the base cost on those two degrees. <strong>Apply the degree bump first, then any Catastrophe doubling.</strong> So a Grade I Rite with that clause costs <strong>2 Ash on a Catastrophe</strong> (the bump makes it 1, the doubling makes it 2), 1 Ash on a Costly Success, and 0 otherwise. A Rite with no bump clause simply doubles its listed base.</p>\n<p><strong>Modifiers.</strong> Rushing a Grade I\u2013II Rite to a single combat round: \u221220 and +1 Ash (Grades III\u2013IV cannot be rushed). Assistants with any Ritual Casting rank grant +5 each (max +15) and may pay part of the Resolve cost from their own pools \u2014 the arithmetic that makes cults dangerous (Chapter 6). Casting from the text instead of a held Rite: \u221210.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 300, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "sqolh6llxsBx0fze", "name": "4. Resolve in Full", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>4. Resolve in Full</h2>\n<p><strong>Resolve = Willpower \u00d7 2</strong> (Chapter 1) \u2014 the mind&rsquo;s fuel: will, nerve, and self.</p>\n<p><strong>Spending it (anyone, not just casters):</strong>\n- <strong>Casting</strong> \u2014 Rites cost Resolve as listed.\n- <strong>Grit the Teeth</strong> \u2014 2 Resolve before rolling a Composure Reckoning: +10.\n- <strong>Defy the Flinch</strong> \u2014 3 Resolve to suppress a Trauma&rsquo;s Flinch for one trigger. The Trauma remains; you white-knuckled it, once.</p>\n<p><strong>Recovering it:</strong>\n- A full night&rsquo;s rest restores <strong>half of maximum (round up)</strong>. <em>(The Lay Priest&rsquo;s Trade Boon \u2014 full recovery on a night&rsquo;s rest \u2014 now means what it says.)</em>\n- A full day of genuine peace restores it entirely.\n- Acting on your Conviction at real cost: +1 (Chapter 2).</p>\n<p><strong>The floor.</strong> Resolve cannot drop below 0, and <strong>a Rite cannot be attempted without the full listed cost in hand</strong> \u2014 the Seam does not extend credit. (Assistants may cover part of the cost from their own pools, as in Section 3; the shortfall rule applies to the combined pot.)</p>\n<p><strong>Spent (Resolve 0):</strong> you cannot cast, take \u221210 to all Willpower-based Reckonings, and any <em>Failure</em> on a Composure Reckoning counts as a Catastrophe \u2014 an empty vessel panics easily.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 400, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "D9CwNwvF24QMwvE8", "name": "5. Ash \u2014 the Corruption System", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>5. Ash \u2014 the Corruption System</h2>\n<p><strong>Ash</strong> is corruption: the Seam&rsquo;s residue in a living person. It comes in two forms.</p>\n<p><strong>Temporary Ash</strong> accrues from casting (Section 3), and from the world:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Source</th>\n<th>Ash</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A night spent in a Blighted place</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Handling a potent relic unwarded</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Consuming Seam-touched flesh or ingredients</td>\n<td>1+ (Chapters 5 and 7)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Studying a Weighted text, per week</td>\n<td>Text&rsquo;s Weight (Section 7)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Certain monster abilities</td>\n<td>Listed per creature (Chapter 7)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Temporary Ash fades at <strong>1 per full day without casting or exposure</strong>. Certain preparations purge it faster (Section 6, the Patient Art; Chapter 5).</p>\n<h3>The Ash Threshold and Blighting</h3>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Ash Threshold = Willpower.</strong> <em>(This is the Corruption Threshold promised in Chapter 1.)</em></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The moment temporary Ash <strong>equals or exceeds</strong> the Threshold, the character is <strong>Blighted</strong>: roll d10 on the Marks of the Seam table, gain that <strong>Brand</strong> permanently, and clear temporary Ash to zero. There is no save. Willpower already did its work by setting the Threshold; the Seam does not negotiate past it.</p>\n<h3>Marks of the Seam (d10)</h3>\n<p>Brands are permanent. Several of them help.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>d10</th>\n<th>Brand</th>\n<th>Effect</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Ash-Veined</td>\n<td>Grey webbing under the skin. \u22125 to Presence-based Reckonings with strangers; +5 Intimidation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Beast-Eyes</td>\n<td>See in darkness as dim light. Animals refuse you: \u221210 Riding and handling.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Numb Flesh</td>\n<td>Ignore the \u22125 from one Wound at a time \u2014 but you don&rsquo;t feel Bleeding; the GM tracks it in secret.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>The Wrong Shadow</td>\n<td>Your shadow lags and looks elsewhere. \u221210 Stealth in any light; once per day it warns you: +10 against one ambush.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Cold Hearth</td>\n<td>Fires gutter near you. Fire damage against you \u22121; everything you cook tastes of ash (\u221210 Cooking).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>Whisper-Ear</td>\n<td>Once per session, ask the GM one yes/no question; the Seam answers truthfully. Each use: +1 Ash.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>The Other Hunger</td>\n<td>You must consume something unspeakable daily \u2014 grave-soil, tallow, raw liver \u2014 or take a Hungry Condition no food removes.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Leaking Dreams</td>\n<td>Sleepers near you share your nightmares: the whole camp takes \u22125 to recovery unless you sleep apart.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Stitched Voice</td>\n<td>Two voices speak when you do. \u221210 Deception; +5 Ritual Casting.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>The Burning Sigil</td>\n<td>A scar-glyph. Your Rites cost 1 less Resolve \u2014 and anyone trained to see it senses you within Near range.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Hollowing</h3>\n<p>At <strong>Brands \u2265 half Willpower (round up)</strong>, the character <strong>Hollows</strong>: over the following days the Seam finishes moving in. The character becomes the GM&rsquo;s \u2014 a vessel, a herald, a thing wearing a familiar face. The player narrates the leaving. This is the game&rsquo;s hard doom-clock.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>GM guidance \u2014 the drip rate.</strong> Ash fades one point per clean day, so a cautious caster who rests between workings may never Blight on their own rites alone. Corruption is paced by the <em>environment</em>: nights in Blighted ground (1), Seam-touched meals (their Corruption rating, per bite), unwarded relics (1), a week over a Weighted text (its Weight). Aim for an active caster earning a <strong>Brand every six to eight sessions</strong>. Zero Brands after a dozen sessions means turn the pressure up; Hollowing every few means ease off.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h3>Cleansing</h3>\n<p>Rest removes temporary Ash slowly; the Grey Draught (Section 6) removes it painfully; nothing reliably removes a Brand. Rumors persist \u2014 a spring, a saint&rsquo;s bones, a rite that trades a Brand for something worse. Every one of those rumors is a sandbox hook, and at least one of them should be true in any campaign.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 500, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "kSJnTE0yInmiTRwW", "name": "6. The Six Traditions", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>6. The Six Traditions</h2>\n<p>Each tradition is a different way of opening the Seam, with its own price written into its bones. Two sample Rites each; full rosters belong to discovered grimoires, which is to say, to play.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>The Red Tithe (Blood Magic)</h3>\n<p><em>Life is the only coin the Seam respects at face value.</em> The Red Tithe pays in blood and living tissue \u2014 the caster&rsquo;s, or someone else&rsquo;s. Using another&rsquo;s is faster, stronger, and filthier: <strong>any Rite paid with unwilling blood adds +1 Ash.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>THE RED MENDING</strong> <em>(Grade II)</em>\n- <strong>Requirements:</strong> Lore: Occult Journeyman; the wounded subject at Reach; a blade.\n- <strong>Cost:</strong> 4 Resolve; the donor (caster or another) takes damage equal to all Health restored, and one Wound of the same severity migrates to the donor&rsquo;s matching location. Ash 1.\n- <strong>Effect:</strong> Close the subject&rsquo;s Wounds and restore Health \u2014 moved, not made. The Seam only relocates debts.\n- <strong>Backlash:</strong> The migrated Wound arrives <em>wrong</em> \u2014 infected, or one severity worse.\n- <strong>Catastrophe:</strong> Both bodies keep the wounds. The blood remembers being asked.</p>\n<p><strong>THE QUICKENED GRAFT</strong> <em>(Grade III)</em> \u2014 <em>Chapter 3&rsquo;s promise, kept.</em>\n- <strong>Requirements:</strong> Lore: Occult Adept; prepared ground; a living donor part \u2014 limb, eye, finger \u2014 matching what was lost, willingly given or freshly taken.\n- <strong>Cost:</strong> 6 Resolve; the sacrifice is the donor part itself; Ash 2 (+1 if taken unwillingly).\n- <strong>Effect:</strong> The lost part regrows over a night, knitted from the donor&rsquo;s flesh. Fully functional.\n- <strong>Backlash:</strong> The graft <em>remembers its first owner</em> \u2014 a mannerism, a tremor toward old habits, \u22125 to fine work with it for a season.\n- <strong>Catastrophe:</strong> It takes. It grows. It is not yours, and it is not finished.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>The Old Tongue (Elemental Magic)</h3>\n<p><em>Fire, wind, water, and stone remember being spoken to, and they have missed it.</em> The Old Tongue commands rather than creates \u2014 and the elements obey the letter, never the spirit. <strong>Standing rule: an element commanded does not stop when you are finished with it</strong>, and Grade II+ workings of the Old Tongue burn like beacons \u2014 the GM checks whether something that feeds on such light was close enough to notice.</p>\n<p><strong>COALHEART</strong> <em>(Grade I)</em>\n- <strong>Requirements:</strong> Lore: Occult Novice; an existing flame, candle to bonfire.\n- <strong>Cost:</strong> 2 Resolve. Ash 0 (1 on Costly/Catastrophe).\n- <strong>Effect:</strong> The flame swells, banks, or leaps to a target within Near (Strike vs. the target&rsquo;s Defense as normal; 1d6 Fire damage, and any Grievous result uses Chapter 3&rsquo;s Fire critical table).\n- <strong>Backlash:</strong> It licks back up the command \u2014 the caster takes 1d4 Fire damage.\n- <strong>Catastrophe:</strong> The flame gets <em>loose</em>, and it is interested in everything.</p>\n<p><strong>CALLING THE CHOLER OF AIR</strong> <em>(Grade II)</em>\n- <strong>Requirements:</strong> Lore: Occult Journeyman; open air or an enclosed space you&rsquo;re willing to ruin.\n- <strong>Cost:</strong> 4 Resolve. Ash 1.\n- <strong>Effect:</strong> A hammer of wind \u2014 targets in a Near-wide line make Athletics Reckonings or are thrown prone one band away; loose objects, fires, and footing suffer accordingly. <em>Or:</em> the air in an enclosed room goes still and thin \u2014 flames gutter out, and every breathing thing inside starts drowning on dry land (Chapter 3 drowning rules) until the door opens.\n- <strong>Backlash:</strong> The wind takes something of yours with it \u2014 the caster&rsquo;s next Reckoning this scene is at \u221210, breath literally stolen.\n- <strong>Catastrophe:</strong> The air remembers older instructions. Weather arrives that no one asked for.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>The Grey Craft (Necromancy)</h3>\n<p><em>The dead are a library and a labor force, and both uses are theft.</em> Every Grey Craft Rite marks the soul: <strong>casting one reduces maximum Resolve by 1 for a week; Grade III and above, permanently</strong> (recoverable only through genuine atonement the GM adjudicates \u2014 not a fee, a story). And the dead remember being used.</p>\n<p><strong>THE CORPSE-QUESTIONING</strong> <em>(Grade II)</em>\n- <strong>Requirements:</strong> Lore: Occult Journeyman; a corpse dead less than a week; grave-salt; the deceased&rsquo;s true name.\n- <strong>Cost:</strong> 4 Resolve; max Resolve \u22121 for a week. Ash 1.\n- <strong>Effect:</strong> The corpse answers three questions truthfully \u2014 <em>as it understood truth in life</em>, with all its ignorance and grudges intact.\n- <strong>Backlash:</strong> It asks <em>you</em> one question. Answer truly, or take +1 Ash as the lie sinks into you.\n- <strong>Catastrophe:</strong> Something else answers through it, and it does not stop when the salt burns out.</p>\n<p><strong>THE STANDING DEAD</strong> <em>(Grade III)</em>\n- <strong>Requirements:</strong> Lore: Occult Adept; prepared ground; a corpse; iron nails; a drop of the caster&rsquo;s blood.\n- <strong>Cost:</strong> 6 Resolve; the sacrifice is the caster&rsquo;s reflection in still water \u2014 gone for as long as the dead thing stands; max Resolve \u22121, permanent. Ash 2.\n- <strong>Effect:</strong> The corpse rises and serves \u2014 tireless, mute, rotting on schedule. Stats per Chapter 7&rsquo;s dead; it obeys the letter of orders.\n- <strong>Backlash:</strong> It keeps one habit from life, at the worst hour.\n- <strong>Catastrophe:</strong> It remembers who put it in the ground \u2014 or decides that you&rsquo;ll do.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>The Pact-Way (Spirit Magic)</h3>\n<p><em>Nothing in the unseen world is free; everything in it is for trade.</em> The Pact-Way bargains with spirits of hearth, land, beast, and ancestor. Its price is never paid up front: <strong>every Rite creates a Debt, recorded by the GM.</strong> Spirits keep flawless ledgers, compound what&rsquo;s owed, and always collect at the least convenient moment. An unpaid Debt is a Complication with patience.</p>\n<p><strong>THE HEARTH-WARD</strong> <em>(Grade I)</em>\n- <strong>Requirements:</strong> Lore: Occult Novice; a dwelling or camp with a fire; an offering (food, drink, something honestly valued).\n- <strong>Cost:</strong> 2 Resolve; the offering. Ash 0 (1 on Costly/Catastrophe). <strong>Debt:</strong> minor.\n- <strong>Effect:</strong> Until the fire dies, the resident spirit turns aside ill luck and ill will: the first hostile intrusion each night is warned against \u2014 the camp wakes, no Reckoning needed.\n- <strong>Backlash:</strong> The spirit takes something extra from the packs and considers the matter settled. It is wrong.\n- <strong>Catastrophe:</strong> You have offended the house. Nothing in it works, sours, or stays where it was put until amends are made.</p>\n<p><strong>BORROWED EYES</strong> <em>(Grade II)</em>\n- <strong>Requirements:</strong> Lore: Occult Journeyman; a beast within Far that has seen you; something it wants.\n- <strong>Cost:</strong> 4 Resolve. Ash 1. <strong>Debt:</strong> owed to the beast&rsquo;s kind, not the beast.\n- <strong>Effect:</strong> For up to an hour, ride the animal&rsquo;s senses. Your body sits empty and undefended.\n- <strong>Backlash:</strong> You stay a beat too long \u2014 for the rest of the day, its instincts leak: Composure Reckoning when startled or the animal in you answers first.\n- <strong>Catastrophe:</strong> Something rides <em>back</em>. Chapter 2&rsquo;s Trauma table has room for what it leaves.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>The Hollow Song (Forbidden Cosmic Magic)</h3>\n<p><em>Beyond the Seam, past the dead age, something is still awake \u2014 and it answers letters.</em> The Hollow Song is the tradition every other tradition fears. Merely <em>studying</em> it corrupts (Section 7). Its workings damage the world itself: space, memory, and the reasonable expectation that the floor will be there tomorrow.</p>\n<p><strong>THE THIN STEP</strong> <em>(Grade III)</em> \u2014 <em>teleportation, and why no one sane uses it.</em>\n- <strong>Requirements:</strong> Lore: Occult Adept; prepared ground at the departure point; having physically stood at the destination before.\n- <strong>Cost:</strong> 6 Resolve; the sacrifice is one clear memory of the destination \u2014 gone forever, chosen by the player. Ash 2.\n- <strong>Effect:</strong> The caster and what they carry step through the Seam and out at the destination, instantly.\n- <strong>Backlash:</strong> The arrival is <em>wrong by inches</em> \u2014 sprawled, deafened one minute, or 1d4 damage as the world reasserts its opinion.\n- <strong>Catastrophe:</strong> Arrive wrong by more than inches. Something of you was left in transit: a Grievous Wound with no attacker (roll the Piercing critical table).\n- <strong>Spatial damage \u2014 always, on any result:</strong> both ends become <strong>Thin Places</strong>: spots where the Seam stays slightly open. Things seep through Thin Places. The GM adds both to the map, permanently. This is the brief&rsquo;s promise that teleportation wounds space, kept as a sandbox engine: every shortcut becomes a future problem with a location attached.</p>\n<p><strong>THE QUESTION PUT TO THE DARK</strong> <em>(Grade IV)</em>\n- <strong>Requirements:</strong> Lore: Occult Master; a full night; prepared ground; a mirror broken over running water; solitude.\n- <strong>Cost:</strong> 8 Resolve; max Resolve \u22121, permanent; Ash 3; Composure Reckoning at Fear \u221240 upon the answer.\n- <strong>Effect:</strong> Ask the Outside one question. The answer is true. Not kind, not complete, not safe \u2014 true.\n- <strong>Backlash:</strong> The answer includes one thing about <em>you</em> that you did not know and did not want to.\n- <strong>Catastrophe:</strong> It answers a different question \u2014 the one you were afraid of. And now It knows your name; the campaign has gained an interested party.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>The Patient Art (Alchemy)</h3>\n<p><em>The only tradition that opens the Seam through matter instead of mind \u2014 which is why its practitioners die of old age more often than the rest.</em> Alchemy transforms what already is: essences, reactions, distillations. Its full crafting engine \u2014 ingredients, reactions, experimentation \u2014 is Chapter 5&rsquo;s centerpiece; what belongs here is its standing in the Art and its corruption interface: <strong>alchemy generates no Ash in the caster, but Seam-touched ingredients carry Ash into the product</strong>, and whoever drinks the result inherits it (the Corruption ingredient property, Chapter 5). This is why licensed alchemists exist and licensed necromancers do not.</p>\n<p><strong>THE GREY DRAUGHT</strong> <em>(Grade II preparation \u2014 Alchemy Reckoning, not Ritual Casting)</em>\n- <strong>Requirements:</strong> Alchemy Journeyman; a still; petals of hollowbell, silver salt, and rain that never touched the ground.\n- <strong>Effect:</strong> The drinker purges 1d4 temporary Ash \u2014 and makes an Endurance Reckoning or takes 1d4 Health and a Condition of the GM&rsquo;s choice as the Ash comes <em>out</em>. Nothing about the Seam leaves gently.\n- <strong>Backlash (on a Costly Success in the brewing):</strong> the purge works but the Ash goes somewhere \u2014 the still is Blighted until scoured.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 600, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "ED3H47Gnb7SNvJKw", "name": "7. Forbidden Knowledge and Magical Disease", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>7. Forbidden Knowledge and Magical Disease</h2>\n<h3>Weighted Texts</h3>\n<p>Occult writings carry <strong>Weight</strong> \u2014 Ash gained per week of study.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Text</th>\n<th>Weight</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Field notes, folk charms, trial records</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>Grants Lore: Occult marks (Chapter 1 advancement).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A true grimoire (Grades I\u2013II)</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Teaches its Rites; grants marks.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A Profane text (Grade III)</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Plus a Composure Reckoning per week of study.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A Forbidden text (Grade IV, Hollow Song)</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Plus Composure at Fear \u221220 per week; a Failure inflicts a Trauma.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Knowledge in this game is literally corrupting. A library can be a slow poison, a treasure, and a faction&rsquo;s whole reason for existing \u2014 often all three.</p>\n<h3>Magical Diseases</h3>\n<p>Built on Chapter 3&rsquo;s disease framework (Virulence penalty; onset \u2192 grip \u2192 crisis):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ashlung</strong> <em>(Virulence \u221210; caught breathing the air of Blighted places).</em> Onset: a grey, glittering cough. Grip: Winded (all Stamina costs doubled). Crisis: an Endurance Reckoning \u2014 shake it, or the lungs fill with what the Seam exhales.</li>\n<li><strong>Whisper-Fever</strong> <em>(Virulence \u221220; proximity to Hollow Song workings or Thin Places).</em> Onset: overheard fragments in an unknown voice; \u22125 Composure. Grip: sleep stops helping \u2014 Resolve recovery halved; Sleepless threatens nightly. Crisis: Composure at Fear \u221220 \u2014 Failure leaves a permanent Trauma even after cure.</li>\n<li><strong>Grave-Chill</strong> <em>(Virulence \u221210; lingering where the Grey Craft has worked).</em> Onset: a Freezing Condition no fire removes. Grip: natural Health recovery stops. Crisis: Endurance Reckoning \u2014 Failure means one Death&rsquo;s Mark that never heals, a piece of the sufferer already keeping the dead company.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 700, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "fB6gZDePMmU4uoAa", "name": "8. Witnessing the Art", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>8. Witnessing the Art</h2>\n<p>Sorcery frightens people because it should. When someone witnesses casting:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Witnessed</th>\n<th>Composure demand</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Grade I</td>\n<td>None, for most. (Traumas still trigger \u2014 <em>The Watching Dark</em>, Chapter 2, bites on <strong>any</strong> casting nearby.)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Grade II</td>\n<td>Composure Reckoning, unmodified.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Grade III</td>\n<td>Composure at Fear \u221220; counts as &ldquo;the openly unnatural&rdquo; for <strong>Aftermath</strong> (Chapter 3).</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Grade IV</td>\n<td>Composure at Fear \u221240; Aftermath is automatic for all present.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>And there are social teeth: a witnessed casting is testimony. Word travels at the speed of fear, witch-finders pay for names, and Chapter 6&rsquo;s faction rules will treat a known caster as a resource, a threat, or a bonfire waiting for a schedule \u2014 depending on who hears first.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 800, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "Ppr3vLRiPj8AxzTP", "name": "9. Worked Example: Edrik and the Dead Man", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>9. Worked Example: Edrik and the Dead Man</h2>\n<p><em>Edrik Vane \u2014 Grave-Robber turned reluctant practitioner. Willpower 12 (Resolve 24, Ash Threshold 12, Composure 60), Intellect 14 (holds up to 7 Rites; knows 3), Ritual Casting 42, Lore: Occult 45, current Ash 4. A murdered ferryman knows where the ledger is buried, and Edrik has his name, his corpse, and a pouch of grave-salt.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Preparation:</strong> The Corpse-Questioning is Grade II \u2014 ten minutes, requirements met. Edrik pays <strong>4 Resolve</strong> (24 \u2192 20).</p>\n<p><strong>The Reckoning:</strong> Ritual Casting 42. He rolls <strong>51</strong> \u2014 above 42, within 62: a <strong>Costly Success</strong>. The rite works, with Backlash.</p>\n<p><strong>Effect:</strong> the ferryman&rsquo;s jaw unhinges and answers three questions truthfully as <em>he</em> understood truth \u2014 including one grudge presented as fact. <strong>Ash +1</strong> (4 \u2192 5 of 12). <strong>Soul-mark:</strong> max Resolve \u22121 for a week (23).</p>\n<p><strong>Backlash:</strong> the dead man asks <em>&ldquo;Did you rob my grave before you woke me, boy?&rdquo;</em> Edrik answers truly \u2014 yes \u2014 and keeps the Ash a lie would have cost. The table learns something about Edrik either way.</p>\n<p><strong>The witness:</strong> Edrik&rsquo;s companion Isolde (Composure 50) watches a corpse talk. Grade II demands an unmodified Composure Reckoning: she rolls <strong>71</strong> \u2014 Failure. <strong>Shaken</strong> (\u221210) while the working persists, and she cannot willingly close to Reach with the thing on the slab. If she carried <em>The Watching Dark</em>, that Flinch would fire too. No Aftermath check is owed \u2014 Grade II sits below that line. Barely.</p>\n<p><strong>The ledger of the scene:</strong> three answers, one debt of honesty paid, 5 of 12 Ash, a friend who now stands a little farther away, and a corpse that must be re-buried before dawn or explained to a magistrate. That is one Grade II casting, going <em>well</em>.</p>"}, "sort": 900, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}], "folder": null, "sort": 400, "ownership": {"default": 0}, "flags": {}}
{"_id": "jx2tx22Ixzjwyy94", "name": "Chapter 5 \u2014 Crafting, Cooking and Professions", "pages": [{"_id": "F6rVaGEpxAvNgqtU", "name": "Overview", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h1>CHAPTER 5: CRAFTING, COOKING AND PROFESSIONS</h1>\n<p><em>Everything made in this world keeps a ledger \u2014 same as everything living.</em></p>\n<p>Nothing in the Dying Age comes off a shelf. Every blade, boot, loaf, and draught was made by someone, from something, somewhere \u2014 and the system tracks all three.</p>\n<p><strong>Contents:</strong> 1. The Maker&rsquo;s Reckoning \u00b7 2. Blacksmithing \u00b7 3. Carpentry \u00b7 4. Leatherworking \u00b7 5. The Patient Art (Alchemy) \u00b7 6. The Bound Work (Enchanting) \u00b7 7. Cooking \u00b7 8. Herbalism and Brewing \u00b7 9. Craft Techniques \u00b7 10. Coin, Carry, and the Working Life \u00b7 11. Worked Examples</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 0, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "Ke3r2YRtDKiFb23Y", "name": "1. The Maker's Reckoning", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>1. The Maker&rsquo;s Reckoning</h2>\n<p>All crafting uses one loop: <strong>materials set the ceiling, skill decides whether you reach it, time is the price of ambition.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Extended tasks.</strong> Every project needs <strong>Progress</strong>: Simple 1, Standard 3, Complex 5, a masterwork ambition 8. Each work session (hours to a day, by trade) is one craft Reckoning:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Result</th>\n<th>Progress</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Masterstroke</td>\n<td>2 Progress, and the item gains a <strong>Virtue</strong> (a special quality \u2014 maker&rsquo;s choice with GM assent)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Success</td>\n<td>1 Progress</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Costly Success</td>\n<td>1 Progress, but the work takes a <strong>Flaw</strong> or spoils some material</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Failure</td>\n<td>0; the material strains (one more Failure on this project degrades its grade)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Catastrophe</td>\n<td>Materials ruined \u2014 and the workshop bites: forge burns use Chapter 3&rsquo;s Fire critical table, a still bursts, a lathe takes a finger</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Material grades:</strong> Crude \u2192 Common \u2192 Fine \u2192 Superior \u2192 Peerless. <strong>A finished item&rsquo;s quality can never exceed its worst essential material.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Every item is unique.</strong> A finished piece records: its <strong>name</strong> (material + provenance + form + maker \u2014 <em>&ldquo;high-carbon river-iron longsword, forged by Master Alric&rdquo;</em>), rated <strong>properties</strong> (by type, below), any <strong>Virtues and Flaws</strong>, and a <strong>History</strong> line. In play, items accrue <strong>Deeds</strong> \u2014 the sword that opened the Vael Wyrm&rsquo;s throat is thereafter <em>that</em> sword, worth more, recognized, and coveted. Objects keep ledgers too; factions read them (Chapter 6).</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 100, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "omBZgOZuv4PDJsxt", "name": "2. Blacksmithing", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>2. Blacksmithing</h2>\n<p><strong>Metallurgy.</strong> The metal is half the smith.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Metal</th>\n<th>Grade ceiling</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Bog iron</td>\n<td>Common</td>\n<td>Cheap, sulfurous; blades take the <em>Brittle</em> Flaw on any quench Failure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>River iron</td>\n<td>Fine</td>\n<td>Clean ore; some beds carry <strong>high-carbon pockets</strong> \u2014 Fine grade with a Keenness edge</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mountain steel</td>\n<td>Superior</td>\n<td>Deep-mined, guild-controlled (Chapter 6)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Crucible steel</td>\n<td>Peerless</td>\n<td>A guarded technique, not just an ore; knowing it is treasure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Star-iron</td>\n<td>Peerless</td>\n<td>Seam-touched skyfall metal. Enchantable (Section 6). Working it unwarded: 1 Ash per project (Chapter 4)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>The forging sequence</strong> (each step one Reckoning of the extended task): <em>Shape</em> \u2192 <strong>Harden</strong> \u2192 <em>Temper</em> \u2192 <em>Finish</em>. Hardening is the famous risk: the quench Reckoning at \u221210; Failure there means the <em>Brittle</em> Flaw (notch capacity \u22121) unless the smith re-forges from the start. Alloying (a Master option) trades properties \u2014 more Keenness for less Durability, and so on \u2014 declared before the Shape step.</p>\n<p><strong>Weapon properties:</strong> <strong>Keenness</strong> (steps the damage die: 1d8 \u2192 1d10 at Superior Keenness), <strong>Durability</strong> (notch capacity before the blade degrades \u2014 Chapter 3&rsquo;s Sunder now has its bookkeeping: Crude holds 1 notch, Common 2, Fine 3, Superior 4, Peerless 5), <strong>Balance</strong> (Fine: +5 to Parries <em>or</em> +1 Initiative with it; Superior: both; Peerless: both, and +5 to the wielder&rsquo;s attack Reckonings).</p>\n<p><strong>Repair.</strong> A notch costs a forge, an hour, and a Standard Blacksmithing Reckoning; armor AV lost to Sunder is restored the same way. Field whetstones can hold a blade&rsquo;s edge but never remove a notch \u2014 steel remembers until a smith makes it forget.</p>\n<p><strong>Fuel.</strong> The forge eats forests: working iron at <strong>Fine grade or better requires charcoal</strong> \u2014 roughly a cartload of standing wood per project, burned down slow by a collier. Where smithies cluster, the treeline retreats, and when the Woodland runs Scarce (the Wood-Take, Chapter 8), Fine work stops until someone imports charcoal or cuts where they shouldn&rsquo;t.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 200, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "NnGjJfDWFjJiQ4Fi", "name": "3. Carpentry", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>3. Carpentry</h2>\n<p><strong>Wood is chosen, not found.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Wood</th>\n<th>Grade ceiling</th>\n<th>Best use</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Green pine</td>\n<td>Crude</td>\n<td>Firewood pretending otherwise</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Seasoned oak</td>\n<td>Fine</td>\n<td>Construction, shields, hafts</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mountain ash</td>\n<td>Fine</td>\n<td>Weapon shafts \u2014 a Fine ash haft grants a polearm Balance (+5 Parry)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Yew</td>\n<td>Superior</td>\n<td>Bows; a Superior yew stave steps bow damage 1d8 \u2192 1d10</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ironoak</td>\n<td>Superior</td>\n<td>Siege-grade construction; shield cores (+1 notch capacity)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wychwood</td>\n<td>Peerless</td>\n<td>Seam-touched; enchantable; the tree it came from remembers being cut</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Shafts, staves, and shields follow the Maker&rsquo;s Reckoning directly. <strong>Construction</strong> \u2014 bridges, palisades, mills \u2014 runs as long extended tasks measured in weeks, and feeds straight into settlement Provision and prosperity (Chapter 6): a party that rebuilds a mill has moved a number the whole region feels.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 300, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "4AjopUxIE3xVq2x4", "name": "4. Leatherworking", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>4. Leatherworking</h2>\n<p>Hides come from the hunt (harvesting rules, Chapter 7) \u2014 this craft chain starts with a carcass and a knife.</p>\n<p><strong>Tanning:</strong> <em>salt-cure</em> (days; Crude\u2013Common), <em>brain-tan</em> (a week; supple \u2014 finished Burden \u22121, minimum 0), <em>bark-tan</em> (a month; Fine\u2013Superior, durable). Monster hides carry their ceilings and quirks in their Chapter 7 harvest tables \u2014 Bristleback plate tans to Superior and keeps its ridges.</p>\n<p><strong>Armor properties:</strong> <strong>Protection</strong> (AV, as Chapter 3), <strong>Suppleness</strong> (Burden, per tanning and fit), and <strong>Insulation</strong> \u2014 the survival property: Insulated gear (fleece-lined, fur-trimmed, greased) <strong>prevents the Freezing Condition in ordinary cold</strong> and grants +20 to Endurance Reckonings against extreme cold (Chapter 3). In the north, a leatherworker outranks a swordsmith.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 400, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "uzZs9aPaPx757vMT", "name": "5. The Patient Art \u2014 Alchemy in Full", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>5. The Patient Art \u2014 Alchemy in Full</h2>\n<p>Chapter 4 gave alchemy its standing; here is its engine. Alchemy and Cooking read the <strong>same ingredient database</strong> (Section 7) \u2014 the alchemist concentrates what the cook balances.</p>\n<p><strong>Extraction</strong> (an Alchemy Reckoning each): <em>infusion</em> (gentle \u2014 pulls the highest property only), <em>decoction</em> (pulls all properties at half strength), <em>distillation</em> (concentrates: <strong>doubles every property \u2014 including Corruption</strong>), <em>calcination</em> (burns to salts \u2014 preserves one property indefinitely as a stable reagent).</p>\n<p><strong>Reactions.</strong> Combined essences interact by pair:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Pairing</th>\n<th>Product family</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Vitality + Regeneration</td>\n<td>Healing draughts (restore Health; speed Wound healing)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Heat + Energy</td>\n<td>Fire-heart oils (weapon coating: +2 Fire damage for 3 strikes; volatile)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cold + Toxicity</td>\n<td>Numbing venoms (the struck victim suffers Numb Flesh \u2014 with its hidden-Bleeding risk)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Spirit + Memory</td>\n<td>Clarities (+10 to a Lore Reckoning; true dreams; Whisper-Fever if abused)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Spirit + Cold</td>\n<td>The Grey Draught family \u2014 Ash purges (Chapter 4)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Toxicity + Toxicity</td>\n<td>Poisons proper (Virulence-rated, on Chapter 3&rsquo;s disease framework)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Anything + Corruption</td>\n<td>Don&rsquo;t. The product carries the Ash to whoever takes it (Section 7).</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Every preparation requires its listed ingredients.</strong> The Hedge-Alchemist&rsquo;s Trade Boon (Chapter 2) now means what it says: once per day they may waive one component, substituting from their hoarded residues and second decoctions \u2014 the privilege of a working still, not a loophole.</p>\n<p><strong>Experimentation</strong> \u2014 the heart of the Art: an untested combination is a <strong>Wild Preparation</strong>, using Chapter 1&rsquo;s Experimentation rule exactly (marks Alchemy twice; both 99 and 100 are Catastrophes), with the GM resolving the product honestly from the properties in the flask. A proven discovery becomes a <strong>Formula</strong>: +10 to repeat, recordable, teachable, stealable. An alchemist&rsquo;s formulary is loot, and factions know it.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 500, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "Dc6C7C85QiZkzc0l", "name": "6. The Bound Work \u2014 Enchanting", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>6. The Bound Work \u2014 Enchanting</h2>\n<p>There is no Enchanting skill: enchantment is a <em>collaboration</em> between a Peerless craft and the Art \u2014 which is why it is vanishingly rare.</p>\n<p>An enchanted item requires: a <strong>Peerless item</strong> (the vessel), a <strong>Seam-touched essential material</strong> (star-iron, wychwood, wyrm-bone, saint&rsquo;s tallow \u2014 Chapter 7&rsquo;s harvest tables mark these), and a <strong>Binding</strong> \u2014 a Grade III Rite (Chapter 4: prepared ground, 6 Resolve, a stated sacrifice, Ash 2) cast by a Ritual Casting Adept while the maker completes the final Progress.</p>\n<p>Every enchantment carries a <strong>Hunger</strong> \u2014 an upkeep the item demands (blood weekly, a truth spoken over it, moonlight, a name whispered before battle). A Hunger unfed leaves the item merely Peerless until fed \u2014 or worse, resentful. Two examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Emberwake</strong> <em>(star-iron arming sword)</em>: wounds it deals cannot be closed by the Red Tithe \u2014 only honest medicine. Hunger: quenched in fresh snow each new moon.</li>\n<li><strong>The Listening Haft</strong> <em>(wychwood spear)</em>: its wielder gains the <em>Set</em> trigger against any foe, seen or unseen. Hunger: once a season it must stand overnight in living soil. It dreams.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>An enchanted item is a <em>regional fact</em>: factions bid, cults circle, the Pale Flame opens a file (Chapter 6). Making one is a plot event, not a purchase.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 600, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "wvHiq5ii5I4lPgxJ", "name": "7. Cooking", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>7. Cooking</h2>\n<p><em>The most consequential craft in the game, because Chapter 3 made it so: full Stamina recovery requires a night&rsquo;s sleep and a real meal. The cook is the party&rsquo;s engine-keeper.</em></p>\n<p><strong>A real meal</strong> = actual ingredients (fresh or preserved \u2014 not bare trail biscuit), fire or hearth, and a cook of at least Novice tier. Trail rations alone restore only half Stamina overnight, and a week of nothing else inflicts the Hungry Condition regardless of quantity \u2014 the body knows the difference.</p>\n<h3>The Nine Properties</h3>\n<p>Every ingredient carries rated properties (1\u20133). Cooking balances them into effects; alchemy concentrates them. One shared scale across both crafts.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Property</th>\n<th>In a meal \u2014 effect at total 3+ in a diner&rsquo;s portion</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Heat</strong></td>\n<td>Immune to Freezing for a day; +5 vs cold hazards</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Cold</strong></td>\n<td>Immune to Sweltering for a day; +5 vs heat hazards</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Toxicity</strong></td>\n<td>At 2+: poison \u2014 Endurance Reckoning or 1d4 Health and a Condition (low doses are medicine; that&rsquo;s alchemy&rsquo;s business)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Vitality</strong></td>\n<td>Natural healing +50% today</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Energy</strong></td>\n<td>+2 maximum Stamina today, or clear one Sleepless Condition</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Spirit</strong></td>\n<td>Recover 1 Resolve now; +5 Composure today</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Regeneration</strong></td>\n<td>Wound-healing time counts double today</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Memory</strong></td>\n<td>+10 to Lore Reckonings today; sleep may bring a true dream (the GM&rsquo;s gift)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Corruption</strong></td>\n<td><strong>Every point = 1 temporary Ash per diner. No threshold. No exceptions.</strong> (Chapter 4)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>The Ingredient Database (foundation set \u2014 Chapter 7&rsquo;s harvest tables extend it)</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Ingredient</th>\n<th>Source</th>\n<th>Properties</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Marrowfat</td>\n<td>Animal</td>\n<td>Vitality 2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Aurochs heart</td>\n<td>Animal</td>\n<td>Vitality 2, Energy 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>River eel</td>\n<td>Animal</td>\n<td>Energy 1, Vitality 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bristleback loin</td>\n<td>Monster</td>\n<td>Vitality 2, Heat 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wyrm liver</td>\n<td>Monster</td>\n<td>Vitality 3, Memory 1, Corruption 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tallow-hound fat</td>\n<td>Monster</td>\n<td>Heat 1, Corruption 2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Seam-moth dust</td>\n<td>Monster</td>\n<td>Spirit 2, Corruption 2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fireroot</td>\n<td>Plant</td>\n<td>Heat 2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Winterbark</td>\n<td>Plant</td>\n<td>Cold 2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hollowbell petals</td>\n<td>Plant</td>\n<td>Spirit 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Graincorn</td>\n<td>Plant</td>\n<td>Vitality 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Monk&rsquo;s-bread</td>\n<td>Fungus</td>\n<td>Vitality 1, Memory 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Embercap</td>\n<td>Fungus</td>\n<td>Heat 1, Energy 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Widow&rsquo;s veil</td>\n<td>Fungus</td>\n<td>Toxicity 3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rock salt</td>\n<td>Mineral</td>\n<td>(preservative)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Grave-salt</td>\n<td>Mineral</td>\n<td>Spirit 1 (other uses \u2014 Chapter 4)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ashen honey</td>\n<td>Seam-touched</td>\n<td>Regeneration 2, Corruption 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pale fruit</td>\n<td>Seam-touched</td>\n<td>Memory 2, Corruption 2</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>The Nine Methods</h3>\n<p>Method transforms properties:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Method</th>\n<th>Transformation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Roasting</td>\n<td>+1 Heat to the dish; renders fat (Vitality keeps)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Boiling</td>\n<td>\u22121 Toxicity; mellows (no single property above 2)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stewing</td>\n<td>Melds \u2014 sum all ingredient properties gently; the party dish</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Baking</td>\n<td>Binds \u2014 keeps a week; travel food with its effects intact</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Smoking</td>\n<td>Preserves a season; +1 Heat; halve Vitality</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Drying</td>\n<td>Preserves indefinitely; halve Vitality; light to carry</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pickling</td>\n<td>Preserves a season; +1 Cold</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fermentation</td>\n<td>Time-alchemy: converts 1 Toxicity \u2192 1 Spirit per week fermented (widow&rsquo;s veil becomes something monks fight over)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Distilling</td>\n<td>Alchemy&rsquo;s door: <strong>doubles all properties, including Corruption</strong> \u2014 a drink or a disaster</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Preservation runs on wood and salt.</strong> Smoking eats fuel; salting, pickling, and the brine half of fermentation eat <strong>salt stock</strong> \u2014 no salt, no larder, no winter. A settlement without salt access cannot bank stores past autumn, which makes salt the most political mineral in the Reach (the Salt-Take, Chapter 8) and the Saltmark Combine&rsquo;s name no accident.</p>\n<h3>Cooking a Meal</h3>\n<p>Choose up to three ingredients and a method; make a <strong>Cooking Reckoning</strong> (proper kitchen +0, campfire \u221210, rain and one dented pot \u221220).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Masterstroke:</strong> effects land, plus +1 to one property <em>or</em> the dish becomes a <strong>Signature</strong> \u2014 a personal Formula (+10 to repeat), and your name travels with it.</li>\n<li><strong>Success:</strong> tally properties per portion; apply effects.</li>\n<li><strong>Costly Success:</strong> effects land with a complication \u2014 scorched (no table-fellowship, no Regard movement tonight), one portion got all the Toxicity, or the smell carries (worsen the Chapter 3 camp roll one degree).</li>\n<li><strong>Failure:</strong> nourishment only. It counts as a real meal, barely, and everyone knows it.</li>\n<li><strong>Catastrophe:</strong> food poisoning (Toxicity 2 to all diners), the fire gets loose \u2014 or, if any Corruption was in the pot, something in the stew <em>looks back</em>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Portions.</strong> A cook&rsquo;s full effects reach the cook plus diners by tier: Novice 1, Journeyman 2, Adept 4, Master 8, Legendary \u2014 everyone at the fire. <strong>The Camp Cook&rsquo;s Trade Boon (Chapter 2) overrides this outright: their meal&rsquo;s bonus always reaches the whole party.</strong> Visibly one of the strongest boons in the game \u2014 as intended.</p>\n<h3>Recipes, Regions, and Legends</h3>\n<p><strong>Recipe discovery:</strong> an untested ingredient-and-method idea is Experimentation (Chapter 1 \u2014 double marks, doubled Catastrophe range). A proven <strong>Recipe</strong> grants +10 and predictable results; recipes are written, traded, dowried, and stolen. A regional cookbook is treasure with a market price.</p>\n<p><strong>Regional cuisine</strong> (one signature per Chapter 2 culture): Highland <strong>blood-and-oat sausage</strong> (Vitality 2, Heat 1 \u2014 winter marching food); River-Cities <strong>eel in three vinegars</strong> (Energy 2 \u2014 a negotiator&rsquo;s lunch); Steppe <strong>wind-dried aurochs heart</strong> (Vitality 2, Energy 1 \u2014 a campaign in a saddlebag); Coastal <strong>grey-salted cod stew</strong> (Vitality 2, Spirit 1 \u2014 funeral food, and they&rsquo;d tell you why); Heartlands <strong>harvest bread</strong> (Vitality 1, baked \u2014 tithe, symbol, and currency at once); Deep-Forest <strong>fermented monk&rsquo;s-bread beer</strong> (Spirit 2, Memory 1 \u2014 drunk before oaths, never after).</p>\n<p><strong>Legendary dishes</strong> \u2014 cooking&rsquo;s masterworks, each demanding a story to even attempt:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The Wyrm-Heart Roast</strong> <em>(Master cook; a wyrm&rsquo;s heart, roasted with fireroot within a day of the kill)</em>: Vitality 3, Regeneration 2, Memory 2, Corruption 1 \u2014 every diner heals a full Wound level, dreams the wyrm&rsquo;s last century, and takes 1 Ash. A dish that ends feuds and starts sagas.</li>\n<li><strong>The Widow&rsquo;s Mercy</strong> <em>(Adept cook, Journeyman brewer; widow&rsquo;s veil fermented three weeks with hollowbell)</em>: the Toxicity fully converted \u2014 Spirit 3, Memory 1. One true dream of a dead loved one; once per mourner, ever. Villages guard the recipe like a relic, because it is one.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>The Food Economy</h3>\n<p>Preservation is why trade exists: smoked, salted, and dried goods travel, and a region&rsquo;s <strong>Provision</strong> rating (Chapter 6) is the sum of its harvests, hunts, and larders. This is the load-bearing wall of the living world \u2014 when Chapter 8&rsquo;s Harvest Roll fails, Provision drops, prices triple, the granary factions smile, cult recruiters preach to full rooms, and the famine cascade runs on rails this chapter laid.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 700, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "v41dRfuxAHCBTm1M", "name": "8. Herbalism and Brewing", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>8. Herbalism and Brewing</h2>\n<p><strong>Herbalism</strong> is the gathering trade: field-identification Reckonings by region and season (tables in Chapter 8), feeding both pot and still. Its signature product pays Chapter 3&rsquo;s debt in full: a <strong>poultice</strong> (Herbalism, one hour, common simples) grants the promised <strong>+20 to infection Reckonings</strong> and a fresh check when applied. In plague years, herbalists get rich or get blamed \u2014 usually both.</p>\n<p><strong>Brewing &amp; Distilling</strong> is the social craft: shared drink is the cheapest tool ever made for moving <strong>Regard</strong> (Chapter 6), and every culture&rsquo;s diplomacy runs through it. Distilling proper crosses into alchemy \u2014 same still, same rules, same warning about Corruption.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 800, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "42NrzDNnYyfestYt", "name": "9. Craft Techniques", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>9. Craft Techniques</h2>\n<p>Chapter 1&rsquo;s promise, kept for the trades (Adept at Rating 51+, Master at 76+; Legendary remains bespoke):</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Skill</th>\n<th>Adept \u2014 choose one at 51+</th>\n<th>Master \u2014 choose one at 76+</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Blacksmithing</td>\n<td><strong>True Quench</strong> \u2014 your Harden step takes no penalty. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Cold Read</strong> \u2014 inspect any blade to learn its grade, Virtues, and Flaws before buying or fighting it.</td>\n<td><strong>Read the Grain</strong> \u2014 re-roll one Failure per project. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Signature Steel</strong> \u2014 items you forge carry your maker&rsquo;s mark; their Deeds build your Renown faster.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Carpentry</td>\n<td><strong>Sound Joint</strong> \u2014 your constructions hold one durability grade above their wood. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Rapid Frame</strong> \u2014 halve the time to build fortifications, rafts, and siege works.</td>\n<td><strong>The Living Fit</strong> \u2014 shafts you cut grant Balance one grade above their material. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>The Standing Work</strong> \u2014 a structure you raise survives one disaster (fire, flood, siege) it should not have.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Leatherworking</td>\n<td><strong>Second Skin</strong> \u2014 your armor&rsquo;s Burden \u22121 (minimum 0). <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Snug Fit</strong> \u2014 armor you fit grants its wearer +5 to Acrobatics and Stealth.</td>\n<td><strong>Weathercraft</strong> \u2014 your Insulated gear also grants +10 vs Sweltering. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Boiled Hardening</strong> \u2014 your leather armor counts AV +1 against the first Grievous hit each fight.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alchemy</td>\n<td><strong>Clean Separation</strong> \u2014 your distillations may exclude one property. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Stable Draught</strong> \u2014 your preparations keep twice as long before they spoil or turn.</td>\n<td><strong>The Third Reading</strong> \u2014 on any Wild Preparation, learn true properties before finishing. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Twinned Brew</strong> \u2014 a Masterstroke yields two doses instead of one.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cooking</td>\n<td><strong>Field Kitchen</strong> \u2014 no circumstance penalty worse than \u221210 applies to your cooking. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Stretch the Pot</strong> \u2014 feed twice the number from the same ingredients, at one grade lower effect.</td>\n<td><strong>The Remembered Table</strong> \u2014 diners at your Masterstroke meals recover +1 Resolve. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Feast-Giver</strong> \u2014 a shared Masterstroke feast grants the whole table its bonus for a full day, not a scene.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Herbalism</td>\n<td><strong>Eye for Green</strong> \u2014 forage yields double in terrain you know. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Bitter Knowledge</strong> \u2014 identify any plant, poison, or fungus on sight, and its antidote.</td>\n<td><strong>The Old Simples</strong> \u2014 your poultices grant +30 vs infection, not +20. <strong>\u00b7 or \u00b7</strong> <strong>Green Mercy</strong> \u2014 your remedies ease a Grievous Wound&rsquo;s recovery by one week.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}, "sort": 900, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "ttADMiTYJkbYOMyh", "name": "10. Coin, Carry, and the Working Life", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>10. Coin, Carry, and the Working Life</h2>\n<p><em>For the full priced catalogue \u2014 200+ weapons, armor, travel gear, tools, trade goods, cooking ingredients, monster parts, and services \u2014 see </em><em>Appendix A: The Armory</em><em>. What follows are the rules those prices hang on.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Coin.</strong> Three metals in a simple chain: <strong>100 copper = 1 silver</strong>, and <strong>100 silver = 1 gold</strong> \u2014 so a single gold piece is 10,000 copper, a fortune to most who will ever hold one. Anchors: a laborer&rsquo;s day, 20 copper; a night&rsquo;s inn and board, 40 copper; a Common arming sword, 5 silver; a Fine one from a named smith, 20 silver and a waiting list. Peerless work is negotiated in favors, land, or marriages, not coin.</p>\n<p><strong>Carry.</strong> Chapter 1&rsquo;s Encumbrance Threshold (Might \u00d7 3) now bites. Everything worn or carried has a <strong>Load</strong> value; total it and compare to the Threshold.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>Load</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Dagger, sling, tool, pouch of simples</td>\n<td>0 (trivial)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>One-handed weapon, bow, shield</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Two-handed weapon, polearm, crossbow</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Worn armor</td>\n<td>its Burden \u00d7 2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Day&rsquo;s trail food and water</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bedroll, cloak, pack-frame</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rope, tools, a full climbing or surgeon&rsquo;s kit</td>\n<td>1 each</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>100 coins</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>A body, or a person carried</td>\n<td>6</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Over Threshold: <strong>Burdened</strong> \u2014 +2 effective Burden (subtract from maximum Stamina) and \u221210 to Athletics, Acrobatics, and Stealth. Over double Threshold: you are cargo that walks \u2014 no running, no fighting well, and a \u221220 to anything athletic. <em>(The Character Ledger totals slot and pack Load automatically.)</em></p>\n<p><strong>The working life.</strong> Downtime at a trade earns keep plus tier wages: Novice, keep only; Journeyman, 60 coppers a week; Adept, 2 silvers; Master, by commission \u2014 and commissions are adventures with invoices. A maker&rsquo;s reputation compounds through their items&rsquo; Deeds, and guild membership (Chapter 6) sets prices, protects secrets like crucible steel, and occasionally decides what happens to smiths who won&rsquo;t join.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 1000, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "g3tayHNoEqXge3OE", "name": "11. Worked Examples", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>11. Worked Examples</h2>\n<p><strong>The brief&rsquo;s own sword, forged.</strong> <em>Master Alric (Blacksmithing 82) takes a commission: a longsword from river iron he pulled himself, from a bed with a high-carbon pocket (Fine grade, Keenness edge). A masterwork ambition: 8 Progress.</em> The sessions run: Success, Success, <strong>Masterstroke</strong> (2 Progress; a Virtue banked), then the Harden step at \u221210 (effective Rating 72) \u2014 the Masterstroke band is computed from the clamped effective Rating, 72 \u00f7 5 \u2192 15, and he rolls 9 \u2192 <strong>Masterstroke</strong>; the quench sings. Two more Successes close it at 8 Progress. Alric spends the Virtue on Balance. The ledger reads:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>High-carbon river-iron longsword, forged by Master Alric.</strong> Damage <strong>High</strong> (1d10 \u2014 the carbon pocket&rsquo;s Keenness). Durability <strong>Excellent</strong> (quench-perfect Fine steel; 3 notches). Balance <strong>Exceptional</strong> (Virtue: +5 Parry <em>and</em> +1 Initiative). <strong>History:</strong> commissioned by a knight of Vael who never returned to claim it \u2014 a Deed line waiting for whoever earns the name it&rsquo;s owed.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>The stew after the fight.</strong> <em>Petra, Camp Cook (Cooking 51 \u2014 Adept; Trade Boon), feeds Yorna&rsquo;s party the night after the bridge: bristleback loin, fireroot, monk&rsquo;s-bread \u2014 stewed.</em> Campfire \u221210: effective 41. She rolls 26 \u2014 Success. Stewing melds the pot: Vitality 3, Heat 3, Memory 1. Every diner \u2014 the <em>whole</em> party, boon \u2014 heals half again as fast tomorrow and shrugs off the cold snap on the ford road. And Memory 1 does what Memory 1 does: that night somebody dreams of Aldwatch, and it&rsquo;s accurate.</p>"}, "sort": 1100, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}], "folder": null, "sort": 500, "ownership": {"default": 0}, "flags": {}}
{"_id": "V1yvViGmUHCSi8PW", "name": "Appendix A \u2014 The Armory", "pages": [{"_id": "D7gfRAa1IWfO8Klq", "name": "Overview", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h1>Appendix A \u2014 The Armory</h1>\n<p>Prices are <strong>Common</strong> grade in a peaceful market town. Coin: 100 copper = 1 silver, 100 silver = 1 gold. Load per Chapter 5. Fine and Superior goods cost 3\u20136\u00d7; Peerless is not for sale. War, famine, and distance move all of it.</p>"}, "sort": 0, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "8UbMnOHHknsiE83M", "name": "A.1 \u2014 Weapons", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>A.1 \u2014 Weapons</h2>\n<p>Damage, Class, and Traits as Chapter 3. Price is Common grade.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Weapon</th>\n<th>Damage</th>\n<th>Class</th>\n<th>Traits</th>\n<th>Load</th>\n<th>Price</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Knife / eating dagger</td>\n<td>1d3</td>\n<td>Blade</td>\n<td>Close, Concealable</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>8c</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dagger</td>\n<td>1d4</td>\n<td>Blade</td>\n<td>Close, Concealable</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>25c</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rondel (armor-spike)</td>\n<td>1d4</td>\n<td>Piercing</td>\n<td>Close, Armor-Piercing</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>60c</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cudgel / club</td>\n<td>1d6</td>\n<td>Crushing</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>10c</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hatchet</td>\n<td>1d6</td>\n<td>Blade</td>\n<td>Sunder, Throwable</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>40c</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hand axe</td>\n<td>1d6</td>\n<td>Blade</td>\n<td>Sunder, Throwable</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>70c</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shortsword / falchion</td>\n<td>1d6</td>\n<td>Blade</td>\n<td>Close</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>3s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Arming sword</td>\n<td>1d8</td>\n<td>Blade</td>\n<td>Balanced (+5 Parries)</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>5s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Backsword / saber</td>\n<td>1d8</td>\n<td>Blade</td>\n<td>Balanced</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>5s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mace</td>\n<td>1d8</td>\n<td>Crushing</td>\n<td>Brutal (target AV \u22121)</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>4s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Warhammer</td>\n<td>1d8</td>\n<td>Crushing</td>\n<td>Brutal</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>4s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Battle axe</td>\n<td>1d8</td>\n<td>Blade</td>\n<td>Sunder</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>4s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>War-pick</td>\n<td>1d6</td>\n<td>Piercing</td>\n<td>Armor-Piercing, Brutal</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>5s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Spear</td>\n<td>1d8</td>\n<td>Piercing</td>\n<td>Reach, Set</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>2s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Boar-spear</td>\n<td>1d8</td>\n<td>Piercing</td>\n<td>Reach, Set, Brutal</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>3s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Longsword (2h)</td>\n<td>1d10</td>\n<td>Blade</td>\n<td>Balanced</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>9s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bastard sword</td>\n<td>1d8/1d10</td>\n<td>Blade</td>\n<td>Balanced, Versatile (1h/2h)</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>12s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Greatsword (2h)</td>\n<td>2d6</td>\n<td>Blade</td>\n<td>Heavy (Might 12+)</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>18s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Poleaxe (2h)</td>\n<td>1d10</td>\n<td>Piercing</td>\n<td>Reach, Armor-Piercing</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>10s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Halberd (2h)</td>\n<td>1d10</td>\n<td>Blade</td>\n<td>Reach, Sunder</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>9s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Glaive (2h)</td>\n<td>1d10</td>\n<td>Blade</td>\n<td>Reach</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>8s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Warspear / pike</td>\n<td>1d8</td>\n<td>Piercing</td>\n<td>Reach 2, Set, Heavy</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>4s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maul (2h)</td>\n<td>2d6</td>\n<td>Crushing</td>\n<td>Brutal, Heavy</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>7s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Great axe (2h)</td>\n<td>2d6</td>\n<td>Blade</td>\n<td>Sunder, Heavy</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>8s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Flail</td>\n<td>1d8</td>\n<td>Crushing</td>\n<td>Brutal, Ignores shield Parry bonus</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>6s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sling</td>\n<td>1d4</td>\n<td>Crushing</td>\n<td>Ranged, cheap ammunition</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>5c</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hunting bow</td>\n<td>1d8</td>\n<td>Piercing</td>\n<td>Ranged</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>3s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Warbow</td>\n<td>1d10</td>\n<td>Piercing</td>\n<td>Ranged, Heavy</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>8s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Recurve horsebow</td>\n<td>1d8</td>\n<td>Piercing</td>\n<td>Ranged, usable mounted</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>12s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Light crossbow</td>\n<td>1d8</td>\n<td>Piercing</td>\n<td>Ranged, Armor-Piercing, Reload</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>9s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Heavy crossbow</td>\n<td>1d10</td>\n<td>Piercing</td>\n<td>Ranged, Armor-Piercing, Reload, Heavy</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>16s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Throwing knives (3)</td>\n<td>1d4</td>\n<td>Blade</td>\n<td>Throwable, Concealable</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>1s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Javelin</td>\n<td>1d6</td>\n<td>Piercing</td>\n<td>Throwable, Reach</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>40c</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Quarterstaff</td>\n<td>1d6</td>\n<td>Crushing</td>\n<td>Reach, Balanced</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>20c</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>War-scythe (peasant)</td>\n<td>1d8</td>\n<td>Blade</td>\n<td>Reach, improvised-Fine</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>1s</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Ammunition:</strong> a quiver of 20 arrows or bolts, 30c; sling stones, free; Fine armor-piercing bodkins, 2s the dozen. Powder and shot do not exist in the Dying Age.</p>\n<p><strong>Weapon traits glossary</strong> \u2014 <em>Balanced</em> +5 to your Parries \u00b7 <em>Brutal</em> target AV \u22121 \u00b7 <em>Sunder</em> on a Masterstroke, notch the enemy&rsquo;s shield or weapon \u00b7 <em>Set</em> vs a charger, free Strike first at +2 \u00b7 <em>Reach</em> strike from one band out; Reach 2, two \u00b7 <em>Armor-Piercing</em> halve AV (round up) \u00b7 <em>Heavy</em> needs Might 12+ or \u221210 \u00b7 <em>Throwable</em> usable at Near \u00b7 <em>Reload</em> costs your Action to reload \u00b7 <em>Versatile</em> one- or two-handed \u00b7 <em>Close</em> usable in a grapple \u00b7 <em>Concealable</em> hidden on a search unless Masterstroke.</p>"}, "sort": 100, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "tvcNH1xGychPkohc", "name": "A.2 \u2014 Armor & Shields", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>A.2 \u2014 Armor &amp; Shields</h2>\n<p>AV and Burden as Chapter 3 (armor rebalance, Rev 1.7). Unarmored characters still soak 1.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Armor</th>\n<th>AV</th>\n<th>Burden</th>\n<th>Load</th>\n<th>Price</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Padded jack / arming coat</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>1s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Heavy leathers</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>2s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Brigandine (studded)</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>8s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gambeson</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>4s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mail shirt (haubergeon)</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>15s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mail hauberk (full)</td>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>28s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reinforced mail (mail + plates)</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>45s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Half-plate</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>1g 20s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Full harness</td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>4g</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Open helm / skullcap</td>\n<td>+helm rule</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>2s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nasal / kettle helm</td>\n<td>+helm rule</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>4s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Closed great helm</td>\n<td>+helm rule (\u221210 senses, +5 Intim.)</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>12s</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Shield</th>\n<th>Effect</th>\n<th>Load</th>\n<th>Price</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Buckler</td>\n<td>+10 Parries; may Parry missiles</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>1s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Round shield</td>\n<td>+10 Parries, Parry missiles</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>2s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Kite shield</td>\n<td>+10 Parries, Parry missiles, +5 vs mounted</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>4s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tower / pavise</td>\n<td>+10 Parries, full cover kneeling; Heavy</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>7s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ironoak-core shield</td>\n<td>+10 Parries; holds one extra Sunder notch</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>9s</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}, "sort": 200, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "jrQvPHNfTDxRaymE", "name": "A.3 \u2014 Travel & Survival Gear", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>A.3 \u2014 Travel &amp; Survival Gear</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>Load</th>\n<th>Price</th>\n<th>Note</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Bedroll &amp; blanket</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>30c</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Winter bedroll (fleece)</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>1s</td>\n<td>Insulated: prevents ordinary Freezing (Ch. 5)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Traveler&rsquo;s cloak</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>40c</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Oiled rain-cloak</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>1s</td>\n<td>+10 vs wet-cold Conditions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fur-lined cloak</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>4s</td>\n<td>Insulated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Backpack / pack-frame</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>50c</td>\n<td>Holds up to 8 Load</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Satchel</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>20c</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Waterskin (full)</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>10c</td>\n<td>A day&rsquo;s water</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Trail rations (1 week)</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>70c</td>\n<td>Restores half Stamina; no real-meal bonus</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tinderbox &amp; flint</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>15c</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Torch (each)</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>2c</td>\n<td>1 hour; Near light</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lantern &amp; oil (night&rsquo;s worth)</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>2s</td>\n<td>Near light, hooded</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>50 ft hemp rope</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>40c</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Grappling hook</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>1s</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Climbing kit (pitons, harness)</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>3s</td>\n<td>+10 Acrobatics on climbs</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tent (two-person)</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>3s</td>\n<td>Shelter for two</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cook-pot &amp; kit</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>1s</td>\n<td>Enables field Cooking</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fishing line &amp; hooks</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>20c</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Snowshoes</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>60c</td>\n<td>Negates deep-snow travel penalty</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Skis / sled runners</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>2s</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Whetstone</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>10c</td>\n<td>Holds a blade&rsquo;s edge (not notches)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sewing kit</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>15c</td>\n<td>Mends cloth &amp; gambeson</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Spyglass (crude)</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>8s</td>\n<td>Far detail; rare</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Compass / lodestone</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>6s</td>\n<td>+10 Navigation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Chalk, charcoal sticks</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>5c</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Signal whistle / horn</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>30c</td>\n<td>Heard a mile</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Manacles &amp; key</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>1s</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Caltrops (bag)</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>1s</td>\n<td>Scatter: Near area, foot injury</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Oilskin map-case</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>20c</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}, "sort": 300, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "uqH5AMby9d5GwrTb", "name": "A.4 \u2014 Tools & Profession Kits", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>A.4 \u2014 Tools &amp; Profession Kits</h2>\n<p>A profession kit is the difference between attempting a skill and attempting it <em>well</em>: without the kit, the relevant skill suffers \u221220, and some tasks are impossible outright.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Kit</th>\n<th>Load</th>\n<th>Price</th>\n<th>Enables / Bonus</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Surgeon&rsquo;s roll</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>6s</td>\n<td>Surgery; treats Grievous Wounds (Ch. 3)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Herbalist&rsquo;s satchel</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>2s</td>\n<td>Herbalism; poultices</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Alchemist&rsquo;s case</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>9s</td>\n<td>Alchemy; distillation off a fixed bench</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Portable alchemy bench</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>18s</td>\n<td>Alchemy anywhere, \u221210 vs a real lab</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Smith&rsquo;s travel tools</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>5s</td>\n<td>Field repair; not Fine work (needs a forge)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Carpenter&rsquo;s tools</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>3s</td>\n<td>Carpentry, construction</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Leatherworker&rsquo;s kit</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>2s</td>\n<td>Leatherworking, armor repair</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cook&rsquo;s knife roll</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>2s</td>\n<td>Cooking without penalty</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Thief&rsquo;s tools (picks)</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>3s</td>\n<td>Larceny on locks; without, impossible</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Forger&rsquo;s kit (inks, seals)</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>5s</td>\n<td>Deception on documents</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cartographer&rsquo;s kit</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>2s</td>\n<td>Navigation, map-making</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Miner&rsquo;s tools</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>2s</td>\n<td>Excavation, ore</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fishing net</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>1s</td>\n<td>Bulk catch</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hunting &amp; skinning kit</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>1s</td>\n<td>Harvesting (Ch. 7) without penalty</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scribe&rsquo;s kit (quill, ink, vellum)</td>\n<td>0</td>\n<td>2s</td>\n<td>Records, letters, copying</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Musician&rsquo;s instrument</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>3s</td>\n<td>Performance, Presence in company</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Farrier&rsquo;s kit</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>2s</td>\n<td>Shoe &amp; tend horses</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Brewer&rsquo;s &amp; still kit</td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>6s</td>\n<td>Brewing &amp; Distilling</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}, "sort": 400, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "8nQNGNhgZBC0nizk", "name": "A.5 \u2014 Clothing, Containers & Sundries", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>A.5 \u2014 Clothing, Containers &amp; Sundries</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>Price</th>\n<th>Item</th>\n<th>Price</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Peasant&rsquo;s rough clothes</td>\n<td>30c</td>\n<td>Merchant&rsquo;s good clothes</td>\n<td>4s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Traveler&rsquo;s practical outfit</td>\n<td>1s</td>\n<td>Noble&rsquo;s fine attire</td>\n<td>2g</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Winter layers (wool)</td>\n<td>2s</td>\n<td>Courtly gown / doublet</td>\n<td>6s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sturdy boots</td>\n<td>1s</td>\n<td>Riding boots</td>\n<td>3s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wide hat / hood</td>\n<td>10c</td>\n<td>Signet ring (blank)</td>\n<td>5s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Belt &amp; pouches</td>\n<td>20c</td>\n<td>Locked strongbox</td>\n<td>3s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sack (large)</td>\n<td>3c</td>\n<td>Barrel</td>\n<td>20c</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Clay jug / flask</td>\n<td>4c</td>\n<td>Glass vial (each)</td>\n<td>8c</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wooden chest</td>\n<td>1s</td>\n<td>Iron-bound chest</td>\n<td>4s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Candles (bundle)</td>\n<td>10c</td>\n<td>Soap &amp; razor</td>\n<td>8c</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}, "sort": 500, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "X4vzHoqJdbJ93Ouw", "name": "A.6 \u2014 Trade Goods & Provisions", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>A.6 \u2014 Trade Goods &amp; Provisions</h2>\n<p>What caravans carry and what a settlement&rsquo;s Provision is made of. Prices are per unit noted; bulk moves with the Season and the Harvest (Ch. 8).</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Good</th>\n<th>Unit</th>\n<th>Price</th>\n<th>Note</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Grain (wheat, rye)</td>\n<td>sack</td>\n<td>30c</td>\n<td>Provision staple; price doubles in a Poor harvest</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Salt</td>\n<td>pound</td>\n<td>2s</td>\n<td>Gates preservation (Ch. 5); the Combine&rsquo;s whole business</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Charcoal</td>\n<td>cartload</td>\n<td>4s</td>\n<td>Gates Fine+ smithing (the Wood-Take)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Firewood</td>\n<td>cartload</td>\n<td>1s</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ale</td>\n<td>cask</td>\n<td>1s</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wine (common)</td>\n<td>bottle</td>\n<td>20c</td>\n<td>Wine (fine), 3s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cheese wheel</td>\n<td>each</td>\n<td>40c</td>\n<td>Keeps; travel food</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Salt fish / pork</td>\n<td>barrel</td>\n<td>5s</td>\n<td>Winter stores</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Honey</td>\n<td>jar</td>\n<td>50c</td>\n<td>Preservative, sweetener, poultice base</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wool (raw)</td>\n<td>bale</td>\n<td>3s</td>\n<td>Spun, 8s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Linen cloth</td>\n<td>bolt</td>\n<td>4s</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wool cloth</td>\n<td>bolt</td>\n<td>3s</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dyed / fine cloth</td>\n<td>bolt</td>\n<td>2g</td>\n<td>Presence in trade</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Iron (pig)</td>\n<td>bar</td>\n<td>1s</td>\n<td>Worked value many times this</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Copper / tin</td>\n<td>bar</td>\n<td>2s</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Silver (raw)</td>\n<td>ingot</td>\n<td>1g</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Leather (tanned hide)</td>\n<td>each</td>\n<td>1s</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Furs (common)</td>\n<td>each</td>\n<td>2s</td>\n<td>Furs (marten, fox), 1g</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Spices (pepper, clove)</td>\n<td>ounce</td>\n<td>3s</td>\n<td>Worth their weight; Cooking bonuses</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Amber / incense</td>\n<td>ounce</td>\n<td>6s</td>\n<td>Ritual &amp; trade</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Parchment / vellum</td>\n<td>sheet</td>\n<td>20c</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Oil (lamp)</td>\n<td>flask</td>\n<td>15c</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pottery (set)</td>\n<td>each</td>\n<td>30c</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Livestock: chicken</td>\n<td>each</td>\n<td>20c</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Livestock: goat / sheep</td>\n<td>each</td>\n<td>2s</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Livestock: pig</td>\n<td>each</td>\n<td>4s</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Livestock: milk cow</td>\n<td>each</td>\n<td>2g</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Draft ox (a plow-team half)</td>\n<td>each</td>\n<td>5g</td>\n<td>The Ox-Paradox made flesh (Ch. 8)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Riding horse</td>\n<td>each</td>\n<td>8g</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Warhorse (trained)</td>\n<td>each</td>\n<td>40g</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mule / donkey</td>\n<td>each</td>\n<td>3g</td>\n<td>Carries 12 Load, sure-footed</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}, "sort": 600, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "uohMw6Sqya87hqAT", "name": "A.7 \u2014 Cooking Ingredients & Their Properties", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>A.7 \u2014 Cooking Ingredients &amp; Their Properties</h2>\n<p>Ingredients carry the Nine Properties (Ch. 5). Common market stock below; monster parts (A.8) and foraged rarities carry the potent ones. A meal&rsquo;s effect is built from its ingredients&rsquo; properties on a Cooking Reckoning.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Ingredient</th>\n<th>Properties</th>\n<th>Price</th>\n<th>Note</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Bread, oats, turnip</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n<td>5c</td>\n<td>Filling; the base of every pot</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Root vegetables</td>\n<td>Vitality 1</td>\n<td>8c</td>\n<td>Keeps through winter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Onion, garlic</td>\n<td>Vitality 1, +infection resist</td>\n<td>6c</td>\n<td>Folk medicine that works</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Red meat (beef, pork)</td>\n<td>Vitality 2, Energy 1</td>\n<td>20c</td>\n<td>The real-meal core (Ch. 5)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Game meat</td>\n<td>Vitality 1, Energy 1</td>\n<td>free-15c</td>\n<td>If you hunted it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fish</td>\n<td>Vitality 1</td>\n<td>10c</td>\n<td>Fresh; salts for winter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Eggs</td>\n<td>Vitality 1, Spirit 1</td>\n<td>8c</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Butter, cream</td>\n<td>Vitality 1, Heat 1</td>\n<td>15c</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Honey</td>\n<td>Regeneration 1</td>\n<td>50c/jar</td>\n<td>Wound-food; preservative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Chilies, mustard, horseradish</td>\n<td>Heat 2</td>\n<td>20c</td>\n<td>Warms vs cold Conditions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mint, snowberry</td>\n<td>Cold 1</td>\n<td>10c</td>\n<td>Cools vs Sweltering</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bitterroot</td>\n<td>Toxicity 1 (mild)</td>\n<td>free</td>\n<td>Purge; hangover cure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Kingsfoil / heal-all</td>\n<td>Regeneration 1</td>\n<td>30c</td>\n<td>Poultice &amp; broth base</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wake-root (foraged)</td>\n<td>Energy 2</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n<td>Sleeplessness resistance one night</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Emberleaf tea</td>\n<td>Heat 1, Energy 1</td>\n<td>20c</td>\n<td>Morning of a cold march</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fellwort mushroom</td>\n<td>Toxicity 2</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n<td>Deadly raw; a delicacy prepared right (Cooking 60+)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Marrowbone</td>\n<td>Vitality 2, Regeneration 1</td>\n<td>free</td>\n<td>Broth that mends</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Grave-morel</td>\n<td>Spirit 2, Corruption 1</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n<td>Thin-Place fungus; risky flavor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sweetsalt (rare)</td>\n<td>preserves + Vitality 1</td>\n<td>4s</td>\n<td>Combine luxury good</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}, "sort": 700, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "ptVt2ENhywNB2nCI", "name": "A.8 \u2014 Monster Parts & Rare Materials", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>A.8 \u2014 Monster Parts &amp; Rare Materials</h2>\n<p>Harvested from the bestiary (Chapter 7 and Appendix B). These are the ingredients, reagents, and crafting materials that make a hunt pay \u2014 and the reason a killed monster ripples through an economy.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Part</th>\n<th>From</th>\n<th>Uses / Properties</th>\n<th>Value</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Wolf pelt (winter)</td>\n<td>Grey Wolves</td>\n<td>Insulation-grade leather</td>\n<td>2s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Aurochs horn &amp; hide</td>\n<td>Marsh Aurochs</td>\n<td>Horn: tools/bows; hide: heavy leather</td>\n<td>3s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bristleback plate</td>\n<td>Bristleback</td>\n<td>Natural brigandine; AV crafting</td>\n<td>6s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tallow-hound fat</td>\n<td>Tallow Hound</td>\n<td>Renders to corpse-candles (Cold Hearth ward)</td>\n<td>2s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Seam-moth dust</td>\n<td>Seam-Moths</td>\n<td>Alchemy: Spirit 2, Corruption 1</td>\n<td>3s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wyrm scale</td>\n<td>Vael Wyrm</td>\n<td>Peerless armor plate; nearly unpierceable</td>\n<td>not for sale</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wyrm blood</td>\n<td>Vael Wyrm</td>\n<td>Alchemy reagent; Corruption 3; Ash on the untrained</td>\n<td>1g/vial</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Saint&rsquo;s tears (calcified)</td>\n<td>Weeping Saint</td>\n<td>Reliquary relic; Spirit 3; witch-finder bait</td>\n<td>priceless/damning</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Boar tusk</td>\n<td>wild boar</td>\n<td>Charms, spear-points</td>\n<td>40c</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stag antler</td>\n<td>red deer</td>\n<td>Tools, buttons, glue</td>\n<td>60c</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bear gall &amp; fat</td>\n<td>cave bear</td>\n<td>Medicine reagent; Regeneration 2</td>\n<td>2s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Venom gland</td>\n<td>adder, spider</td>\n<td>Toxicity 2\u20133; coating or cure</td>\n<td>1s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Raptor feathers</td>\n<td>eagle, owl</td>\n<td>Fletching (+ to Archery Fine arrows)</td>\n<td>50c</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Beeswax &amp; comb</td>\n<td>wild bees</td>\n<td>Seals, candles, salves</td>\n<td>30c</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ambergris (beach-find)</td>\n<td>the sea</td>\n<td>Incense, perfume, ritual</td>\n<td>6g</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ironoak heartwood</td>\n<td>the Thornwold</td>\n<td>Shield cores, hafts that don&rsquo;t break</td>\n<td>4s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Grave-iron (barrow-find)</td>\n<td>old dead</td>\n<td>Cold-forged; bites the Hollowed</td>\n<td>varies</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}, "sort": 800, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "5Atbqv1TYluoNqHc", "name": "A.9 \u2014 Services, Lodging & Passage", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>A.9 \u2014 Services, Lodging &amp; Passage</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Service</th>\n<th>Price</th>\n<th>Service</th>\n<th>Price</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Poor meal / bread &amp; ale</td>\n<td>3c</td>\n<td>Common inn room &amp; board</td>\n<td>40c/night</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Good meal</td>\n<td>10c</td>\n<td>Fine inn / private room</td>\n<td>1s/night</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Stable a horse</td>\n<td>5c/night</td>\n<td>Bath &amp; laundry</td>\n<td>8c</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cart passage (per day)</td>\n<td>10c</td>\n<td>River barge passage</td>\n<td>30c/day</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sea passage (coastal leg)</td>\n<td>2s</td>\n<td>Guide / porter (per day)</td>\n<td>15c</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Message carried (per region)</td>\n<td>1s</td>\n<td>Bribe (petty official)</td>\n<td>1\u20135s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Healer (village)</td>\n<td>20c</td>\n<td>Surgeon (town)</td>\n<td>2s + risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scribe writes a letter</td>\n<td>10c</td>\n<td>Lawyer / advocate before a bench</td>\n<td>1g</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mercenary (per day)</td>\n<td>1s</td>\n<td>Mercenary band (per week)</td>\n<td>varies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Toll (bridge, gate)</td>\n<td>2\u201310c</td>\n<td>Guild entry fee</td>\n<td>5g+</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}, "sort": 900, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}], "folder": null, "sort": 600, "ownership": {"default": 0}, "flags": {}}
{"_id": "wHPD3PvfjEJEa7E1", "name": "Appendix B \u2014 The Field Guide", "pages": [{"_id": "AdAF2fREAuRzH0h5", "name": "Overview", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h1>Appendix B \u2014 The Field Guide</h1>\n<p>What walks, grazes, hunts, grows, and should not exist. Each entry gives a stat line, what it does, and what it yields when harvested. For deep stat blocks, ecology cascades, and the Dread rule, see Chapter 7.</p>\n<p><strong>Reading the line:</strong> HP / Threshold / Defense \u00b7 attack \u00b7 Fear \u00b7 Morale \u00b7 <strong>Harvest</strong> (what a successful Harvesting Reckoning yields, Ch. 7). Where Fear is &lsquo;\u2014&rsquo;, the thing is not frightening; where Morale is &lsquo;n/a&rsquo;, it does not fight. Prices reference Appendix A.</p>"}, "sort": 0, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "RtaoYCMwvQGnRMfq", "name": "B.1 \u2014 Game & Grazers (the larder that walks)", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>B.1 \u2014 Game &amp; Grazers (the larder that walks)</h2>\n<p>These feed the world. Their Population Dials (Ch. 7) drive Provision as surely as any harvest; hunt them out and predators starve or turn to livestock.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Animal</th>\n<th>HP/Thr/Def</th>\n<th>Attack</th>\n<th>Fear/Morale</th>\n<th>Harvest</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Hare / rabbit</td>\n<td>3/2/13</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n<td>\u2014/Wavering</td>\n<td>1 ration; pelt (10c)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Red deer / stag</td>\n<td>10/4/12</td>\n<td>Antler 1d6</td>\n<td>\u2014/Wavering</td>\n<td>4 rations; hide (1s); antler (60c)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Roe deer</td>\n<td>7/3/12</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n<td>\u2014/Wavering</td>\n<td>2 rations; hide</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wild boar</td>\n<td>14/5/11</td>\n<td>Tusk 1d8 Brutal</td>\n<td>\u221210 (charging)/Steady</td>\n<td>4 rations (Vitality 2); tusks (40c); hide</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Marsh aurochs</td>\n<td>26/8/8</td>\n<td>Gore 1d10+5</td>\n<td>\u221210 (herd)/Steady</td>\n<td>12 rations; horn &amp; hide (3s); see Ch. 7</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mountain goat / ibex</td>\n<td>6/3/14</td>\n<td>Butt 1d6</td>\n<td>\u2014/Wavering</td>\n<td>2 rations; horn; sure-footed hide</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wild sheep (mouflon)</td>\n<td>6/3/12</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n<td>\u2014/Wavering</td>\n<td>2 rations; wool; horn</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Elk / moose</td>\n<td>18/6/10</td>\n<td>Kick 1d10; antler 1d8</td>\n<td>\u221210 (rut)/Steady</td>\n<td>8 rations; huge hide; antler</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wild pony</td>\n<td>12/5/12</td>\n<td>Kick 1d8</td>\n<td>\u2014/Wavering</td>\n<td>6 rations; hide; tameable</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Duck / goose / grouse</td>\n<td>2/1/14 (flies)</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n<td>\u2014/n-a</td>\n<td>1 ration; feathers; down (Insulation)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pheasant / partridge</td>\n<td>2/1/14</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n<td>\u2014/n-a</td>\n<td>1 ration; fine feathers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Salmon / trout run</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n<td>\u2014/n-a</td>\n<td>bulk rations in season; the river&rsquo;s wealth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Eel</td>\n<td>2/1/12</td>\n<td>Bite 1d3</td>\n<td>\u2014/Wavering</td>\n<td>2 rations (fatty); skin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Carp / pike (pond)</td>\n<td>3/2/11</td>\n<td>Pike bite 1d4</td>\n<td>\u2014/Wavering</td>\n<td>2 rations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hedgehog / squirrel</td>\n<td>1/1/13</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n<td>\u2014/n-a</td>\n<td>scraps; a child&rsquo;s supper</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wild fowl eggs (nest)</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n<td>\u2014/n-a</td>\n<td>Vitality; Spirit 1 (Cooking)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Beaver</td>\n<td>5/2/12</td>\n<td>Bite 1d4</td>\n<td>\u2014/Wavering</td>\n<td>3 rations; prized pelt (2s); castor (Alchemy)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Otter</td>\n<td>4/2/14</td>\n<td>Bite 1d4</td>\n<td>\u2014/Wavering</td>\n<td>pelt (2s); fish-thief, farmer&rsquo;s foe</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wild honeybee swarm</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n<td>Stings: Toxicity 1 area</td>\n<td>\u22125/n-a</td>\n<td>honey &amp; wax (30c); Regeneration</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}, "sort": 100, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "EiHO3lPulVpxYjmb", "name": "B.2 \u2014 Predators (the teeth in the dark)", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>B.2 \u2014 Predators (the teeth in the dark)</h2>\n<p>Every one is also ecology: remove it and its prey floods the fields (the wolf cascade, Ch. 7); leave it hungry in a famine year and it comes to the byres.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Animal</th>\n<th>HP/Thr/Def</th>\n<th>Attack</th>\n<th>Fear/Morale</th>\n<th>Harvest</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Grey wolf</td>\n<td>22/6/13</td>\n<td>Bite 1d6+1</td>\n<td>\u2014/Wavering (pack \u221210)</td>\n<td>pelt (2s); teeth; 4 rations. See Ch. 7</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wolf pack (6\u201312)</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n<td>as wolves, Harry Move</td>\n<td>\u221210 at night/Wavering</td>\n<td>the whole pack&rsquo;s worth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Red fox</td>\n<td>5/2/14</td>\n<td>Bite 1d4</td>\n<td>\u2014/Wavering</td>\n<td>fine pelt (1g); hen-thief</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lynx / wildcat</td>\n<td>10/4/14</td>\n<td>Claw 1d6, pounce</td>\n<td>\u22125/Wavering</td>\n<td>spotted pelt (3s); 2 rations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Brown / cave bear</td>\n<td>30/8/10</td>\n<td>Claw 1d8; maul 1d10</td>\n<td>\u221215/Steady</td>\n<td>huge pelt (6s); gall &amp; fat (Regeneration 2); 10 rations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Boar (cornered)</td>\n<td>14/5/11</td>\n<td>Tusk 1d8 Brutal</td>\n<td>\u221210/Hard</td>\n<td>see B.1; deadlier when cornered</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wolverine</td>\n<td>12/5/13</td>\n<td>Claw 1d6 Brutal</td>\n<td>\u221210 (fearless)/Hard</td>\n<td>pelt; a reputation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Badger (cornered)</td>\n<td>8/4/12</td>\n<td>Bite 1d6 Brutal</td>\n<td>\u22125/Steady</td>\n<td>pelt; fat; ferocity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Golden eagle / raptor</td>\n<td>6/3/16 (flies)</td>\n<td>Talon 1d6</td>\n<td>\u22125/Wavering</td>\n<td>feathers (fletching); talons; awe</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Great owl</td>\n<td>4/2/16 (flies)</td>\n<td>Talon 1d4</td>\n<td>\u22125 (silent)/Wavering</td>\n<td>feathers; an omen in most villages</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Adder / viper</td>\n<td>2/1/13</td>\n<td>Bite: Toxicity 2</td>\n<td>\u22125/Wavering</td>\n<td>venom gland (1s); skin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Constrictor (marsh)</td>\n<td>14/5/11</td>\n<td>Coil 1d6 + grapple</td>\n<td>\u221210/Steady</td>\n<td>skin; fat; 4 rations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Giant marsh leech</td>\n<td>3/2/9</td>\n<td>Latch: Bleeding 1/turn</td>\n<td>\u22125/Wavering</td>\n<td>medicinal (Medicine +10 bleeding)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wild dog pack (feral)</td>\n<td>10/4/12</td>\n<td>Bite 1d4</td>\n<td>\u2014/Wavering</td>\n<td>the desperate cousins of wolves</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wildcat swarm (rats)</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n<td>Bite: disease risk</td>\n<td>\u22125/n-a</td>\n<td>plague vector (Ch. 8); nothing worth taking</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cave spider (large)</td>\n<td>6/3/13</td>\n<td>Bite: Toxicity 2</td>\n<td>\u221210/Wavering</td>\n<td>venom; silk (cordage)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Boar-hound (war-bred)</td>\n<td>12/5/12</td>\n<td>Bite 1d6 + hold</td>\n<td>\u2014/Hard</td>\n<td>trained, loyal; the poacher&rsquo;s dread</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}, "sort": 200, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "nqCucbXRYVJf0iax", "name": "B.3 \u2014 Livestock, Beasts of Burden & Vermin", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>B.3 \u2014 Livestock, Beasts of Burden &amp; Vermin</h2>\n<p>The domestic economy and its parasites. Livestock are Provision and Draft (Ch. 8); vermin are the Murrain&rsquo;s carriers and the famine&rsquo;s heralds.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Animal</th>\n<th>HP/Thr/Def</th>\n<th>Note / Attack</th>\n<th>Harvest / Yield</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Chicken / duck (kept)</td>\n<td>1/1/12</td>\n<td>eggs daily</td>\n<td>meat; eggs; feathers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Goat</td>\n<td>5/3/11</td>\n<td>Butt 1d4; eats anything</td>\n<td>milk; meat; hide; the poor&rsquo;s cow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sheep</td>\n<td>6/3/10</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n<td>wool (annual); meat; the wool trade</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pig</td>\n<td>8/4/10</td>\n<td>Bite 1d4</td>\n<td>the most meat per acre; eats scraps &amp; worse</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Milk cow</td>\n<td>14/6/9</td>\n<td>Kick 1d6</td>\n<td>milk; calves; a family&rsquo;s wealth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Draft ox</td>\n<td>18/7/8</td>\n<td>Gore 1d8</td>\n<td>a plow-team; the Ox-Paradox (Ch. 8)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Riding horse</td>\n<td>16/6/11</td>\n<td>Kick 1d8</td>\n<td>travel; status; 8g</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Warhorse (destrier)</td>\n<td>22/7/11</td>\n<td>Hooves 1d10; trained</td>\n<td>charges; +5 mounted; 40g</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mule / donkey</td>\n<td>12/5/11</td>\n<td>Kick 1d6</td>\n<td>carries 12 Load; stubborn; sure-footed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Herding dog</td>\n<td>6/3/12</td>\n<td>Bite 1d3</td>\n<td>worth three shepherds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Barn cat</td>\n<td>1/1/14</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n<td>rat-killer; keeps the grain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rats (infestation)</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n<td>disease; grain-loss</td>\n<td>the Murrain&rsquo;s carriage; Provision drain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Crows / ravens</td>\n<td>1/1/15</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n<td>omen; carrion; cleverer than they look</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Locust swarm</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n<td>strips a field in a day</td>\n<td>a Harvest Failure with wings (Ch. 8)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Weevils / grain-worm</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n<td>spoils stores</td>\n<td>why the granary must stay dry</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Flies / maggots</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n<td>disease; wound-rot</td>\n<td>also debride wounds (Medicine folk-trick)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fleas / lice</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n<td>plague vector</td>\n<td>the true killer in every siege</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}, "sort": 300, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "BGa3V8g1VbGtKcJ1", "name": "B.4 \u2014 Flora, Fungi & Foraged Things", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>B.4 \u2014 Flora, Fungi &amp; Foraged Things</h2>\n<p>The green economy: food, medicine, poison, and the ingredients of both cooking and the Patient Art (Ch. 5). A Herbalism or Lore: Nature Reckoning identifies and gathers; the Properties are for Cooking and Alchemy.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Plant / Fungus</th>\n<th>Properties / Use</th>\n<th>Where</th>\n<th>Note</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Wheat / rye / barley</td>\n<td>Provision staple</td>\n<td>farmland</td>\n<td>the whole world rests on it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Turnip / cabbage / bean</td>\n<td>Vitality 1; keeps</td>\n<td>gardens</td>\n<td>winter survival</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Apple / pear / berry</td>\n<td>Vitality 1</td>\n<td>orchards, hedge</td>\n<td>cider; preserves</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Kingsfoil (heal-all)</td>\n<td>Regeneration 1</td>\n<td>meadow</td>\n<td>poultice &amp; broth; +10 recovery</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Willowbark</td>\n<td>pain relief; fever</td>\n<td>riverbank</td>\n<td>the poor man&rsquo;s medicine</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Yarrow</td>\n<td>stops bleeding</td>\n<td>field</td>\n<td>field-dressing; +10 First Aid</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Comfrey (knitbone)</td>\n<td>mends bone</td>\n<td>damp ground</td>\n<td>Grievous-Wound care</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Feverfew</td>\n<td>breaks fever</td>\n<td>garden</td>\n<td>plague nursing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wake-root</td>\n<td>Energy 2</td>\n<td>deep forest</td>\n<td>one sleepless night without penalty</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bitterroot</td>\n<td>purge; Toxicity 1</td>\n<td>waste ground</td>\n<td>poison cure; hangover</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nightcap mushroom</td>\n<td>Toxicity 3 (lethal)</td>\n<td>shade</td>\n<td>murder; or a delicacy at Cooking 70+</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fellwort mushroom</td>\n<td>Toxicity 2</td>\n<td>rot</td>\n<td>deadly raw, prized prepared</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Emberleaf</td>\n<td>Heat 1, Energy 1</td>\n<td>hills</td>\n<td>the marching tea</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Snowberry / mint</td>\n<td>Cold 1</td>\n<td>cold ground</td>\n<td>cools; freshens</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Deathcap / destroying angel</td>\n<td>Toxicity 3</td>\n<td>woodland</td>\n<td>no antidote; assassins&rsquo; friend</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Grave-morel</td>\n<td>Spirit 2, Corruption 1</td>\n<td>Thin Places</td>\n<td>risky flavor; Seam-touched</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Witch-hazel</td>\n<td>astringent; wards</td>\n<td>hedgerow</td>\n<td>folk protection; real infection-resistance</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mandrake</td>\n<td>Spirit 3; hallucinogen</td>\n<td>old graves</td>\n<td>ritual reagent; screams when pulled (folklore)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Flax / hemp</td>\n<td>fiber; rope; oil</td>\n<td>field</td>\n<td>cloth, cordage, lamp-oil</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Woad / madder</td>\n<td>dye</td>\n<td>field</td>\n<td>the cloth trade&rsquo;s color</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ironoak</td>\n<td>Peerless timber</td>\n<td>the Thornwold</td>\n<td>shield cores; hafts; sacred to Deep-Forest Folk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ash / yew / hornbeam</td>\n<td>bow-wood; shafts</td>\n<td>forest</td>\n<td>the warbow&rsquo;s soul (Carpentry)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bog-myrtle / sweet gale</td>\n<td>preserves ale; repels flies</td>\n<td>marsh</td>\n<td>brewer&rsquo;s herb</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mistletoe</td>\n<td>Spirit 1; poison in dose</td>\n<td>old oaks</td>\n<td>ritual; kiss or kill</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Blightweed</td>\n<td>Corruption 1; spreads on Ashen ground</td>\n<td>the Ashen March</td>\n<td>the land turning; a warning to farmers</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}, "sort": 400, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "qsHlP3GPJ8ihbHe2", "name": "B.5 \u2014 Waters & Skies", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>B.5 \u2014 Waters &amp; Skies</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Animal</th>\n<th>HP/Thr/Def</th>\n<th>Note</th>\n<th>Harvest</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>River fish (shoal)</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n<td>seasonal run</td>\n<td>bulk rations; the barge-towns&rsquo; wealth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pike (large)</td>\n<td>6/3/11</td>\n<td>Bite 1d6; ambush</td>\n<td>4 rations; a pond&rsquo;s tyrant</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Catfish (deep)</td>\n<td>10/4/9</td>\n<td>Gulp 1d6</td>\n<td>fatty rations; whiskers (Alchemy)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sea cod / herring</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n<td>coastal shoals</td>\n<td>the salt-fish trade (Ch. 5)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Crab / lobster (coast)</td>\n<td>2/2/12</td>\n<td>Pinch 1d4</td>\n<td>delicacy; shell (Alchemy)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Seal</td>\n<td>10/4/12</td>\n<td>Bite 1d6</td>\n<td>blubber (oil, Insulation); pelt; meat</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gull / seabird</td>\n<td>1/1/16</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n<td>eggs (cliff-harvest, dangerous); feathers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Heron / crane</td>\n<td>3/2/15</td>\n<td>Stab 1d4</td>\n<td>marsh omen; feathers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Swan</td>\n<td>6/3/13</td>\n<td>Buffet 1d6</td>\n<td>noble&rsquo;s game; fiercely territorial</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bat colony</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n<td>disease risk</td>\n<td>guano (Alchemy: saltpetre); omen</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Songbirds</td>\n<td>1/1/16</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n<td>the dawn chorus; caged, a luxury</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Kingfisher / dipper</td>\n<td>1/1/17</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n<td>a clean-river sign; fortune</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}, "sort": 500, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "XMSSyU2EI4pODduX", "name": "B.6 \u2014 The Seam-Touched & the Horrors", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>B.6 \u2014 The Seam-Touched &amp; the Horrors</h2>\n<p>What the dying age left behind, and what magic makes. These are rare by design \u2014 a region should have <em>one</em> signature horror, not a menagerie. All inflict Ash on the harvest (Ch. 4); several are Dread (Ch. 7). Fear ratings are real; bring Composure.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Horror</th>\n<th>HP/Thr/Def</th>\n<th>Attack</th>\n<th>Fear/Morale</th>\n<th>Harvest / Ash</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Tallow Hound</td>\n<td>14/5/12</td>\n<td>Bite 1d6; corpse-stink</td>\n<td>\u221210/Hard</td>\n<td>fat \u2192 corpse-candles; Ash 1. See Ch. 7</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Seam-Moths (swarm)</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n<td>Spirit-drain: 1 Resolve/turn near</td>\n<td>\u221210/n-a</td>\n<td>moth-dust (Alchemy Spirit 2); Ash 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The Hollowed</td>\n<td>varies (was a PC)</td>\n<td>as its lost skills</td>\n<td>\u221220 (\u221240 if known)/Fanatic</td>\n<td>nothing but grief; Dread. Ch. 7</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The Standing Dead</td>\n<td>18/6/9</td>\n<td>Grave-cold grip 1d8</td>\n<td>\u221215/Fanatic</td>\n<td>grave-iron; the rite that made it. Ch. 7</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Barrow-wight</td>\n<td>20/7/11</td>\n<td>Chill blade 1d8; saps Might</td>\n<td>\u221220/Fanatic</td>\n<td>old gold; a cursed blade; Ash 2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ash-Revenant</td>\n<td>16/6/10</td>\n<td>Burning grasp 1d6 Fire</td>\n<td>\u221215/Fanatic</td>\n<td>ash-glass; the memory of its death; Ash 2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bog-drowned (grabber)</td>\n<td>10/4/8</td>\n<td>Drag under 1d6 + drown</td>\n<td>\u221210/Steady</td>\n<td>what it pulled down before you; Ash 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Thin-Place Echo</td>\n<td>\u2014</td>\n<td>repeats your worst moment</td>\n<td>\u221215/n-a</td>\n<td>a Thin Place opens; no body; Ash 2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wretch (Blighted beast)</td>\n<td>by base animal +50%</td>\n<td>base +Corruption</td>\n<td>\u221210/Hard</td>\n<td>corrupted parts; Ash 1; a warning</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Corpse-choir</td>\n<td>12/5/9 (each)</td>\n<td>Wailing: Composure or flee</td>\n<td>\u221215/Fanatic</td>\n<td>nothing; a congregation&rsquo;s sin made flesh</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The Weeping Saint</td>\n<td>\u2014/region-scale</td>\n<td>see Ch. 7</td>\n<td>\u221225/Fanatic</td>\n<td>calcified tears; Dread; a campaign</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vael Wyrm</td>\n<td>31/11/12</td>\n<td>Bite 2d8+8; tail 2d6+8</td>\n<td>\u221230/Hard</td>\n<td>scale, blood; apex Dread. Ch. 7</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Grave-moth queen</td>\n<td>20/6/10</td>\n<td>brood; Spirit-drain aura</td>\n<td>\u221220/Steady</td>\n<td>a Thin Place&rsquo;s heart; Ash 3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hunger-that-walks (famine spirit)</td>\n<td>24/7/9</td>\n<td>touch: inflicts Hungry (no food cures)</td>\n<td>\u221220/Fanatic</td>\n<td>ends when the famine ends; Ash 2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ash-wolf pack (Blighted)</td>\n<td>22/6/13</td>\n<td>Bite 1d6+1; Ash on wound</td>\n<td>\u221215/Hard</td>\n<td>corrupted pelts; the wolf cascade gone wrong</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Building your own horror:</strong> take a real animal or a human grief, add a Seam-wound, and ask what it <em>wants</em> \u2014 horrors in Blightmarch are hungers with shapes, not monsters with hit points. Give it one Fear rating, one Move, one thing it yields, and one thing it costs the world. That is a Chapter 7 Ecology Block with the lights off.</p>"}, "sort": 600, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "yOr74ioqil81cHHa", "name": "B.7 \u2014 Foraging & Encounter Tables", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>B.7 \u2014 Foraging &amp; Encounter Tables</h2>\n<p>Roll when the party travels, camps, or forages. Terrain sets the die; the Season and Population Dials (Ch. 8) shift it.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>d12</th>\n<th>Forest</th>\n<th>Marsh</th>\n<th>Hill / Moor</th>\n<th>Coast</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>red deer</td>\n<td>eel</td>\n<td>mountain goat</td>\n<td>cod shoal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>boar</td>\n<td>heron</td>\n<td>wild sheep</td>\n<td>seal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>grey wolves</td>\n<td>constrictor</td>\n<td>golden eagle</td>\n<td>gulls (eggs)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>fox</td>\n<td>marsh leech</td>\n<td>lynx</td>\n<td>crab</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>hare</td>\n<td>wildfowl</td>\n<td>hare</td>\n<td>driftwood &amp; salvage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>kingsfoil patch</td>\n<td>willowbark</td>\n<td>emberleaf</td>\n<td>ambergris (rare)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>boar (cornered)</td>\n<td>bog-drowned!</td>\n<td>bear</td>\n<td>storm coming (Ch. 8)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>woodcutters</td>\n<td>fisher-folk</td>\n<td>shepherds</td>\n<td>fishing boat</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>bandits (B.7 group)</td>\n<td>smugglers</td>\n<td>raiders</td>\n<td>pirates (offshore)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>ironoak grove</td>\n<td>Thin-Place echo!</td>\n<td>barrow (wight?)</td>\n<td>wreck (loot + risk)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>deep-forest folk</td>\n<td>fever-air (disease)</td>\n<td>standing stones</td>\n<td>salt-pans (Combine)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12</td>\n<td>the wrong quiet (Seam)</td>\n<td>the Weeping Saint&rsquo;s reach</td>\n<td>ash on the wind</td>\n<td>a ship, becalmed &amp; silent</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"}, "sort": 700, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}], "folder": null, "sort": 700, "ownership": {"default": 0}, "flags": {}}
{"_id": "i9QvyMswmPjJd8vN", "name": "Chapter 6 \u2014 World Building and Factions", "pages": [{"_id": "mhTEF5L15tGtYqDl", "name": "Overview", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h1>PART TWO \u2014 THE GAME MASTER&rsquo;S BOOK</h1>\n<p><em>Chapters 6 through 8 are written for the Game Master. They contain the machinery behind the curtain: the true faces of factions, the secrets of the Cindral Reach, what the monsters really are, and the engines that move the world between sessions. Players will meet all of it from the inside, at the table \u2014 reading it here trades that discovery for a map. </em><em>If you intend to play rather than run Blightmarch, stop at the end of Chapter 5.</em>**</p>\n<hr />\n<h1>CHAPTER 6: WORLD BUILDING AND FACTIONS</h1>\n<p><em>Ask who rules a valley and you&rsquo;ll get a name. Ask who decides what bread costs there, who the miller fears, and where the grain went the year nobody starved but everybody paid \u2014 now you&rsquo;re reading the actual map.</em></p>\n<p>This chapter is the living world&rsquo;s skeleton: how to build regions, settlements, ruins, and trade; how factions think, act, and collide on their own clock; and how consequences travel \u2014 the Ripple engine that makes a killed wolf or a failed harvest matter three villages away. It names, at last, the polities Chapter 2&rsquo;s cultures promised, and pays Chapter 4&rsquo;s debts: witch-finders, grimoire politics, and the arithmetic of cults.</p>\n<p><strong>Contents:</strong> 1. The Cindral Reach \u00b7 2. Building Regions \u00b7 3. Settlements \u00b7 4. Ruins and Dungeons \u00b7 5. Trade Routes \u00b7 6. The Faction Engine \u00b7 7. Faction Types \u00b7 8. Cults in Full \u00b7 9. Three Worked Factions \u00b7 10. NPCs That Act \u00b7 11. The Ripple Engine \u00b7 12. A Faction Turn, Worked</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 0, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "TZlQ1YGaKFzNER35", "name": "1. The Cindral Reach \u2014 the Example Setting", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>1. The Cindral Reach \u2014 the Example Setting</h2>\n<p>One river basin, seven powers, and enough fault lines to run a decade of play. Every culture archetype from Chapter 2 has its address here. GMs may use it whole, strip it for parts, or generate their own with Sections 2\u20135.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The Kingdom of Vael</strong> <em>(Feudal Heartlands).</em> Grain, cavalry, and a dynasty three generations past its best king. <strong>Aldwatch Ford</strong> \u2014 where Yorna Vask&rsquo;s unit died (Chapters 2\u20133) \u2014 is a Vaeli crossing, officially a victory. Tension: the crown owes the Saltmark Combine two harvests it hasn&rsquo;t grown yet.</li>\n<li><strong>The Cindral League</strong> <em>(River-Cities).</em> Five toll-cities strung down the river Cindral, married to each other&rsquo;s banks and suspicious of each other&rsquo;s scales. Tension: the League&rsquo;s charter forbids a standing army, and someone is quietly hiring one anyway.</li>\n<li><strong>The Free Port of Karsh</strong> <em>(Coastal Free Towns).</em> Salt, ships, and no questions at the customs house that coin can&rsquo;t answer. Tension: Karsh&rsquo;s grey market is where confiscated grimoires surface \u2014 which means the Pale Flame&rsquo;s inventory leaks.</li>\n<li><strong>The Morr Clanholds</strong> <em>(Highland Clans).</em> Iron in the hills, debts in the blood. Tension: the mountain-steel seams the guilds covet sit under clan barrow-grounds.</li>\n<li><strong>The Vashkar Grass</strong> <em>(Steppe-Riders).</em> Horse-lords who tax the east road by escorting it. Tension: something is moving the herds off their thousand-year routes, and the Vashkari want to know what before they admit it&rsquo;s happening.</li>\n<li><strong>The Thornwold</strong> <em>(Deep-Forest Folk).</em> The forest that remembers promise-breakers. Tension: charcoal burners from Vael keep not coming home, and Vael keeps sending more.</li>\n<li><strong>The Ashen March.</strong> The seventh power is not a nation: the Blighted frontier upriver, where the old age died hardest \u2014 Thin Places, Ashfall weather (Chapter 8), and the ruins everyone wants excavated by somebody else. Every faction below has a March policy. None of them will say it aloud.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 100, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "qPwBhozTJHk9jyaA", "name": "2. Building Regions", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>2. Building Regions</h2>\n<p>Build outward from wherever the players stand. A region needs six lines:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Terrain &amp; Climate Profile</strong> \u2014 landform plus the climate inputs Chapter 8&rsquo;s weather engine reads (latitude band, altitude, humidity, wind exposure).</li>\n<li><strong>Resources (1d6 of them)</strong> \u2014 grain, timber, ore, salt, fish, pasture, quarry, herbs, game, or something stranger. Resources are what trade routes exist <em>for</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>One Wound</strong> \u2014 the regional problem: a plague pocket, a broken bridge, a Thin Place, a tax revolt, an empty granary.</li>\n<li><strong>One Wonder</strong> \u2014 the reason anyone comes: a shrine, a master&rsquo;s forge, a hot spring, a library, a legendary hunting ground.</li>\n<li><strong>Two Powers</strong> \u2014 the factions that contest it.</li>\n<li><strong>One Fault Line</strong> \u2014 the specific issue those powers will collide over first.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><em>(Quick-roll d-tables for all six live in Chapter 8; this is the thinking version.)</em></p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 200, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "ymhfyZPzOK7OeDB4", "name": "3. Settlements", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>3. Settlements</h2>\n<p>Every settlement, hamlet to capital, gets one card:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Size</strong> \u2014 Hamlet / Village / Town / City.</li>\n<li><strong>Provision</strong> \u2014 food security, rated Starving / Lean / Fed / Full / Overflowing. <em>The single most political number in the game</em> (Chapter 5). Harvest Rolls (Chapter 8), hunting yields (Chapter 7), and trade move it; famine cascades start when it drops.</li>\n<li><strong>Draft</strong> \u2014 the plow-teams and pack beasts the fields depend on, on a Scarce / Stable / Abundant dial. Optional but load-bearing: Chapter 8&rsquo;s Harvest Roll reads it, war and the Murrain move it, and <em>eating it</em> is the famine spiral&rsquo;s favorite bad decision (the Ox-Paradox, Chapter 8).</li>\n<li><strong>Power</strong> \u2014 who actually rules, which is a different question from who wears the chain.</li>\n<li><strong>Trouble</strong> \u2014 what&rsquo;s wrong <em>now</em>, player-facing on arrival.</li>\n<li><strong>Secret</strong> \u2014 what&rsquo;s wrong <em>underneath</em>, findable.</li>\n<li><strong>Three NPCs</strong> \u2014 built on the card in Section 10; at least one with a Mask and Truth (Chapter 2) in towns and larger.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Prosperity is Provision plus trade minus fear: a Fed village on a guarded road grows; a Full one beside a Thin Place empties anyway.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 300, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "OtswCFeY1jAFwLMC", "name": "4. Ruins and Dungeons", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>4. Ruins and Dungeons</h2>\n<p>The Dying Age is pre-ruined \u2014 the trick is making each ruin an argument, not a container. Five questions:</p>\n<p><strong>What was it?</strong> \u2192 <strong>What ended it?</strong> \u2192 <strong>What moved in?</strong> \u2192 <strong>What remains worth taking?</strong> \u2192 <strong>Why now?</strong> (What just changed \u2014 a collapse, a map sold in Karsh, a Ripple \u2014 that puts it in play this season?)</p>\n<p>The answers should disagree with each other productively: a Vaeli border fort (what it was), ended by plague not war (so the official story is a lie), now housing Tallow Hounds (Chapter 7) fat on the mass grave, holding the garrison&rsquo;s pay chest and their <em>actual</em> dispatches (worth taking twice over), because spring floods just washed the west wall open (why now). That&rsquo;s an adventure with no further prep.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 400, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "umVYXi07LcoUSJMY", "name": "5. Trade Routes", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>5. Trade Routes</h2>\n<p>Routes connect a resource to a need. Each carries two stats: <strong>Volume</strong> (how much moves) and <strong>Risk</strong> (banditry, tolls, terrain, weather). Goods flow, Provision and prices follow, and <em>disruption cascades</em> \u2014 a cut route is a Ripple (Section 11) with a schedule: within a season, the buying end goes Lean, the selling end chokes on surplus, carters go unpaid, and someone offers the players honest work that is secretly one faction&rsquo;s Undermine action against another. The brief&rsquo;s chain \u2014 monster on the road \u2192 hunting stops \u2192 food tightens \u2192 trade shifts \u2192 settlement prosperity moves \u2014 is not flavor text here; it&rsquo;s Sections 3, 5, and 11 executing in order.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 500, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "OsTS60EznLwjgiEc", "name": "6. The Faction Engine", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>6. The Faction Engine</h2>\n<p>Factions are characters at a different scale, and they run on the same dice.</p>\n<p><strong>The faction sheet:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Public Face / True Purpose</strong> \u2014 what it says it is; what it is. These may even match. It&rsquo;s rarer than you&rsquo;d think.</li>\n<li><strong>Five stats, rated 1\u201310:</strong> <strong>Reach</strong> (territory, access, presence), <strong>Wealth</strong>, <strong>Force</strong> (arms and the will to use them), <strong>Guile</strong> (spies, secrets, blackmail), <strong>Zeal</strong> (cohesion and belief).</li>\n<li><strong>Assets</strong> \u2014 concrete holdings: a mercenary company, a granary network, a grimoire, a saint&rsquo;s relic, a spy in a rival&rsquo;s kitchen. Assets grant bonuses, can be seized, and are what players actually interact with.</li>\n<li><strong>Goal Clocks</strong> \u2014 each goal is a 4-, 6-, or 8-segment clock. Filled clock: the world changes.</li>\n<li><strong>Leadership</strong> \u2014 named NPCs (Section 10); majors get Masks and Truths (Chapter 2).</li>\n<li><strong>Allies / Enemies</strong>, <strong>Recruitment</strong> (who joins, and what hole in their life the faction fills), <strong>Fault Line</strong> (the internal conflict \u2014 every faction ships with one), <strong>Secret</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The Faction Turn.</strong> Each season (Chapter 8&rsquo;s Season Turn, step 2), every faction takes one Action: <strong>Expand</strong> (Reach), <strong>Seize</strong> (Force), <strong>Undermine</strong> (Guile), <strong>Recruit</strong> (Zeal), <strong>Fortify</strong> (defensive, any stat), <strong>Research</strong> (Guile or Wealth \u2014 grimoires, formulas, maps), <strong>Court</strong> (diplomacy \u2014 Wealth or Zeal), <strong>Aid</strong> (an ally&rsquo;s action at +10).</p>\n<p>Resolve as a <strong>Faction Reckoning</strong>: d100 against <strong>(stat \u00d7 10)</strong>, opposed by the target&rsquo;s relevant stat when contested, comparing degrees exactly as Chapter 1 \u2014 <em>the whole game runs on one engine, on purpose.</em> <strong>The Clamp and the World&rsquo;s Share apply here too:</strong> cap the effective target at 95 even for a stat of 10, so 96\u201399 always fails and a natural 100 is always a Catastrophe. Even the Combine&rsquo;s gold and the Wyrm-cult&rsquo;s zeal miss one move in twenty and blunder one in a hundred \u2014 dominance buys reliability, never certainty. (Opposed contests still resolve by comparing degrees; the clamp only governs each side&rsquo;s own roll.) Masterstroke: 2 clock segments or an asset gained; Success: 1 segment; Costly Success: 1 segment and a cost (an asset exposed, a stat strained \u22121 for a turn); Failure: nothing, and rivals notice; Catastrophe: an asset lost, a secret out, or the Fault Line splits open.</p>\n<p><strong>Players in the machine:</strong> player actions can add or erase clock segments, destroy or deliver assets, or make the party itself an Asset on somebody&rsquo;s sheet \u2014 usually before the party realizes it.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 600, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "GHn2V9x0dnRHNIDM", "name": "7. Faction Types", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>7. Faction Types</h2>\n<p>Quick frames \u2014 which stats they lean on, and a sample asset:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type</th>\n<th>Leans on</th>\n<th>Sample asset</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Religion</td>\n<td>Zeal, Reach</td>\n<td>A pilgrimage route and every inn on it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Noble house</td>\n<td>Force, Wealth</td>\n<td>The only bridge for forty miles</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Merchant guild</td>\n<td>Wealth, Guile</td>\n<td>The grain ledgers of three kingdoms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Criminal organization</td>\n<td>Guile, Reach</td>\n<td>Every dock-tally clerk in Karsh</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Military order</td>\n<td>Force, Zeal</td>\n<td>A fortress-monastery and its armory</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Secret society</td>\n<td>Guile, Zeal</td>\n<td>A membership list nobody knows exists \u2014 including most members</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>The Rig.</strong> The Dying Age&rsquo;s institutions are not neutral terrain \u2014 they were built by their winners, for their winners, and the game says so out loud. In any <em>formal</em> contest \u2014 a court, a toll dispute, a guild bench, a tithe assessment, a witch-trial \u2014 <strong>the side with the greater institutional standing takes +20</strong> (the local Power, the chartered guild, the Office with the writ). <strong>Leverage cancels the Rig:</strong> hard evidence, a patron of equal standing, or a crowd the institution genuinely fears. This is not a house rule about unfairness; it is the setting&rsquo;s load-bearing injustice, priced and visible \u2014 which means players can <em>see</em> it, route around it, buy into it, or spend a campaign prying one piece of it loose. All three worked factions below are, among other things, Rigs with names.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 700, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "OynGxA3vRO5j2y1p", "name": "8. Cults in Full", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>8. Cults in Full</h2>\n<p>A cult is a machine for converting despair into Zeal. Build every cult on nine parts:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Belief</strong> \u2014 the one claim that explains everything (&ldquo;the world&rsquo;s death is a mercy interrupted&rdquo;).</li>\n<li><strong>Doctrine</strong> \u2014 what the Belief demands daily: practices, prohibitions, tithes.</li>\n<li><strong>Initiation</strong> \u2014 graded like Rites (Chapter 4), because for casting cults it literally is: each rank&rsquo;s initiation exposes the member to one Grade higher.</li>\n<li><strong>Symbols</strong> \u2014 a sign for members, a mark for meeting places, a phrase for strangers.</li>\n<li><strong>Ranks</strong> \u2014 a five-step ladder; each step costs more and reveals more, in that order.</li>\n<li><strong>Rituals</strong> \u2014 for practicing cults, this is Chapter 4&rsquo;s group-ritual arithmetic weaponized: five Novice assistants make a mediocre Cantor cast like an Adept, and share the Resolve bill. <em>This is why cults out-cast lone sorcerers, and why the Pale Flame fears congregations more than hedge-witches.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Recruitment</strong> \u2014 the vectors: grief, debt, famine (Provision drops are recruiting seasons \u2014 the cascade is load-bearing), exile, and the specific loneliness of people who know something true that no one believes.</li>\n<li><strong>Forbidden Knowledge</strong> \u2014 the Weighted text or terrible fact at the center (Chapter 4), doled out by rank.</li>\n<li><strong>Fracture</strong> \u2014 the built-in internal disagreement. Cults are arguments wearing robes; know the argument.</li>\n</ol>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 800, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "nffQR95FCX8YXrWp", "name": "9. Three Worked Factions", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>9. Three Worked Factions</h2>\n<h3>The Office of the Pale Flame <em>(military order / church tribunal)</em></h3>\n<p><strong>Public Face:</strong> the realm&rsquo;s shield against sorcery \u2014 licensed witch-finders, paid by the head, welcome at every frightened village. <strong>True Purpose:</strong> monopoly, not extermination. The Office burns <em>practitioners</em> and files <em>practices</em>: every confiscated grimoire is recorded as destroyed and shelved in the Deep Archive. They are, by holdings, the greatest occult library in the Reach.\n<strong>Stats:</strong> Reach 7, Wealth 5, Force 6, Guile 6, Zeal 7. <strong>Assets:</strong> the Deep Archive; writ of tribunal in Vael and the League; the <em>Candle Roll</em> (a census of suspected casters, updated by paid informers). <strong>Goal Clocks:</strong> <em>Locate the Choir&rsquo;s Doorwright</em> (6); <em>Legal standing in Karsh</em> (8 \u2014 the Free Port keeps declining, because the grey market is the Free Port). <strong>Fault Line:</strong> the <strong>Censers</strong> (true believers who want the Archive burned) versus the <strong>Readers</strong> (who run it, use it, and are two bad decades from becoming what they hunt). <strong>Secret:</strong> at least one Reader is Ash-Branded, and the Candle Roll doesn&rsquo;t have a page for that.</p>\n<h3>The Saltmark Combine <em>(merchant guild)</em></h3>\n<p><strong>Public Face:</strong> a respectable freight-and-factor league \u2014 salt, grain, and credit, the boring backbone of civilization. <strong>True Purpose:</strong> to corner Provision itself. Whoever holds the grain when the Harvest Roll fails (Chapter 8) doesn&rsquo;t need a crown; crowns come to them. Vael already owes two harvests.\n<strong>Stats:</strong> Reach 8, Wealth 9, Force 3, Guile 7, Zeal 3. <strong>Assets:</strong> granary network across three polities; the Vaeli crown&rsquo;s debt; a tame League customs bench. <strong>Goal Clocks:</strong> <em>Grain monopoly on the middle Cindral</em> (8); <em>A famine somewhere useful</em> (6 \u2014 they don&rsquo;t cause them; they <em>position</em> for them, which the ledgers insist is different). <strong>Fault Line:</strong> the old factor houses (patient, generational) versus the new shareholders (who&rsquo;ve noticed a famine could be arranged). <strong>Secret:</strong> a junior factor has been selling route schedules to river pirates \u2014 the Combine&rsquo;s own Undermine actions, privatized.</p>\n<h3>The Choir of the Last Door <em>(cult \u2014 full anatomy)</em></h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Belief:</strong> the old age&rsquo;s death was a mercy left unfinished. The Seam is not a wound; it is a Door ajar. Something merciful waits to be <em>let through</em> \u2014 or let out.</li>\n<li><strong>Doctrine:</strong> grieve openly; own nothing that outlives you; learn one verse of the Hollow Song per rank; ease the dying (the Choir&rsquo;s public works are genuinely gentle hospices, which is the horror of it).</li>\n<li><strong>Initiation:</strong> graded I\u2013IV. The Bereft witness a Grade I working; Listeners assist a Grade II; each step up the ladder is a step up Chapter 4&rsquo;s Grade table, Ash and all.</li>\n<li><strong>Symbols:</strong> an unlatched-door glyph; grey thread on the left wrist; the greeting <em>&ldquo;the house is nearly empty.&rdquo;</em></li>\n<li><strong>Ranks:</strong> the Bereft \u2192 Listeners \u2192 Cantors \u2192 the Keyed \u2192 the <strong>Doorwright</strong> (singular, hunted, currently somewhere in the Ashen March).</li>\n<li><strong>Rituals:</strong> congregation-scale Hollow Song castings \u2014 the group-ritual math means forty grieving Bereft can help a single Keyed attempt Grade IV work. The Pale Flame&rsquo;s nightmares are arithmetic.</li>\n<li><strong>Recruitment:</strong> hospices, plague roads, the pews of every village whose Provision just dropped. They recruit the <em>bereaved</em>, and the Dying Age manufactures those at scale.</li>\n<li><strong>Forbidden Knowledge:</strong> <em>The Last Door</em> itself \u2014 a Weight 2 text (Chapter 4). The Keyed have read fragments. Only the Doorwright has read the ending.</li>\n<li><strong>Fracture \u2014 the beautiful one:</strong> the founder&rsquo;s True Purpose was to <em>guard</em> the Door \u2014 the Choir began as wardens, easing grief so despair couldn&rsquo;t be harvested to open it. Doctrine drifted. The current Doorwright wants it <em>open</em>. A minority of the Keyed suspect the founding truth, and the Office&rsquo;s <em>Locate the Doorwright</em> clock might, filled, save the world \u2014 or hand the Readers the address of a Grade IV text.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Stats:</strong> Reach 4, Wealth 2, Force 2, Guile 8, Zeal 10. <strong>Assets:</strong> eleven hospices; the fragments; the grief of a bad decade.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 900, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "nyFiwDH9Rz56Z4mn", "name": "10. NPCs That Act", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>10. NPCs That Act</h2>\n<p><strong>The card</strong> \u2014 five lines make anyone playable: <strong>Want</strong> (concrete, pursuable) \u00b7 <strong>Fear</strong> (specific, exploitable) \u00b7 <strong>Bond</strong> (one person or place they&rsquo;d bleed for) \u00b7 <strong>Secret</strong> (with stakes, as Chapter 2) \u00b7 <strong>Philosophy</strong> (one sentence they&rsquo;d actually say). Major NPCs add a <strong>Mask and Truth</strong> (Chapter 2 \u2014 the archetypes were always for NPCs too).</p>\n<p><strong>Political NPCs get four layers.</strong> Anyone who holds power holds it in layers, and the layers rarely agree: <strong>Office</strong> (the public role and its duties \u2014 what the reeve <em>must</em> do), <strong>Obligation</strong> (the institution or lord they actually answer to \u2014 what the reeve <em>owes</em>), <strong>Want</strong> (the personal ambition underneath \u2014 what the reeve is <em>for</em>), and <strong>Patron</strong> (the hidden allegiance, debt, or blackmail \u2014 who the reeve <em>belongs to</em>, which may surprise even the reeve). Write all four; make at least two pull in different directions. The gaps between layers are where players get leverage \u2014 and a <strong>crisis is the tool that exposes them</strong>: when the Harvest fails or the writ arrives, which layer does this person obey? Answer that once and the NPC will run themselves for a campaign.</p>\n<p><strong>Regard</strong> \u2014 the opinion track: Hostile \u2192 Cold \u2192 Neutral \u2192 Warm \u2192 Devoted. Moved by <em>deeds and costs</em>, not charm alone: Persuasion wins a scene; paying a real price moves Regard. Shared drink helps (Chapter 5); shared danger helps more; being lied to and finding out moves it two steps the wrong way.</p>\n<p><strong>Agendas</strong> \u2014 the &ldquo;NPCs keep acting&rdquo; rule, made mechanical: every named NPC carries a personal clock (their Want, in 4\u20136 segments) that advances <strong>one segment each Season Turn the players don&rsquo;t interact with it</strong>. Check the clocks each season; announce the world&rsquo;s movement through rumor (Chapter 8). The quartermaster Cobb is calling in favors whether or not Yorna writes back. That blood-debt family from her Bonds? Their clock has been ticking since Chapter 2.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 1000, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "l3rt7yMrsnNFHErl", "name": "11. The Ripple Engine", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>11. The Ripple Engine</h2>\n<p>The living world&rsquo;s core rule. When a <strong>Significant Event</strong> occurs \u2014 a keystone creature killed, a harvest failed, an artifact surfacing, a route cut, a leader dead \u2014 the GM writes it as a <strong>Ripple</strong>: the event, plus a chain of 3\u20136 consequence links, each naming <em>what changes</em> and <em>who feels it</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Each Season Turn, every active Ripple advances one link.</strong> Players who learn of a Ripple can intercept <em>any link</em> \u2014 that&rsquo;s the sandbox promise: consequences are real, and so is the chance to stand in front of one.</p>\n<p>The brief&rsquo;s three cascades, written as Ripples in this system&rsquo;s terms:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A keystone monster killed</strong> <em>(say, the Vael Wyrm \u2014 Chapter 7)</em>: wyrm dies \u2192 Seam-moth Dial rises toward Abundant (Chapter 7) \u2192 Blight creeps along the middle Cindral; river hunting yields fall \u2192 riverside Provision drops a step \u2192 League tolls fall, Combine grain moves in at famine prices \u2192 Choir hospices open two new houses on the grieving bank. Six links; any one of them is a place the players can stand.</li>\n<li><strong>A failed harvest</strong> <em>(Chapter 8&rsquo;s Harvest Roll comes up bad)</em>: Provision drops \u2192 crime clock starts in the market towns \u2192 Choir recruitment surges; Combine calls Vael&rsquo;s debt \u2192 migration down the east road \u2192 the Vashkari raise escort prices \u2192 two polities blame each other, and a border incident goes looking for a spark.</li>\n<li><strong>A magical artifact discovered</strong> <em>(a grimoire out of the Ashen March)</em>: rumor reaches Karsh&rsquo;s grey market \u2192 Pale Flame <em>Research</em> and Combine <em>Seize</em> actions converge on the seller \u2192 the Choir sends a Listener who genuinely just wants to <em>read</em> it \u2192 whoever wins the auction gains an Asset; everyone else gains an enemy; the regional balance tilts one faction-stat&rsquo;s worth \u2014 exactly the brief&rsquo;s promise, running on the turn structure.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 1100, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "iXaA5GxNCjmdwfaG", "name": "12. A Faction Turn, Worked", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>12. A Faction Turn, Worked</h2>\n<p><em>Autumn, middle Cindral. Three actions from the season&rsquo;s docket:</em></p>\n<p><strong>The Pale Flame \u2014 Research</strong> (Guile 6, vs the Choir&rsquo;s Guile 8: \u221220): tracing hospice supply carts toward the Doorwright. Rolls 71 vs effective 40 \u2014 <strong>Failure</strong>, and rivals notice: a Listener spots the informer, and the Choir now knows the Office is close. (The GM starts a Choir counter-clock: <em>Move the Doorwright</em>, 4 segments.)</p>\n<p><strong>The Saltmark Combine \u2014 Court</strong> (Wealth 9): pressing Vael to secure next year&rsquo;s grain against the debt. Rolls 34 vs 90 \u2014 <strong>Success</strong>, 1 segment on <em>Grain monopoly</em>. A rumor for the players&rsquo; next tavern: <em>&ldquo;Crown&rsquo;s sold the harvest before it&rsquo;s sown.&rdquo;</em></p>\n<p><strong>The Choir \u2014 Recruit</strong> (Zeal 10): the flood-struck villages downriver. Rolls 07 \u2014 <strong>Masterstroke</strong> (1\u201320 band): two segments, and a new Asset \u2014 a hospice in a town the players know. Grey threads start appearing on wrists at the market.</p>\n<p>Three rolls, four new facts, and next season&rsquo;s problems \u2014 none of which required the players, all of which will find them.</p>"}, "sort": 1200, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}], "folder": null, "sort": 800, "ownership": {"default": 0}, "flags": {}}
{"_id": "KT8PCm6qpdrO9ASs", "name": "Chapter 7 \u2014 Bestiary and Ecology", "pages": [{"_id": "hXiyaahu1IK2XA75", "name": "Overview", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h1>CHAPTER 7: BESTIARY AND ECOLOGY</h1>\n<p><em>Nothing here is only a monster. It is somebody&rsquo;s predator, somebody&rsquo;s dinner, and somebody&rsquo;s god \u2014 and the trouble starts the day one of those stops being true.</em></p>\n<p>Every creature here exists in a web: kill a thing and the world reorganizes around the hole.</p>\n<p><strong>Contents:</strong> 1. Reading a Creature \u00b7 2. The Ecology Block and Population Dials \u00b7 3. Harvesting \u00b7 4. The Bestiary (nine entries) \u00b7 5. Building New Creatures</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 0, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "LCmQwrvwk6RQ1Kjr", "name": "1. Reading a Creature", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>1. Reading a Creature</h2>\n<p>Stat blocks use player-facing math throughout:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Attributes</strong> as normal; <strong>Health</strong>, <strong>Wound Threshold</strong>, <strong>Defense Rating</strong> derived per Chapters 1 and 3. Natural armor lists a single <strong>AV</strong>; where the fiction hides a weak point, spending an Action to aim for it lowers the AV for that strike.</li>\n<li><strong>Attacks</strong> carry a <strong>damage class</strong> (Blade for claws and talons, Piercing for bites, horns, and stings, Crushing for slams, hooves, and tails) \u2014 monster Grievous Wounds use Chapter 3&rsquo;s critical tables like everyone else&rsquo;s.</li>\n<li><strong>Fear</strong> is the Composure modifier for facing it (Chapter 3). <strong>Ash</strong> lists corruption exposure, if any (Chapter 4). <strong>Morale</strong> rates the will to keep fighting; wild animals are usually Wavering \u2014 <em>most animal encounters end in retreat, not death, and hunters know it.</em></li>\n<li><strong>Moves</strong> are the creature&rsquo;s Techniques \u2014 options on its Masterstrokes or at listed costs.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The Dread rule \u2014 how one monster fights four heroes.</strong> A lone creature marked <strong>Dread</strong> in its entry (the great and the unnatural \u2014 the Tuskbeast, the Weeping Saint, the Vael Wyrm, the Hollowed&rsquo;s worst) faces a problem the fiction shouldn&rsquo;t allow: four heroes throw eight-plus actions and reactions a round, and would bury it under sheer numbers. A Dread creature answers this two ways at once. First, <strong>resilience:</strong> it takes a Grievous Wound only on net damage of <strong>2\u00bd\u00d7 its Wound Threshold</strong> (not the usual 2\u00d7), so a lucky party can&rsquo;t simply burst it down before it acts. Second, <strong>presence:</strong> whenever a player scores a <strong>Costly Success</strong> against it, the creature immediately takes a free Reaction \u2014 a Move, a reposition, or a bare Strike (no more than once per player per round). Its own signature results feed it.</p>\n<p>But the deadliest thing about a Dread creature is not its action count \u2014 it is that a single blow from something this size can erase a lesser fighter outright. Read its damage honestly before you run it: against unarmored or lightly-armored PCs, a Dread creature&rsquo;s attacks are written to <strong>wound and terrify, not to one-shot</strong> (Chapter 3&rsquo;s rule that no single blow fells a healthy character catches the rest). If your table wants a given monster to feel truly apocalyptic, raise its damage knowingly and tell the players what they&rsquo;re looking at \u2014 a saga-beast should be a decision, not an ambush. <em>(For merely tough foes, don&rsquo;t use any of this \u2014 give them numbers instead. Dread is for the thing a saga is named after.)</em></p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 100, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "7FuOGUMbDrFd5C3w", "name": "2. The Ecology Block and Population Dials", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>2. The Ecology Block and Population Dials</h2>\n<p>Every entry carries an <strong>Ecology Block</strong>: <em>Role</em> (apex, mesopredator, grazer, scavenger, parasite, keystone) \u00b7 <em>Prey</em> \u00b7 <em>Predators</em> \u00b7 <em>Range &amp; Season</em> \u00b7 <em>Breeding</em> \u00b7 and the two lines that run the living world:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>If Removed</strong> \u2014 what happens when the population crashes.</li>\n<li><strong>If Overabundant</strong> \u2014 what happens when nothing checks it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Population Dials.</strong> Each region tracks its keystone species on a three-step dial: <strong>Scarce \u2013 Stable \u2013 Abundant.</strong> Dials move at the Season Turn (Chapter 8) from hunting pressure, predator loss, weather, and Ripples \u2014 and moving a dial <em>is</em> a Significant Event: it triggers the species&rsquo; If Removed or If Overabundant line as a new Ripple (Chapter 6). This is the whole ecology simulation: three positions per species, checked four times a year, consequences pre-written. Light enough to run, heavy enough to matter.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 200, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "HtTF28m51X9GE62A", "name": "3. Harvesting", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>3. Harvesting</h2>\n<p>A carcass is a resource window. <strong>Freshness:</strong> most yields spoil in a day (organs by nightfall) unless preserved (Chapter 5&rsquo;s methods) \u2014 field decisions are real decisions.</p>\n<p>Harvesting is a <strong>Hunting Reckoning</strong> (field-dressing) or <strong>Medicine Reckoning</strong> (fine anatomical work \u2014 glands, organs, anything marked <em>delicate</em>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Masterstroke:</strong> +1 yield, or one delicate part taken intact regardless of listing.</li>\n<li><strong>Success:</strong> listed yields.</li>\n<li><strong>Costly Success:</strong> yields, but one part spoiled \u2014 or the knife slips: for venomous or Seam-touched carcasses, the harvester takes the listed Toxicity or Ash.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure:</strong> half yields, roughly butchered.</li>\n<li><strong>Catastrophe:</strong> the carcass is ruined \u2014 or it wasn&rsquo;t quite a carcass.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Yields are Chapter 5 ingredients and materials, properties included. <strong>Seam-touched parts are marked</strong> \u2014 they carry Corruption into pot, flask, or forge exactly as Chapters 4\u20135 warn.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 300, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "TCKmbjj0Ei5sOZTM", "name": "4. The Bestiary", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>4. The Bestiary</h2>\n<hr />\n<h3>GREY WOLVES <em>(pack predator \u2014 the baseline, and the brief&rsquo;s own example)</em></h3>\n<p>Lean winter shadows; a pack is six to a dozen, smarter together than apart.</p>\n<p><strong>Might 11 \u00b7 Agility 13 \u00b7 Endurance 12 \u00b7 Intellect 4 \u00b7 Willpower 10 \u00b7 Presence 8</strong> \u2014 Health 22, Threshold 6, Defense 13. <strong>Bite</strong> 1d6+1 (Piercing). <strong>Fear</strong> \u2014 (0; the <em>pack at night</em> is Fear \u221210). <strong>Morale</strong> Wavering 30 \u2014 they test the weak and leave the strong. <strong>Moves:</strong> <em>Harry</em> \u2014 wolves flank in pairs; a target engaged by two or more wolves loses the Defense Rating modifier against them.</p>\n<p><strong>Ecology.</strong> Role: mesopredator, near-keystone. Prey: deer, marsh aurochs calves, stragglers. Predators: the Vael Wyrm, winter. Range: forest and hill margins; bolder in famine years. <strong>If Removed:</strong> deer and aurochs Dials rise toward Abundant within two seasons \u2192 crop damage \u2192 farm Provision drops \u2192 <em>famine risk</em> \u2014 the brief&rsquo;s cascade, on rails. <strong>If Overabundant:</strong> livestock losses, road fear, bounties posted \u2014 and bounties draw hunters, which is an adventure economy.</p>\n<p><strong>Harvest:</strong> pelt (winter coat: Insulation-grade for Leatherworking), 4 rations of gamey meat (Vitality 1), teeth (charms; Highland debt-tokens). <strong>Cooking:</strong> stew only; smoke it and it&rsquo;s marching food nobody brags about. <strong>Cultural impact:</strong> the Thornwold treats a wolf-killing without cause as promise-breaking. Vael pays two silver a tail. Both are ecology policy; neither knows it.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>MARSH AUROCHS <em>(grazer \u2014 the walking larder)</em></h3>\n<p>Shoulder-tall at a man&rsquo;s head, horns like a yoke, temper like a landslide when calving.</p>\n<p><strong>Might 17 \u00b7 Agility 8 \u00b7 Endurance 16 \u00b7 Intellect 3 \u00b7 Willpower 11 \u00b7 Presence 10</strong> \u2014 Health 26, Threshold 8, Defense 8; hide AV 2. <strong>Gore</strong> 1d10+5 (Piercing) \u00b7 <strong>Trample</strong> 1d8+5 (Crushing, vs prone or Off-Balance foes). <strong>Fear</strong> \u2014 (herd stampede: Fear \u221210). <strong>Morale</strong> Steady 50 defending calves; otherwise they simply <em>leave</em>, through whatever&rsquo;s in the way. <strong>Moves:</strong> <em>The Wall Turns</em> \u2014 a threatened herd forms horns-out; charging it is a Composure Reckoning first.</p>\n<p><strong>Ecology.</strong> Role: keystone grazer. Prey of: wolves, wyrm, and every settlement within a day&rsquo;s cart. Range: river marsh and wet pasture; they <em>make</em> the meadowland by grazing it. <strong>If Removed:</strong> marsh scrubs over in two years \u2014 grazing economy collapses, hunting thins, riverside Provision drops a step. <strong>If Overabundant:</strong> trampled plantings, and the herds draw wolves right through the villages.</p>\n<p><strong>Harvest:</strong> 20+ rations (Vitality 1 each), <strong>aurochs heart</strong> (Vitality 2, Energy 1 \u2014 Chapter 5&rsquo;s database), hide (bark-tans toward Fine), horn and bone (Carpentry fittings), marrowfat (Vitality 2). <strong>Cooking:</strong> the Steppe&rsquo;s wind-dried heart is a campaign in a saddlebag; the whole Vashkar Grass runs on this animal. <strong>Cultural impact:</strong> Vashkari wealth is counted in aurochs. Moving the herds <em>is</em> moving the nation \u2014 which is why something shifting their routes (Chapter 6) is a crisis, not a curiosity.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>BRISTLEBACK <em>(armored beast \u2014 the hunter&rsquo;s economy \u00b7 Dread)</em></h3>\n<p>A boar the size of a cart, plated in horn-ridge scales from crown to haunch, with a temperament to match the armor.</p>\n<p><strong>Might 16 \u00b7 Agility 10 \u00b7 Endurance 15 \u00b7 Intellect 3 \u00b7 Willpower 12 \u00b7 Presence 7</strong> \u2014 Health 25, Threshold 8, Defense 10; plate AV 4 \u2014 but a foe who spends an Action to <em>aim for the belly</em> (the weakness every huntsman teaches first) strikes at AV 1 that turn. <strong>Tusks</strong> 1d10+6 (Piercing) \u00b7 <strong>Slam</strong> 1d8+6 (Crushing). <strong>Fear</strong> \u221210 (the charge). <strong>Morale</strong> Hard 70 \u2014 it does not read the situation; it <em>is</em> the situation. <strong>Moves:</strong> <em>Bulldoze</em> \u2014 its charge forces an Athletics Reckoning or prone; <em>Enrage</em> \u2014 first Grievous Wound it takes: +2 damage, \u221210 to its own attacks, and it stops retreating ever.</p>\n<p><strong>Ecology.</strong> Role: mesograzer/rooter. Prey: roots, mast, crops given half a chance. Predators: the wyrm; humans, professionally. <strong>If Removed:</strong> oak-mast rots ungrazed, truffle grounds go fallow, and the leather trade loses its Superior tier. <strong>If Overabundant:</strong> fields ruined faster than wolves ever managed; farm Provision drops while <em>hunter</em> income rises \u2014 a Ripple with two winners.</p>\n<p><strong>Harvest:</strong> <strong>bristleback loin</strong> (Vitality 2, Heat 1), plate hide (Leatherworking to <em>Superior</em>, keeps its ridges \u2014 Chapter 5&rsquo;s payoff), tusks (Fine carving stock), rendered fat (cooking base; lamp-oil). <strong>Cooking:</strong> the loin is Petra&rsquo;s stew (Chapter 5) and half the Reach&rsquo;s feast-days. <strong>Cultural impact:</strong> a first bristleback kill is a Heartlands rite of adulthood; the plates of a famous one get names.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>TALLOW HOUND <em>(corpse-fed pack horror)</em></h3>\n<p>Dog-shaped at a distance. Closer, the proportions are wrong \u2014 rendered-looking, waxy, patient. They follow plague and war at exactly the distance grief stops watching the treeline.</p>\n<p><strong>Might 12 \u00b7 Agility 12 \u00b7 Endurance 13 \u00b7 Intellect 5 \u00b7 Willpower 12 \u00b7 Presence 4</strong> \u2014 Health 23, Threshold 7, Defense 12. <strong>Bite</strong> 1d6+2 (Piercing) \u00b7 <strong>Waxen Grip</strong> (grapple; Grip&rsquo;d victims take the bite automatically each round). <strong>Fear \u221210</strong> (a pack in lamplight: \u221220). <strong>Ash:</strong> none to fight; 1 to <em>eat</em> (see harvest). <strong>Morale</strong> Wavering 30 \u2014 <strong>but Hard 70 anywhere the dead outnumber the living.</strong> <strong>Weakness:</strong> fire \u2014 Heat damage against them ignores their Threshold for Grievous purposes; they will not cross open flame. <strong>Moves:</strong> <em>Grave-Breath</em> \u2014 once per fight, a gout of chill rot: everyone at Reach, Endurance Reckoning or contract <strong>Grave-Chill</strong> (Chapter 4&rsquo;s disease, exactly).</p>\n<p><strong>Ecology.</strong> Role: scavenger \u2014 the Seam&rsquo;s sanitation. Prey: carrion, then the bereaved who tend it. Predators: none that want the meat; fire; the Pale Flame&rsquo;s crematory doctrine. Range: battlefields, plague roads, the Ashen March; they <em>migrate along death</em>, which makes their movements a map of the Reach&rsquo;s misery \u2014 Vashkari scouts read hound-sign the way sailors read weather. <strong>If Removed:</strong> carrion accumulates; ordinary disease Ripples worsen \u2014 <em>the horror was doing a job.</em> <strong>If Overabundant:</strong> graveyards open from beneath, Grave-Chill clusters, and villages start burning their dead, which breaks three religions&rsquo; rites at once (Chapter 6 tension, delivered).</p>\n<p><strong>Harvest:</strong> <strong>tallow-hound fat</strong> (Heat 1, Corruption 2 \u2014 Chapter 5&rsquo;s marked entry; renders into candles that burn in wind and <em>should not be read by</em>), hide (Crude \u2014 it never tans right). <strong>Cooking:</strong> legally, no. Famine years disagree, and the Ash bill (2 per portion) comes due in Brands. <strong>Cultural impact:</strong> &ldquo;hound-weather&rdquo; means a bad season coming. Candlemakers who buy the fat don&rsquo;t ask; buyers who know, pay more.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>SEAM-MOTHS <em>(swarm \u2014 Thin Place fauna)</em></h3>\n<p>Grey-white moths, wingspans like two spread hands, drawn to the Seam&rsquo;s leakage as their mundane cousins are drawn to lanterns. A Thin Place (Chapter 4) wears its moth-cloud like a signature.</p>\n<p><strong>Swarm:</strong> Health 18, Threshold \u2014, Defense 14; weapons deal 1 damage max (fire and smoke deal full). <strong>Engulf</strong> \u2014 a creature inside the swarm takes \u221210 to everything and <strong>1 Ash per scene engulfed</strong>. <strong>Fear</strong> \u221210 (it&rsquo;s the <em>silence</em> of them). <strong>Morale</strong> \u2014 (disperse at 0 Health or in strong wind/smoke).</p>\n<p><strong>Ecology.</strong> Role: <strong>the Blight&rsquo;s pollinators</strong> \u2014 they carry Seam-residue as bees carry pollen; where they nest, pale fruit and ashen honey follow (Chapter 5&rsquo;s Seam-touched larder exists <em>because of them</em>). Prey of: <strong>the Vael Wyrm</strong>, which strains them from the air in bulk \u2014 the single load-bearing fact of the middle Cindral&rsquo;s ecology. <strong>If Removed:</strong> the Seam-touched harvest fails \u2014 and the strange upside: local Ash exposure actually drops. <strong>If Overabundant:</strong> Blight creep \u2014 Thin Places widen, Ashfall weather (Chapter 8) grows likelier, and the March takes another field.</p>\n<p><strong>Harvest:</strong> <strong>seam-moth dust</strong> (Spirit 2, Corruption 2 \u2014 swept from a dispersed swarm, harvester&rsquo;s Ash risk on a Costly Success), <strong>ashen honey</strong> from their nest-warrens (Regeneration 2, Corruption 1). <strong>Cooking:</strong> the honey is the Reach&rsquo;s most seductive bad idea \u2014 a wound-knitting sweetness with a Brand at the bottom of the jar. <strong>Cultural impact:</strong> March folk read moth-clouds like weather. The Choir considers them holy. The Pale Flame burns the warrens, which works about as well as burning bees.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>THE HOLLOWED <em>(what Chapter 4 promised \u00b7 Dread)</em></h3>\n<p>When a caster&rsquo;s Brands reach the Hollowing line, this walks away wearing their face: unhurried, symmetrical, wrong. Each Hollowed keeps <strong>one Brand-echo</strong> from the person it emptied \u2014 beast-eyes, a doubled voice, a shadow that arrives first.</p>\n<p><strong>Might 13 \u00b7 Agility 11 \u00b7 Endurance 14 \u00b7 Intellect 9 \u00b7 Willpower 16 \u00b7 Presence 6</strong> \u2014 Health 24, Threshold 7, Defense 11. <strong>Touch of the Seam</strong> 1d6+3 (class matches its Brand-echo) <strong>plus 1 Ash on any Grievous Wound it inflicts.</strong> <strong>Fear \u221220</strong> \u2014 worse for anyone who knew them (<strong>\u221240</strong>, and the Aftermath rules apply, Chapter 3). <strong>Morale</strong> Fanatic 90 \u2014 nothing is home to be afraid. <strong>Weakness \u2014 the name they lost:</strong> speaking the person&rsquo;s true name to it forces a pause: it takes no Action for one round, <em>listening</em>. Once per scene. It is not enough. It is exactly enough to run.</p>\n<p><strong>Ecology.</strong> Role: the Seam&rsquo;s finished work \u2014 not a species but a <em>symptom</em>; each is unique. They drift toward Thin Places and toward whoever grieves them, in that order. <strong>If Removed:</strong> mourned, quietly, twice. <strong>If &ldquo;Overabundant&rdquo;:</strong> three Hollowed in one region means a cult has been ambitious or a Thin Place has widened \u2014 either is a Ripple with a faction&rsquo;s name on it.</p>\n<p><strong>Harvest:</strong> nothing sane. The Deep Archive pays for verified Brand-echo tissue (Weight 1 to even study \u2014 Chapter 4). <strong>Cultural impact:</strong> every culture has a rite for a Hollowed relative, and none of the rites work, and everyone performs them anyway. The Choir calls them <em>the Answered</em>. That sentence alone justifies the Office.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>THE STANDING DEAD <em>(Chapter 4&rsquo;s rite, statted)</em></h3>\n<p>What the Grey Craft raises: tireless, mute, decaying on schedule, obedient to the letter.</p>\n<p><strong>Might 13 \u00b7 Agility 7 \u00b7 Endurance \u2014 \u00b7 Intellect 2 \u00b7 Willpower \u2014 \u00b7 Presence 2</strong> \u2014 Health 20, Threshold 8 (dead flesh doesn&rsquo;t flinch), Defense 7. <strong>Grasp and Rend</strong> 1d6+3 (Crushing). <strong>Immune</strong> to fear, morale, bleeding, poison, and persuasion; <strong>head Grievous Wounds drop them outright.</strong> <strong>Fear \u221210</strong> (\u221220 for anyone who buried this one). <strong>Moves:</strong> <em>The Letter of the Law</em> \u2014 it fulfills orders exactly as worded; every GM is owed one disaster from this per campaign.</p>\n<p><strong>Ecology:</strong> none. The wrongness of it is why Grave-Chill pools where they&rsquo;ve labored (Chapter 4). Where one stands, scavengers won&rsquo;t, crops sicken in a ring, and the Tallow Hounds circle <em>outside</em> the ring, waiting for the lease to end.</p>\n<p><strong>Harvest:</strong> grave-wax scraped from the joints (a Crude candle-stock the Grey Craft prizes; Ash 1 per gathering). <strong>Cultural impact:</strong> one witnessed Standing Dead can empty a village faster than plague \u2014 which the Grey Craft&rsquo;s rare practitioners know, and factor.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>THE WEEPING SAINT <em>(unique \u2014 a region-scale horror \u00b7 Dread)</em></h3>\n<p>Saint Verath healed the Reach&rsquo;s worst plague two centuries back and was entombed upright, as Vaeli saints are. The Blight found her reliquary forty years ago. What stands in the crypt now still heals \u2014 <em>involuntarily, indiscriminately, endlessly</em> \u2014 flesh flowing toward every wound near it like water toward low ground, whether the wound wants closing or not, whoever&rsquo;s flesh answers the call. Pilgrims still come. Some walk out healed. The rest are part of why she weeps.</p>\n<p><strong>Might 18 \u00b7 Agility 6 \u00b7 Endurance 20 \u00b7 Intellect 11 \u00b7 Willpower 18 \u00b7 Presence 15</strong> \u2014 Health 30, Threshold 10, Defense 6; calcified reliquary growth AV 5. <strong>Fear \u221240.</strong> <strong>Ash:</strong> 1 per scene within Reach; her <em>tears</em> are a Grievous-only contact toxin (Fire table \u2014 they cauterize). <strong>Morale</strong> \u2014 (she is not fighting; she is <em>continuing</em>). <strong>Moves:</strong> <em>The Mercy</em> \u2014 once per round, a wound within Near closes whether its owner consents or not: restore a Wound level, and the healed take 1 Ash and a scar of pale new flesh; <em>The Gathering</em> \u2014 anyone Downed within Near begins being <em>incorporated</em>: three rounds to pull them free. <strong>Weakness:</strong> her own rite \u2014 the Vaeli canonization litany, sung true (a Presence-based Reckoning by someone who knows it), holds all her Moves for a round. The Church has the litany. The Church has also spent forty years insisting she&rsquo;s fine.</p>\n<p><strong>Ecology.</strong> Unique. Her crypt-hill is a dead zone ringed by the healthiest wildflowers in the Reach. <strong>If Removed:</strong> the pilgrim economy of three villages collapses; the Church must explain forty years; the Office&rsquo;s Readers want the reliquary <em>studied</em> and the Censers want it burned \u2014 her death is a four-faction Ripple before her body cools. <strong>Harvest:</strong> <strong>saint&rsquo;s tallow</strong> (Regeneration 3, Corruption 2 \u2014 enchanting-grade, Chapter 5) and weeping myrrh (Spirit 2, Corruption 1). <strong>Cultural impact:</strong> she is the Reach&rsquo;s open secret \u2014 the proof that the age&rsquo;s holiest things are not exempt, which is the whole setting in one crypt.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>THE VAEL WYRM <em>(apex keystone \u2014 the brief&rsquo;s dragon, operationalized \u00b7 Dread)</em></h3>\n<p>The river&rsquo;s oldest fact: a drake the length of a grain barge, slate-scaled, patient as sediment. It does not hoard gold. It hoards <em>territory</em>, and its territory is the middle Cindral&rsquo;s health.</p>\n<p><strong>Might 22 \u00b7 Agility 12 \u00b7 Endurance 21 \u00b7 Intellect 10 \u00b7 Willpower 17 \u00b7 Presence 16</strong> \u2014 Health 31, Threshold 11, Defense 12; scale AV 6 \u2014 but a foe who spends an Action to <em>aim for the throat-seam or eye</em> (known to those who&rsquo;ve read the old hunting songs) strikes at AV 2 that turn. <strong>Bite</strong> 2d8+8 (Piercing) \u00b7 <strong>Tail</strong> 2d6+8 (Crushing, all at Reach behind it) \u00b7 <strong>Drowning Rush</strong> (it takes fights into the river, where Chapter 3&rsquo;s drowning rules fight for it). <strong>Fear \u221230.</strong> <strong>Morale</strong> Hard 70 \u2014 it has nothing to prove and will leave a fight that bores it, which is somehow worse. <strong>Moves:</strong> <em>Strain the Air</em> \u2014 it inhales a Seam-moth swarm whole (this is <em>feeding</em>, and the tactical note is: it will prioritize swarms over you); <em>The Old Patience</em> \u2014 it takes no Catastrophes; a natural 100 on its Reckonings is merely a Failure. Two centuries teach economy.</p>\n<p><strong>Ecology.</strong> Role: <strong>keystone, twice over</strong> \u2014 apex predator <em>and</em> the Blight&rsquo;s principal check: it eats Seam-moths by the cloud, and the middle Cindral has few Thin Places <em>because it does.</em> Prey: moths, aurochs, bristleback, the occasional barge that didn&rsquo;t pay attention. Predators: none living. Breeding: none observed in living memory \u2014 it may be the last, which every faction has noticed. <strong>If Removed:</strong> the Ripple written in Chapter 6, Section 11 \u2014 moth Dial to Abundant, Blight creep, hunting collapse, Provision drop, Combine grain at famine prices, Choir hospices on the grieving bank. <strong>Killing it is the single largest Significant Event on the map, and the map knows it.</strong> <strong>If Overabundant:</strong> not applicable, and thank the dying gods.</p>\n<p><strong>Harvest</strong> <em>(a Master-tier undertaking; everything delicate):</em> <strong>wyrm liver</strong> (Vitality 3, Memory 1, Corruption 1), heart (the Wyrm-Heart Roast, Chapter 5 \u2014 sagas begin here), heartblood (alchemy&rsquo;s rarest Vitality stock), <strong>wyrm-bone</strong> (Peerless, enchantable \u2014 Chapter 5&rsquo;s Bound Work), a barge-load of AV 6 scale. <strong>Cultural impact:</strong> the League mints its highest coin with the wyrm on the reverse. Vaeli kings are crowned facing the river. Every faction has a plan for its death; only the Thornwold&rsquo;s plan is <em>grief</em>. The party that kills it will be the most famous people in the Reach \u2014 famous the way weather is famous.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 400, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "z2qA5XdY4UvlHLhU", "name": "5. Building New Creatures", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>5. Building New Creatures</h2>\n<p>Start from the ecology, not the stat block: pick the <strong>Role</strong>, write <strong>If Removed / If Overabundant</strong> first \u2014 if those two lines aren&rsquo;t interesting, the creature is set dressing, and that&rsquo;s allowed, but know which you&rsquo;re making. Then: Attributes to fit the fiction, one <strong>damage class</strong> per natural weapon, AV where the fiction armors it, a <strong>weakness a local would know</strong>, Fear by wrongness (0 to \u221240), Ash only if the Seam is in it, one or two <strong>Moves</strong>, and a harvest table written in Chapter 5&rsquo;s nine properties. Full random generators \u2014 role, dread, harvest prize, and an If-Removed template \u2014 are in Chapter 8.</p>"}, "sort": 500, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}], "folder": null, "sort": 900, "ownership": {"default": 0}, "flags": {}}
{"_id": "sotGDHN0iEn7ewPJ", "name": "Chapter 8 \u2014 Game Master Guide", "pages": [{"_id": "mAFpDbU8LtjyY3dF", "name": "Overview", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h1>CHAPTER 8: GAME MASTER GUIDE</h1>\n<p><em>You are not the world&rsquo;s author. You are its weather, its ledgers, its consequences, and its memory. The players will write the story themselves \u2014 your job is to make sure the world was already busy when they arrived.</em></p>\n<p>Everything before this chapter built the machine; this chapter is the operator&rsquo;s manual. The spine is the <strong>Season Turn</strong> \u2014 one procedure that makes weather, factions, NPCs, ecology, and consequences move on schedule. Around it: the climate engine the brief demanded, the promised plague list, and quick-roll generators for every framework Chapters 6 and 7 established.</p>\n<p><strong>Contents:</strong> 1. Principles \u00b7 2. The Season Turn \u00b7 3. Weather and Climate \u00b7 4. The Harvest Roll \u00b7 5. The Agrarian Engine \u00b7 6. The Generators \u00b7 7. The Congregation Engine \u00b7 8. NPCs in One Minute \u00b7 9. The Plague List \u00b7 10. A Cascade, Run Live \u00b7 11. Three Campaign Frames \u00b7 12. The Table Itself \u00b7 Appendices: The Reckoner&rsquo;s Card \u00b7 The Region Sheet \u00b7 The Exchange</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 0, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "OUCDjbmydBwa8WZ6", "name": "1. Principles of the Dying Age", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>1. Principles of the Dying Age</h2>\n<p><strong>The world moves on turns, not on plot.</strong> Run the Season Turn honestly and stories emerge; force a story and the living world becomes scenery.</p>\n<p><strong>Telegraph, then commit.</strong> Brutality is fair when it was visible: hound-sign on the road, the Provision rating falling, the moth-cloud on the horizon. Warn like the world warns. Then let the dice mean what they said.</p>\n<p><strong>Consequences, not punishments.</strong> A Ripple is never revenge \u2014 it&rsquo;s arithmetic. Show the players the links; standing in front of one is the game.</p>\n<p><strong>NPCs are not waiting.</strong> Agendas tick (Chapter 6). When the players return to a town after two seasons, <em>run the missed turns first.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Say what the dice mean.</strong> A Costly Success narrated as a clean win teaches players the costs aren&rsquo;t real. They&rsquo;re the whole game.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 100, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "tBfgVmgFj91cXzPp", "name": "2. The Season Turn", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>2. The Season Turn</h2>\n<p>Run once per in-game season (or monthly, for a tighter clock). Twenty minutes of prep that generates a session&rsquo;s worth of world. In order:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Weather &amp; Season</strong> \u2014 set the season&rsquo;s temperament per region (Section 3); note travel and survival consequences.</li>\n<li><strong>The Harvest Roll</strong> \u2014 autumn only, per agricultural region (Section 4). Adjust Provision.</li>\n<li><strong>Faction Turns</strong> \u2014 every faction takes one Action (Chapter 6); resolve Faction Reckonings; advance Goal Clocks.</li>\n<li><strong>NPC Agendas</strong> \u2014 advance one segment on every named NPC clock the players didn&rsquo;t touch.</li>\n<li><strong>Ecology Dials</strong> \u2014 check keystone species per active region (Chapter 7): hunting pressure, predator loss, weather. <strong>The agrarian dials \u2014 Draft and Woodland \u2014 check here too (Section 5).</strong> Move dials that earned it; a moved dial spawns its Ripple.</li>\n<li><strong>Advance Ripples</strong> \u2014 every active Ripple moves one link. Retire completed ones into standing facts.</li>\n<li><strong>Write the Rumors</strong> \u2014 3\u20135 rumors, each pointing at something that just moved (Section 6 tables help). Rumors are the world&rsquo;s turn signals: this is how players learn where to stand.</li>\n</ol>\n<hr />\n<h3>Quick Reference \u2014 The Season Turn at a Glance</h3>\n<p>Run top to bottom each season, per active region (the Region Sheet, appendix). Roll where a die is shown.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Do this</th>\n<th>Roll / Check</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. Weather</td>\n<td>Set the season&rsquo;s weather</td>\n<td>d10 on the season&rsquo;s Climate row (\u00a73); storms shift it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. Harvest</td>\n<td>(Autumn only) resolve the year&rsquo;s food</td>\n<td>2d10 + mods (\u00a74); moves Provision</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3. Factions</td>\n<td>Each active faction acts on its top Goal</td>\n<td>Faction Reckoning stat\u00d710 (\u00a77); advance a Clock</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4. NPC Agendas</td>\n<td>Tick each named NPC&rsquo;s Agenda clock</td>\n<td>narrate one visible move</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5. Dials</td>\n<td>Check ecology + agrarian dials (Draft, Woodland, Salt)</td>\n<td>move any that earned it \u2192 spawn its Ripple</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6. Ripples</td>\n<td>Advance every active Ripple one link</td>\n<td>resolve any that complete</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7. Rumors</td>\n<td>Write 3\u20135 rumors carrying the news</td>\n<td>these are next session&rsquo;s hooks</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Weather quick-roll (d10, temperate):</strong> 1\u20132 fair \u00b7 3\u20135 grey/overcast \u00b7 6\u20137 rain (travel \u221210) \u00b7 8 storm (travel \u221220, shelter or a Condition) \u00b7 9 hard weather for the season (heat/cold/fog) \u00b7 10 an Ashfall or an omen (GM&rsquo;s choice). Shift the row by season and latitude (\u00a73).</p>\n<h3>Quick Reference \u2014 The Faction Turn</h3>\n<p>For each active faction, once per season. The stat used is whichever fits the move (Reach to expand, Force to fight, Guile to scheme, Wealth to buy, Zeal to convert).</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Do this</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. Goal</td>\n<td>Look at the faction&rsquo;s top unfilled Goal Clock</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. Move</td>\n<td>Pick the obvious action toward it (Expand / Attack / Undermine / Defend / Convert / Acquire)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3. Reckon</td>\n<td>Faction Reckoning: d100 \u2264 (stat \u00d7 10), clamped 5\u201395; opposed if another faction resists</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4. Result</td>\n<td>Masterstroke +2 Clock segments \u00b7 Success +1 \u00b7 Costly +1 but a cost/exposure \u00b7 Failure none \u00b7 Catastrophe a segment <em>back</em> + a scandal</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5. Ripple</td>\n<td>If a Clock fills, fire its consequence as a Ripple; write the rumor</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Faction move (d6)</th>\n<th>The faction is trying to\u2026</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td><strong>Expand</strong> \u2014 take ground, markets, or members (Reach)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td><strong>Attack</strong> \u2014 break a rival by force (Force)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td><strong>Undermine</strong> \u2014 scheme, blackmail, sabotage (Guile)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td><strong>Acquire</strong> \u2014 buy, trade, or seize a resource/relic (Wealth)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td><strong>Convert</strong> \u2014 win hearts, recruit, spread doctrine (Zeal)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td><strong>Defend / Consolidate</strong> \u2014 shore up after a loss, or when threatened</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>The Rig reminder:</strong> in any <em>formal</em> contest between factions (a court, a charter, a tithe), the side with greater institutional standing takes +20 unless the other brings real leverage (\u00a7 Ch. 6).</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 200, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "PlB7CYNMrZiUAUEk", "name": "3. Weather and Climate", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>3. Weather and Climate</h2>\n<p><strong>The Climate Profile</strong> (set once per region, per Chapter 6&rsquo;s region-building): <strong>latitude band</strong> (northern / temperate / southern), <strong>altitude</strong> (lowland / upland / mountain), <strong>humidity</strong> (wet / mixed / dry), <strong>wind exposure</strong> (sheltered / open / coastal). These four choose the column and bias the rolls below.</p>\n<p><strong>Seasonal weather (d10, roll per travel-week or when it matters):</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>d10</th>\n<th>Spring</th>\n<th>Summer</th>\n<th>Autumn</th>\n<th>Winter</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1\u20133</td>\n<td>Rain; roads mire (travel \u221225%)</td>\n<td>Fair and hot</td>\n<td>Fog (visibility Near; ambush checks \u221210)</td>\n<td>Snow (travel \u221250%; Freezing checks in the open)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4\u20136</td>\n<td>Changeable; fords run high</td>\n<td>Heat (Sweltering checks under exertion)</td>\n<td>Cold rain (Freezing risk if soaked)</td>\n<td>Hard frost (Freezing without Insulation)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7\u20138</td>\n<td>Fair</td>\n<td>Storm (no sailing; camps flood on a failed camp roll)</td>\n<td>Fair and cold</td>\n<td>Clear and bitter (\u221220 Endurance checks at night)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Late frost (crop risk \u2014 note for Harvest)</td>\n<td><strong>Drought week</strong> (mark it \u2014 Section 4)</td>\n<td>Gale (coastal shipping halts)</td>\n<td>Blizzard (no travel; Sleepless in poor shelter)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>Extreme (roll below)</td>\n<td>Extreme (roll below)</td>\n<td>Extreme (roll below)</td>\n<td>Extreme (roll below)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Extremes (d6):</strong> 1 flood (a route cut \u2014 Ripple) \u00b7 2 wildfire or killing frost by season \u00b7 3 hailstorm (crop damage; note for Harvest) \u00b7 4 windstorm (structures damaged; construction tasks lose Progress) \u00b7 5 unseasonal week (swap season&rsquo;s column) \u00b7 6 <strong>Ashfall storm</strong> \u2014 grey drifts off the Ashen March: everyone caught outside takes 1 Ash (Chapter 4), Seam-moth Dials tick upward, and the March is one field wider. Adjacent-to-March regions treat a 5\u20136 on this table as Ashfall.</p>\n<p>Weather is a combatant: apply its penalties to Reckonings, its conditions to travelers, and its marks to the Harvest Roll \u2014 it is the one faction that never misses its turn.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 300, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "wXuD7215Q9UEaTO1", "name": "4. The Harvest Roll", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>4. The Harvest Roll</h2>\n<p>Each autumn, per agricultural region: <strong>2d10</strong>, modified by the year&rsquo;s marks \u2014 drought weeks \u22122 each, flood or hail \u22122, late frost \u22121, a good irrigation or labor project (player-built things count \u2014 Chapter 5 construction) +2, war fought over the fields \u22123, <strong>Draft Scarce \u22123 (no teams at all: \u22125, spade-work only), Draft Abundant +1</strong> (Section 5).</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Total</th>\n<th>Result</th>\n<th>Provision</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>4 or less</td>\n<td><strong>Failure</strong> \u2014 the famine cascade begins (Chapter 6&rsquo;s second worked Ripple, verbatim)</td>\n<td>\u22122 steps</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5\u20138</td>\n<td>Poor</td>\n<td>\u22121 step</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9\u201314</td>\n<td>Adequate</td>\n<td>holds</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>15\u201318</td>\n<td>Good</td>\n<td>+1 step</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>19+</td>\n<td>Bounty (and bounty has politics: prices crash, the Combine buys cheap)</td>\n<td>+1 step, and a Ripple of its own</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>This single roll is the living world&rsquo;s heartbeat: Provision moves \u2192 prices, crime clocks, cult recruitment, migration, and faction leverage all read from it (Chapters 5\u20136).</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 400, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "hjcabRqLkwB42PUB", "name": "5. The Agrarian Engine \u2014 Oxen, Wood, and Salt *(GM only)*", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>5. The Agrarian Engine \u2014 Oxen, Wood, and Salt <em>(GM only)</em></h2>\n<p>Three boring resources run every pre-industrial world, and boring is why they rule: nobody sings about fodder, cordwood, or brine, so whoever quietly controls them controls everyone singing. This section gives the GM the three dials underneath the Harvest Roll \u2014 and the Parasite City rule that makes sieges arithmetic.</p>\n<h3>Draft and the Ox-Paradox</h3>\n<p>Fields don&rsquo;t run on farmers; they run on <strong>plow-teams</strong>. Track each agricultural settlement&rsquo;s <strong>Draft</strong> on the standard dial: <strong>Scarce \u2013 Stable \u2013 Abundant</strong> (checked at Season Turn step 5, beside the ecology dials).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The Harvest reads it</strong> (Section 4): Scarce \u22123, none at all \u22125, Abundant +1.</li>\n<li><strong>The paradox:</strong> the teams that plow the grain <em>eat</em> the grain \u2014 roughly a third of what they pull out of the ground goes back into keeping them alive. Each winter, a settlement at <strong>Lean or worse</strong> must choose: <em>feed the teams</em> (treat Provision as one step worse until spring) or <strong>Eat the Plow</strong> \u2014 slaughter draft animals for an immediate <strong>+1 Provision step</strong>, drop Draft one step, and take <strong>\u22124 on next autumn&rsquo;s Harvest Roll</strong>. It is always the wrong decision, and it is always the decision a starving village makes. The famine cascade (Chapter 6) usually runs through this gate.</li>\n<li><strong>Moving the dial:</strong> war and raiding (armies requisition teams first, always), the <strong>Murrain</strong> (Section 9), a hard winter \u2014 or players physically delivering oxen through hostile country, which is a better adventure than most treasure maps.</li>\n<li><strong>A worldbuilding constant:</strong> fields live within a plow-team&rsquo;s daily walk of the byre. That is why villages sit where they sit, why the map is beaded rather than smeared, and why burning one byre can starve three hamlets.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>The Wood-Take</h3>\n<p>Everything burns wood \u2014 hearths, ovens, brew-coppers, kilns, salt-pans \u2014 and the forge burns it <em>concentrated</em>: Fine-or-better metalwork requires charcoal (Chapter 5). Track each region&rsquo;s <strong>Woodland</strong> on the same three-step dial.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Every settlement draws its hearth-share; <strong>every working industry counts as a settlement again</strong> (a busy smithy, a brewery, a salt-pan, a lime kiln). Tally the take once at region creation; move the dial at Season Turns when the count changes.</li>\n<li><strong>Woodland Scarce:</strong> fuel prices triple, the poor make winter Freezing checks <em>indoors</em>, Fine+ smithing halts without imported charcoal \u2014 and colliers push into whatever forest still stands. The Thornwold&rsquo;s missing charcoal burners (Chapter 6) are this rule wearing a name.</li>\n<li><strong>Woodland Abundant:</strong> cheap charcoal invites industry, and industry restarts the take. The dial is a pendulum, not a ladder.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>The Salt-Take</h3>\n<p>No salt, no larder; no larder, no winter (Chapter 5). Salt is a <strong>point resource</strong> \u2014 coastal pans, brine springs, rock seams \u2014 and every source on the map should be an <strong>Asset on some faction&rsquo;s sheet</strong> (the Saltmark Combine is named for exactly this).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A settlement <strong>without salt access by autumn cannot hold Provision above Fed through winter</strong> \u2014 the stores simply rot down to it by midwinter.</li>\n<li>Salt taxes, gabelles, and route tolls are Political Events with teeth (Section 6&rsquo;s d20 already knows); salt smuggling routes write themselves, and hiring the players to run one is a classic Combine <em>Undermine</em> wearing honest clothes.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>The Parasite City</h3>\n<p>Cities do not feed themselves \u2014 each one drinks the surplus of a <strong>Feeding Basin</strong>: name the two or three rural regions whose grain, herds, and salt keep it alive. When a basin route is cut (a Ripple, a war, a wyrm), <strong>urban Provision falls twice as fast as rural</strong> \u2014 a city hits Lean in weeks, not seasons, and Starving cities do not queue politely. A siege is just this rule with banners on it; so, quietly, is the Combine&rsquo;s whole business model.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 500, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "eVRUxNDZfXQMDrVs", "name": "6. The Generators", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>6. The Generators</h2>\n<p>Quick-roll tables behind Chapters 6\u20137&rsquo;s frameworks. Roll straight, or read them as menus.</p>\n<p><strong>Adventure Seed</strong> <em>(roll one die per column)</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>d10</th>\n<th>Patron</th>\n<th>Problem</th>\n<th>Place</th>\n<th>Twist</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>A village reeve</td>\n<td>Something is killing the herds</td>\n<td>A ruin the maps deny</td>\n<td>The patron caused it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>A Combine factor</td>\n<td>A shipment vanished</td>\n<td>A Thin Place</td>\n<td>Both sides hired help</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>A Pale Flame Reader</td>\n<td>A text must be <em>retrieved</em> (not burned)</td>\n<td>A hospice</td>\n<td>It&rsquo;s a person, not a thing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>A grieving parent</td>\n<td>Someone joined a cult</td>\n<td>A toll-city&rsquo;s underside</td>\n<td>They&rsquo;re right to have joined</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>A clan elder</td>\n<td>A debt needs collecting</td>\n<td>Barrow-grounds</td>\n<td>The debt is generations old</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>A hedge-witch</td>\n<td>A creature must die</td>\n<td>The winter road</td>\n<td>The creature is a keystone (Chapter 7)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>An heir</td>\n<td>Prove the official story false</td>\n<td>A battlefield, years cold</td>\n<td>The story is true; the truth is worse</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>A ship&rsquo;s master</td>\n<td>Escort something that mustn&rsquo;t be opened</td>\n<td>The river at flood</td>\n<td>It&rsquo;s already open</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>A Vashkari scout</td>\n<td>Find why the herds turned</td>\n<td>The open Grass</td>\n<td>The answer is moving toward <em>you</em></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>No patron \u2014 a rumor</td>\n<td>A Harvest about to fail</td>\n<td>Home, wherever that is</td>\n<td>The players&rsquo; past caused it</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><em>Clock (d6):</em> 1\u20132 days \u00b7 3\u20134 one season \u00b7 5 until the snow \u00b7 6 it already happened; this is the aftermath.</p>\n<p><strong>Rumor Table</strong> <em>(d12 \u2014 pair each rumor with what&rsquo;s true; tie half of them to live Ripples)</em>: 1 grain sold before it&rsquo;s sown \u00b7 2 grey threads at the market \u00b7 3 hound-weather coming \u00b7 4 the ford&rsquo;s garrison doubled \u00b7 5 a smith won&rsquo;t take commissions (working star-iron \u2014 Ash trouble) \u00b7 6 pilgrims healed at the crypt (and some not returned) \u00b7 7 moths over the north fields \u00b7 8 a Reader asking after old books, paying too well \u00b7 9 the wyrm surfaced in daylight \u00b7 10 a wedding called off over a Debt (spirit kind) \u00b7 11 charcoal burners missing again \u00b7 12 someone in town wears a face the speaker swears was buried.</p>\n<p><strong>Settlement Quick-Gen</strong> <em>(d8 each)</em>: <strong>Size</strong> 1\u20134 village, 5\u20136 town, 7 hamlet, 8 city \u00b7 <strong>Provision</strong> 1 Starving, 2\u20133 Lean, 4\u20136 Fed, 7 Full, 8 Overflowing \u00b7 <strong>Power</strong> 1 reeve, 2 lord&rsquo;s bailiff, 3 temple, 4 guild bench, 5 the one rich family, 6 a criminal quietly, 7 nobody \u2014 that&rsquo;s the trouble, 8 contested \u00b7 <strong>Trouble</strong> 1 tax arrears, 2 disappearances, 3 bad water, 4 feud, 5 cult growth, 6 a creature, 7 route cut, 8 the harvest \u00b7 <strong>Secret</strong> 1 the granary&rsquo;s already empty, 2 a Branded resident, 3 the power owes the Combine, 4 a Thin Place in the old well, 5 the trouble is a cover, 6 a wanted caster hides here, 7 the founding was a crime, 8 the temple&rsquo;s relic is Blighted.</p>\n<p><strong>Ruin Generator</strong> <em>(d10 each; per Chapter 6&rsquo;s five questions \u2014 &ldquo;why now&rdquo; comes from your current Ripples)</em>: <strong>Was</strong> 1 border fort, 2 monastery, 3 mine, 4 wyrm-cult shrine, 5 manor, 6 bridge-town, 7 pale-flame chapterhouse, 8 barrow field, 9 mill complex, 10 something older than the dying age \u00b7 <strong>Ended by</strong> 1 plague, 2 war, 3 famine, 4 Blight, 5 a rite gone wrong, 6 flood, 7 its own secret, 8 abandonment by decree, 9 the ground itself, 10 no one knows \u2014 which is the problem \u00b7 <strong>Moved in</strong> 1 tallow hounds, 2 bandits, 3 a cult cell, 4 seam-moths, 5 a hermit who knows, 6 the Standing Dead (whose?), 7 wolves, 8 refugees, 9 a Hollowed, 10 nothing \u2014 <em>nothing</em> moved in, and animals won&rsquo;t \u00b7 <strong>Remains</strong> 1 the pay chest, 2 the true dispatches, 3 a grimoire, 4 relics, 5 the ore seam, 6 a still-good bridge, 7 seed grain, 8 a formulary, 9 star-iron, 10 the records that hang a living family.</p>\n<p><strong>Cult Generator</strong> <em>(d10 each)</em>: <strong>Venerates</strong> 1 the merciful end, 2 an ancestor who never died, 3 the river, 4 the fire that cleanses, 5 the buried saint&rsquo;s <em>true</em> teaching, 6 the herds&rsquo; turning, 7 the Seam as wound to heal, 8 the Seam as door, 9 silence, 10 the harvest, hungrily \u00b7 <strong>Demands</strong> 1 grief shared aloud, 2 a tithe of memory (secrets), 3 blood monthly, 4 poverty, 5 a death attended, 6 recruitment quotas, 7 abstinence from iron, 8 the burning of texts, 9 the <em>copying</em> of texts, 10 one act of mercy that is also a crime \u00b7 <strong>Hides</strong> 1 the founder&rsquo;s reversal, 2 a Grade IV text, 3 its patron faction, 4 a Hollowed elder, 5 that the miracles are alchemy, 6 that the miracles are real, 7 the second doctrine for inner ranks, 8 a kill list, 9 the treasury, 10 that it&rsquo;s right \u00b7 <strong>Fracture</strong> 1 open vs. guard, 2 zealots vs. moderates, 3 founder&rsquo;s kin vs. new blood, 4 those who&rsquo;ve read it vs. those who haven&rsquo;t, 5 urban vs. rural cells, 6 the money.</p>\n<p><strong>Faction Conflict</strong> <em>(d8 \u00d7 d8 \u00d7 d6)</em>: <strong>Flashpoint</strong> 1 a grimoire surfaces, 2 a debt called, 3 a route cut, 4 a defection, 5 a Harvest failure, 6 a keystone kill, 7 a succession, 8 a miracle \u00b7 <strong>Arena</strong> 1 the courts, 2 the market, 3 the road, 4 the pulpit, 5 the docks, 6 the countryside, 7 a wedding, 8 open ground \u00b7 <strong>Escalation</strong> 1 lawsuits, 2 embargo, 3 hired knives, 4 mob, 5 private war, 6 someone opens a book they shouldn&rsquo;t.</p>\n<p><strong>Political Event</strong> <em>(d20)</em>: 1 succession dispute \u00b7 2 tax revolt \u00b7 3 marriage alliance \u00b7 4 border incident at a ford \u00b7 5 debt called on a crown \u00b7 6 charter challenged \u00b7 7 a lord takes the grey thread \u00b7 8 witch-trial of someone innocent \u00b7 9 witch-trial of someone guilty \u00b7 10 grain tariff war \u00b7 11 a mercenary company unpaid and idle \u00b7 12 pilgrimage boom \u00b7 13 a road re-opened (why now?) \u00b7 14 census \u2014 and what it finds \u00b7 15 an heir returns from the March changed \u00b7 16 guild strike \u00b7 17 a treaty&rsquo;s secret clause leaks \u00b7 18 amnesty declared, cynically \u00b7 19 a coronation needing a wyrm-blessing nobody can get \u00b7 20 two of the above, colliding.</p>\n<p><strong>Monster Ecosystem Generator</strong> <em>(d8 each \u2014 then write If Removed first, per Chapter 7)</em>: <strong>Role</strong> 1\u20132 grazer, 3\u20134 mesopredator, 5 apex, 6 scavenger, 7 parasite, 8 keystone (pick any above and raise the stakes) \u00b7 <strong>Damage class</strong> 1\u20133 Piercing, 4\u20135 Blade, 6\u20138 Crushing \u00b7 <strong>Dread</strong> 1 too quiet, 2 wrong proportions, 3 mimics voices, 4 tool use, 5 tracks that stop, 6 it buries what it doesn&rsquo;t eat, 7 Ash-touched (Fear \u221210 and 1 Ash somewhere in its harvest), 8 beautiful \u00b7 <strong>Prize</strong> 1 pelt (Insulation), 2 organ (Vitality 2+), 3 plate (Superior leather), 4 gland (alchemy), 5 bone (Fine+ carving), 6 delicacy (a named dish waiting to happen), 7 Seam-touched part (mark it), 8 trophy with politics attached \u00b7 <strong>Weakness a local knows</strong> 1 fire, 2 salt, 3 noise, 4 its young, 5 the belly, 6 running water, 7 winter, 8 its own reflection.</p>\n<p><strong>NPC Quick-Gen</strong> <em>(d10 / d10 / d8)</em>: <strong>Want</strong> 1 out of debt, 2 the farm back, 3 a child found, 4 rank, 5 revenge misfiled as justice, 6 to be believed, 7 quiet, 8 the recipe/formula, 9 someone else&rsquo;s spouse, 10 to undo one hour of their life \u00b7 <strong>Fear</strong> 1 winter, 2 the ledger, 3 being known, 4 the Office, 5 their own temper, 6 the water, 7 dying unshriven, 8 the grey thread&rsquo;s appeal, 9 dogs, since the war, 10 that they were right \u00b7 <strong>Mask</strong> <em>(d8 \u2014 or assign a Chapter 2 archetype)</em> 1\u20132 Velvet Mask, 3\u20134 Long Game, 5\u20136 Beautiful Ruin, 7\u20138 Last Vow.</p>\n<p><strong>Grimoire / Weighted Text</strong> <em>(d8 each)</em>: <strong>Form</strong> 1 bound book, 2 scroll case, 3 tally-sticks, 4 tattooed hide, 5 song cycle, 6 ledger with a second cipher, 7 stone rubbings, 8 a child&rsquo;s primer, annotated \u00b7 <strong>Tradition</strong> 1 Red Tithe, 2 Old Tongue, 3 Grey Craft, 4 Pact-Way, 5\u20136 folk mixture (Weight 0\u20131), 7 Patient Art formulary, 8 <strong>Hollow Song</strong> (Weight 2; the Office&rsquo;s clocks start turning) \u00b7 <strong>The catch</strong> 1 incomplete, 2 trapped (a rite triggers on reading), 3 famous \u2014 recognizably stolen, 4 annotated by someone Branded, 5 wrong on purpose, 6 in a dead dialect (Lore: History too), 7 the Choir wants it, 8 it wants something.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 600, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "a2BETvl1lr1Rcfyo", "name": "7. The Congregation Engine \u2014 Cults and Factions, Step by Step *(GM only)*", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>7. The Congregation Engine \u2014 Cults and Factions, Step by Step <em>(GM only)</em></h2>\n<p>Chapter 6 gave the anatomy; this is the assembly line. Ten steps, in order, each one sentence to one paragraph of work \u2014 follow them and a playable cult (or guild, order, or house; the engine doesn&rsquo;t care what it&rsquo;s wearing) falls out the other end. The governing principle throughout: <strong>every rank of a congregation is a trade \u2014 dues paid against boons received \u2014 and the receipt always arrives slightly after the payment.</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>The Claim.</strong> One sentence that explains the whole world to a hurting person. <em>(&ldquo;The world&rsquo;s death is a mercy interrupted.&rdquo;)</em> If the Claim wouldn&rsquo;t tempt <em>you</em> on your worst day, sharpen it.</li>\n<li><strong>The Service.</strong> What it genuinely provides \u2014 hospices, arbitration, grain at interest, protection, burial done right. <strong>A congregation that offers nothing real recruits no one.</strong> The Service is its Public Face, and the horror of a good cult is that the Service is not a lie.</li>\n<li><strong>The Price of the Door.</strong> How joining works, graded like Rites (Chapter 4) for casting cults, or in oaths, debts, and proofs otherwise \u2014 <em>and the price of leaving</em>, which every congregation has and no recruiter mentions. Write both now; the leaving-price is a hook aimed at every member the players will ever like.</li>\n<li><strong>The Ladder.</strong> Three to five ranks. For each, fill two columns: <strong>Dues</strong> (time, tithe, blood, secrets, recruitment quotas, one act that can&rsquo;t be taken back) and <strong>Boons</strong> (make them mechanical: +10 with a named skill inside the fold; access to a Formula, poultice, or safehouse; a Weighted text by rank; seats at group ritual; the Rig&rsquo;s +20 borrowed once per season). Each step up pays more and receives more, in that order.</li>\n<li><strong>The Face and the Followers.</strong> One leader on the full NPC card \u2014 Want, Fear, Bond, Secret, Philosophy, a Mask and Truth, and all four political layers (Chapter 6 \u00a710). Then two or three follower archetypes statted with Section 8&rsquo;s one-minute method. The Face is the congregation&rsquo;s interface; the followers are its texture.</li>\n<li><strong>The House.</strong> A headquarters, built with Chapter 6&rsquo;s ruin-and-settlement questions: what it was before, what it hides now, why <em>here</em>. Give it one architectural fact members swear by and one the leadership doesn&rsquo;t advertise.</li>\n<li><strong>Favored and Disfavored.</strong> Allies, enemies, who it courts \u2014 and who it will never take, which says more than the recruitment list. Vectors as ever: grief, debt, exile, and famine (a Provision drop is recruiting season; the cascade is load-bearing).</li>\n<li><strong>The Relics.</strong> One or two unique holdings: a Weighted text, an enchanted item with a Hunger, a Formula, a ledger that hangs somebody. These go on the faction sheet as Assets \u2014 which means they are stealable, and the engine assumes someone eventually will.</li>\n<li><strong>The Fracture and the Secret.</strong> The built-in internal argument (Chapter 6 \u00a78) \u2014 and what is <em>actually</em> true underneath the Claim, which the founder knew and the current ladder may not.</li>\n<li><strong>The Sheet and the Clocks.</strong> Transcribe to the faction sheet: Reach, Wealth, Force, Guile, Zeal; two Goal Clocks; the Fault Line from step 9. Then write <strong>three hooks</strong> \u2014 one for outsiders hired against it, one aimed at a member&rsquo;s family, and one that fires automatically when a Clock fills. Put it in the Faction Turn from next season. The world will do the rest.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><em>(Dice-first start: roll the cult generator in Section 6, then run the results through these steps in twenty minutes.)</em></p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 700, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "YkqXRFS2G2ZdVEEH", "name": "8. NPCs in One Minute *(GM only)*", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>8. NPCs in One Minute <em>(GM only)</em></h2>\n<p>Most of the world does not need a character sheet. It needs a <strong>Caliber</strong>, a line of teeth, and a reason.</p>\n<p><strong>Caliber sets everything:</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Caliber</th>\n<th>Competence</th>\n<th>Health</th>\n<th>Threshold</th>\n<th>Defense</th>\n<th>Damage bonus</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Green</td>\n<td>35</td>\n<td>15</td>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>+1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Seasoned</td>\n<td>50</td>\n<td>20</td>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>11</td>\n<td>+2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Expert</td>\n<td>65</td>\n<td>24</td>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>12</td>\n<td>+3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Master</td>\n<td>80</td>\n<td>28</td>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>12</td>\n<td>+4</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Competence</strong> is one Rating for everything the NPC would plausibly be good at, given their life; anything outside it, halve. Weapons deal their listed damage plus the Caliber bonus. <strong>Morale</strong> from Chapter 3&rsquo;s bands: Wavering 30 \u00b7 Steady 50 \u00b7 Hard 70 \u00b7 Fanatic 90. That&rsquo;s a finished stat block: <em>&ldquo;Toll-sergeant \u2014 Seasoned 50; spear 1d8+2; Health 20, Threshold 7; Steady 50.&rdquo;</em></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>How deadly is a fight? \u2014 the encounter budget.</strong> Blightmarch combat is lethal, and the numbers are steep. As a rule of thumb for a four-person party against Seasoned foes: <strong>half your number is a warm-up; equal numbers is a real, bloody fight the party usually wins; numbers plus one is genuinely dangerous, with a fair chance someone falls; numbers plus two is a likely defeat.</strong> Difficulty climbs faster than headcount \u2014 every enemy past parity is roughly an extra unparried blow landing each round. To make a fight <em>harder</em> without simply adding bodies, raise Caliber, give the enemy terrain or a Move, or cut off the party&rsquo;s escape; to make one <em>easier</em>, let the party ambush, split the enemy, or fight where their armor and reach tell. And remember the world&rsquo;s own economy (Chapter 5): a pitched fight against bandits often yields less coin than a week&rsquo;s lodging costs \u2014 the Dying Age rewards the party that finds a way <em>around</em> the sword far more than the one that reaches for it.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>The NPC Quick Sheet</strong> \u2014 for anyone who earns a name, ten lines and done:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Name \u00b7 Role</strong> \u2014 Caliber (Competence) \u00b7 weapon &amp; damage \u00b7 Health/Threshold \u00b7 Morale\n<strong>Want:</strong> \u00b7 <strong>Fear:</strong> \u00b7 <strong>Bond:</strong> \u00b7 <strong>Secret:</strong> \u00b7 <strong>Philosophy:</strong> <em>(one line each)</em>\n<strong>Regard:</strong> Hostile \u2013 Cold \u2013 Neutral \u2013 Warm \u2013 Devoted \u00b7 <strong>Agenda:</strong> \u25ef\u25ef\u25ef\u25ef <em>(ticks each untouched Season Turn)</em>\n<em>Political? Add the Four Layers (Chapter 6 \u00a710):</em> <strong>Office / Obligation / Want / Patron</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Full random tables for wants, fears, and masks are in Section 6; the full card and Regard rules are Chapter 6 \u00a710. The rule of thumb: <strong>one line for the crowd, ten lines for a name, the full card only for people who get Agendas.</strong></p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 800, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "e93BjQ7pyZ9b5U6p", "name": "8b. Enemy Groups \u2014 Ready to Run *(GM only)*", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>8b. Enemy Groups \u2014 Ready to Run <em>(GM only)</em></h2>\n<p><em>Pre-built threats using the Caliber system (Section 8). Each is a whole encounter in a line or two: who, how many, how they fight, and when they break. Numbers assume a four-person party \u2014 scale by the encounter budget above. Loot is typical; the Dying Age pays poorly (Ch. 5).</em></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Group</th>\n<th>Make-up</th>\n<th>Tactics &amp; Morale</th>\n<th>Loot</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Lone cutpurse</strong></td>\n<td>1 \u00d7 Green 35, dagger</td>\n<td>Flees on any wound; Wavering</td>\n<td>a few coppers, a stolen trinket</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Footpad pair</strong></td>\n<td>2 \u00d7 Green 35, clubs &amp; knife</td>\n<td>Ambush at Near, demand coin; break if one falls</td>\n<td>20\u201340c, maybe a blade</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Band of thieves</strong></td>\n<td>1 \u00d7 Seasoned 50 (leader) + 4 \u00d7 Green 35</td>\n<td>Surround, threaten; flee if the leader falls; Wavering</td>\n<td>1\u20132s pooled, fenced goods</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Road brigands</strong></td>\n<td>5\u20136 \u00d7 Seasoned 50, spears &amp; bows, 1 bowman on a rise</td>\n<td>Set a toll-ambush; Steady while winning, break at half losses</td>\n<td>1s each, a decent weapon or two</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Deserter company</strong></td>\n<td>6 \u00d7 Seasoned 50 + 1 \u00d7 Expert 65 (sergeant), mail &amp; polearms</td>\n<td>Fight in a line, use Set; Hard while the sergeant stands</td>\n<td>mail, coin, a war-story</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Mercenary squad</strong></td>\n<td>4 \u00d7 Expert 65, mixed arms, disciplined</td>\n<td>Focus-fire, cover each other&rsquo;s Reactions; Steady, and professional about it</td>\n<td>wages owed, good gear</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ship-load of pirates</strong></td>\n<td>10\u201314 \u00d7 Seasoned 50 + 1 \u00d7 Master 80 (captain) + 2 \u00d7 Expert 65 (mates)</td>\n<td>Board in a wave, cut ropes; Hard near the captain, Wavering once he&rsquo;s down</td>\n<td>the hold, the ship, ransoms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Cultist congregation</strong></td>\n<td>6 \u00d7 Seasoned 50 zealots + 1 caster (Ch. 4) + a Face (Expert 65)</td>\n<td>Ritual in progress; zealots buy the caster time; Fanatic 90 \u2014 they do not break</td>\n<td>relics, a Weighted text, the truth</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Town watch</strong></td>\n<td>4\u20138 \u00d7 Seasoned 50, spears &amp; a horn</td>\n<td>Call reinforcements (the horn!), fight to subdue; Steady, and they know the ground</td>\n<td>nothing worth the trouble; now you&rsquo;re wanted</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Noble&rsquo;s retinue</strong></td>\n<td>2 \u00d7 Expert 65 guards + 1 \u00d7 Master 80 (knight) + servants</td>\n<td>The knight duels; guards protect the noble; Hard, honor-bound</td>\n<td>fine arms, ransom, a grudge</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Witch-hunters</strong></td>\n<td>3 \u00d7 Expert 65 + 1 zealot-Master 80, anti-caster gear</td>\n<td>Target the party&rsquo;s caster first, bind and burn; Hard, righteous</td>\n<td>silver, warrants, chains</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Raider warband</strong></td>\n<td>8 \u00d7 Seasoned 50 + 1 \u00d7 Expert 65 + a Dread beast (optional)</td>\n<td>Burn and grab; Steady, here for plunder not glory; break if outmatched</td>\n<td>everything not nailed down</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Starving mob</strong></td>\n<td>12+ \u00d7 Green 35, tools &amp; desperation</td>\n<td>No tactics, just numbers and hunger; break if fed or frightened, deadly if cornered</td>\n<td>nothing \u2014 and everything, if this is a Provision crisis (Ch. 8)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Barrow-guardians</strong></td>\n<td>3 \u00d7 the Standing Dead (Ch. 7) + a wight</td>\n<td>Rise when the tomb is breached; Fanatic; do not pursue past the mound</td>\n<td>grave-gold, grave-iron, a curse</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Scaling a group:</strong> to harden any entry, raise one tier of Caliber, add terrain (a wall, a river, the dark), or cut the party&rsquo;s escape. To soften it, let the party ambush, split the enemy, or catch them unready. Remember morale \u2014 most fights end when one side <em>breaks</em>, not when it dies (Ch. 3). A GM who rolls Morale honestly will kill far fewer PCs than the dice alone suggest, and tell better stories doing it.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 900, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "BqTv0udZre32qdip", "name": "9. The Plague List", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>9. The Plague List</h2>\n<p>The diseases promised since Chapter 3, on its framework (Virulence penalty to the victim&rsquo;s Endurance Reckonings; stages: onset \u2192 grip \u2192 crisis):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Weeping Pox</strong> <em>(Virulence \u221210; contact)</em>: sores that seep \u2192 fever, \u221210 all Reckonings \u2192 crisis: scarring (Presence contexts) or, on a Catastrophe, blindness. Survivors are immune; villages know it, and quarantine politics write themselves.</li>\n<li><strong>Bonefire Fever</strong> <em>(Virulence \u221220; bad water, flood season)</em>: aching joints \u2192 the burn: Stamina maximum halved \u2192 crisis: 2d4 Health, and a Failure leaves one joint permanently stiff (\u22125 to a chosen physical skill).</li>\n<li><strong>Millsick</strong> <em>(Virulence \u221210; blighted grain \u2014 a Poor Harvest&rsquo;s shadow)</em>: tingling and vivid dreams \u2192 tremors, \u221210 fine work; visions (Composure checks in crowds) \u2192 crisis: gangrene&rsquo;s threat to fingers and toes (the Blades critical table, grimly, without a blade). Whole villages at once, when the granary&rsquo;s bad \u2014 a famine cascade accelerant.</li>\n<li><strong>Road Ague</strong> <em>(Virulence \u22125; exhaustion and wet camps)</em>: chills \u2192 the shakes: all travel Reckonings \u221210 \u2192 crisis: mild, mostly \u2014 but it never quite leaves: each winter thereafter, one Freezing check at \u221210. The veteran&rsquo;s souvenir.</li>\n<li><strong>The Murrain</strong> <em>(beasts only \u2014 cattle, oxen, horses; no Virulence, no cure but fire)</em>: it takes herds, not people, and then it takes people the slow way. An afflicted region&rsquo;s <strong>Draft</strong> and herd Dials drop one step per season until it burns out or the byres do (Section 5); next autumn&rsquo;s Harvest feels it in full. Nothing empties a temple faster than healthy parishioners and dying oxen.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 1000, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "gwrFMKAv6Vk1F2sm", "name": "10. A Cascade, Run Live", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>10. A Cascade, Run Live</h2>\n<p><em>Autumn Season Turn, middle Cindral, using every subsystem in order:</em></p>\n<p><strong>(1) Weather:</strong> the year logged two drought weeks. <strong>(2) Harvest Roll:</strong> 2d10 = 8, \u22124 for drought \u2192 <strong>4: Failure.</strong> Riverside Provision drops two steps to Lean-going-Starving. The famine Ripple opens (Chapter 6, verbatim): <em>crime clock \u2192 Choir surge \u2192 Combine calls Vael&rsquo;s debt \u2192 migration east \u2192 Vashkari escort prices \u2192 border spark.</em> <strong>(3) Faction Turns:</strong> the Combine <strong>Courts</strong> Vael (Wealth 9, rolls 22 \u2014 Success: the debt term now names <em>land</em>, not grain); the Choir <strong>Recruits</strong> the hungry bank (Zeal 10, rolls 04 \u2014 <strong>Masterstroke</strong>: two segments and a new hospice); the Pale Flame <strong>Researches</strong> the hospice network (rolls 88 \u2014 Failure, noticed). <strong>(4) Agendas:</strong> Yorna&rsquo;s blood-debt family clock ticks to full \u2014 <em>they&rsquo;ve found her.</em> <strong>(5) Dials:</strong> hunting pressure on aurochs spiked with the hunger; the Dial slips to Scarce \u2014 a second Ripple queues behind the first. <strong>(6) Ripples advance</strong> one link each. <strong>(7) Rumors:</strong> <em>grain sold before it&rsquo;s sown; grey threads by the granary; aurochs gone from the west marsh; strangers asking for a soldier by name.</em></p>\n<p>Twenty minutes of dice. The players wake into a world that visibly moved \u2014 with four different places to stand in front of it.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 1100, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "nqBayc2KzWGSRAxZ", "name": "11. Three Campaign Frames", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>11. Three Campaign Frames</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The Ford Reopened.</strong> Vael rebuilds the garrison at Aldwatch Ford; the crown wants the &ldquo;victory&rdquo; commemorated, the ground disagrees, and one veteran on the survivors&rsquo; list (any PC \u2014 or Yorna, as written) knows the official story is a lie with her name in it. Starts with: a construction contract, a Standing Dead in the shallows nobody raised recently, and a historian asking honest questions.</li>\n<li><strong>The Saint&rsquo;s Vigil.</strong> The pilgrimage villages hire guards for the healing season; the Church hires the same guards to keep certain pilgrims <em>out</em>; a Reader and a Censer arrive in the same week with opposite warrants. Starts with: a healed child, an unreturned mother, and the litany half-remembered by a drunk deacon.</li>\n<li><strong>Wyrmfall.</strong> Someone else already killed the Vael Wyrm \u2014 the players arrive in week two of the largest Ripple on the map: moth-clouds northbound, barge crews unpaid, the League coin suddenly ironic, and every faction hiring. Starts mid-cascade, which is to say: starts honest.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 1200, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "0IT10IJNsEZyhsbn", "name": "12. The Table Itself", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>12. The Table Itself</h2>\n<p>A last word, publisher to purchaser: this is a grim game about costs, and the table&rsquo;s trust is the one resource with no recovery roll. Calibrate the darkness together before play \u2014 traumas, plagues, the Hollowed wearing familiar faces \u2014 and treat any player&rsquo;s line the way this system treats a Catastrophe range: absolute, no negotiation past it. The Dying Age is unforgiving. The table never has to be.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 1300, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "9tiOMuoUAQDRWeyB", "name": "Appendix \u2014 The Reckoner's Card *(the one-page GM reference)*", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>Appendix \u2014 The Reckoner&rsquo;s Card <em>(the one-page GM reference)</em></h2>\n<p><strong>The Reckoning</strong> <em>(roll d100 at or under effective Rating; low is good)</em>: Masterstroke 1 to Rating\u00f75 (round up) \u00b7 Success to Rating \u00b7 Costly Success to Rating+20 (max 95) \u00b7 Failure above \u00b7 <strong>96\u201399 always Failure (the World&rsquo;s Share)</strong> \u00b7 <strong>100 always Catastrophe</strong>. Difficulty: \u00b120 / 40 / 60; <strong>clamp effective Rating to 5\u201395</strong>. Opposed: compare degrees; on a tie, higher successful roll wins; everyone&rsquo;s own costs still land. Second Parry/Dodge in a round costs 1 Stamina. Experimentation: marks twice, 99\u2013100 both Catastrophes.</p>\n<p><strong>Conditions</strong> <em>(Hungry / Parched / Freezing / Sweltering / Sleepless)</em>: each \u22125 all Reckonings, \u22122 max Stamina, stacking; daily escalation End\u00d75 or 1d4 Health. Insulation prevents ordinary Freezing.</p>\n<p><strong>Wounds:</strong> net damage \u2265 Wound Threshold (End\u00f72, up) = Wound (stacking \u22125, <strong>capped at \u221210</strong>); \u2265 2\u00d7 = Grievous \u2192 roll the weapon-class critical table. Masterstroke or unaware target = automatic Grievous. Unarmored still soak 1. <strong>No single blow drops a healthy PC (&gt;\u00bd HP) below Downed.</strong> <strong>Bleeding(X)</strong> at end of victim&rsquo;s turn. <strong>Downed (0 Health):</strong> Death Reckoning End\u00d75 each turn; 3 Marks = dead; 100 = dead; Medicine stabilizes.</p>\n<p><strong>Composure</strong> = WP\u00d75 vs Fear rating; Failure = Shaken (\u221210, can&rsquo;t close); Catastrophe = Panicked. Morale bands: 30 / 50 / 70 / 90.</p>\n<p><strong>Casting:</strong> pay full Resolve (floor 0 \u2014 no credit) \u2192 Ritual Casting Reckoning. Ash by Grade 0/1/2/3+ (Masterstroke waives; Catastrophe doubles). <strong>Ash Threshold = WP; at it: Blighted \u2014 Brand, clear, no save.</strong> Rushing I\u2013II only: \u221220, +1 Ash. Assistants +5 each (max +15), share cost.</p>\n<p><strong>The Season Turn:</strong> 1 Weather \u00b7 2 Harvest (autumn; Draft mods) \u00b7 3 Faction Turns \u00b7 4 NPC Agendas \u00b7 5 Ecology + Agrarian Dials \u00b7 6 Advance Ripples \u00b7 7 Write 3\u20135 Rumors.</p>\n<p><strong>Harvest 2d10:</strong> \u22644 Failure (Provision \u22122, famine Ripple) \u00b7 5\u20138 Poor (\u22121) \u00b7 9\u201314 holds \u00b7 15\u201318 Good (+1) \u00b7 19+ Bounty (+1, its own Ripple). Mods: drought \u22122 each \u00b7 flood/hail \u22122 \u00b7 frost \u22121 \u00b7 works +2 \u00b7 war \u22123 \u00b7 Draft \u22123/\u22125/+1.</p>\n<p><strong>The Rig:</strong> formal contests \u2014 institutional standing +20; leverage cancels.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 1400, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "xNQpHRuGgvzsvitP", "name": "Appendix \u2014 The Region Sheet *(one per active region)*", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>Appendix \u2014 The Region Sheet <em>(one per active region)</em></h2>\n<p>A fully-run Season Turn juggles six-plus trackers per region; copy this block once per region you&rsquo;re actively running and the load stops living in your head. <em>(Blanks fill from Chapters 6\u20138.)</em></p>\n<p>```\nREGION: <strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em>_   Season/Year: </strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><em>\nCLIMATE PROFILE: latitude <strong><em>_  altitude </em></strong></em>  humidity <strong><em>_  wind </em></strong>_</p>\n<p>SETTLEMENTS\n  name <strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em>  Provision [Starv|Lean|Fed|Full|Over]  Draft [Scarce|Stable|Abund]\n  name </strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>  Provision [Starv|Lean|Fed|Full|Over]  Draft [Scarce|Stable|Abund]\nREGION DIALS   Woodland [Scarce|Stable|Abund]   Salt access [Y|N]\nECOLOGY DIALS  </em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong> [S|St|Ab]  <strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em> [S|St|Ab]  </em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong> [S|St|Ab]</p>\n<p>FACTIONS (Goal Clocks)\n  <strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em>  \u25a2\u25a2\u25a2\u25a2\u25a2\u25a2  action last turn: </strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><em>\n  <strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em>  \u25a2\u25a2\u25a2\u25a2\u25a2\u25a2  action last turn: </strong><strong><em>_</em></strong></em>\nNPC AGENDAS\n  <strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em>  \u25a2\u25a2\u25a2\u25a2    </strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>  \u25a2\u25a2\u25a2\u25a2\nACTIVE RIPPLES (advance one link each)\n  </em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_  link </em>_/</strong>\n  <strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong><strong><em>_</em></strong>  link <strong>/</strong></p>\n<p>THIS SEASON: weather <strong><em>_  Harvest(aut) </em></strong>_  \u2192 Provision moves <strong><em>_\nRUMORS TO SEED: 1)</em></strong><strong><em>_</em> 2)</strong><strong><em>_</em>_ 3)</strong>______\n```</p>\n<p>Run the seven Season-Turn steps (Section 2) top to bottom against this sheet; when you finish, everything that moved is already written down, and the rumor line is your next session&rsquo;s hooks.</p>\n<hr />"}, "sort": 1500, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}, {"_id": "kLXx8s7ghrdMYlwJ", "name": "Appendix \u2014 The Exchange *(combat, one attack at a glance)*", "type": "text", "title": {"show": true, "level": 1}, "text": {"format": 1, "content": "<h2>Appendix \u2014 The Exchange <em>(combat, one attack at a glance)</em></h2>\n<p>One trip through the loop per attack; memorize it once and the table lookups fall away.</p>\n<p><code>1. DECLARE   attacker's skill + weapon; defender spends Reaction? (Parry/Dodge)\n2. RECKON    attacker rolls d100 vs Skill Rating (\u22125 \u00d7 [Defense\u221210]; clamp 5\u201395)\n             opposed? defender rolls too \u2192 compare DEGREES, better wins\n3. DEGREE    Masterstroke \u2192 damage twice, take higher; ALWAYS Grievous (roll critical table)\n             Success/Costly \u2192 damage; win-by-2+ adds +2; Costly also pays its cost\n             Failure \u2192 miss;  96\u201399 \u2192 miss;  100 \u2192 Catastrophe (fumble table)\n4. DAMAGE    roll weapon dice + Melee Damage Bonus\n5. ARMOR     subtract the target's single AV  \u2192 NET damage\n6. WOUND     net \u2265 Wound Threshold \u2192 Wound (stacking \u22125)\n             net \u2265 2\u00d7 Threshold  \u2192 Grievous \u2192 roll the class critical table\n             + apply any Bleeding\n7. AFTER     0 Health \u2192 Downed (Death Reckonings). Fear/Aftermath \u2192 Composure.</code></p>\n<p>The only per-exchange lookups are the degree comparison (step 3) and, on a Grievous blow, the one critical table matching the weapon&rsquo;s class (step 6). Everything else is arithmetic the players run themselves.</p>\n<hr />\n<p><em>The world does not exist for the players. The players exist within the world \u2014 and now the world is yours to keep turning.</em></p>"}, "sort": 1600, "ownership": {"default": -1}, "flags": {}}], "folder": null, "sort": 1000, "ownership": {"default": 0}, "flags": {}}
